        "title": "The JBL Live Beam 4 are my favorite wireless earbuds of 2026 after weeks of testing — and I swear it’s not just because of their awesome touchscreen charging case",
        "link": "https://www.techradar.com/audio/earbuds-airpods/jbl-live-beam-4-review",
        "source": "Latest from TechRadar",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.475838+00:00",
        "image": "https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/EphrZLo3AVitvhGXaGXtFc-1920-80.jpg",
        "full_content": "TechRadar Verdict\nWith stellar sound quality, a quirky charging case, and an ultra-comfortable fit, the JBL Live Beam 4 might just be my favorite wireless earbuds of 2026 so far. Yes, the ANC could be a little better — as could call quality — but their expansive set of features, impressive battery life, and fair price still help them to challenge some of the best wireless earbuds around.\nPros\n- +Excellent audio quality with impactful bass\n- +Comfortable, secure fit\n- +Smart case is well-built and fun, even if it’s a bit gimmicky\nCons\n- -Disappointing call quality\n- -ANC isn’t quite top-tier\n- -Poor spatial audio setting\nWhy you can trust TechRadar\nJBL Live Beam 4 review\nJBL is back with its new generation of touchscreen-equipped earbuds, and the JBL Live Beam 4 may just be my favorite of the bunch.\nThese in-ear wireless earbuds come with a number of improvements over their predecessors, including enhanced active noise cancelling capabilities, new “fidelity drivers” for more advanced audio, and better call quality to boot. That’s not to mention an updated UI for the smart charging case, which is packed with more features than ever before.\nBut are these changes good enough to cement the JBL Live Beam 4 as some of the best wireless earbuds that money can buy? Here’s what I think after weeks of testing.\nLet’s start by talking about the smart charging case, which is the Live series’ unique selling point. Although it certainly borders on gimmicky, the revamped UI is actually pretty responsive, and is packed with plenty of functionality. You can adjust the ANC mode, equalizer, spatial sound settings, and playback settings, for instance, and 'favorite' certain settings for speedier access.\nBut that’s not all. You can also access tools such as a timer, flashlight, and a Find My Buds application, and also set a background — including a custom photo, which I loved doing. Gaining access to these instruments without needing to remove your phone (or other source device) may be handy in certain situations — like when your device is in a bag or on charge.\nAs chic as the touchscreen case is, though, I still found myself gravitating towards the JBL Headphones app on my phone — the ease of a larger screen, and the full control available in the companion software made it a more natural choice for me.\nThe feature-set available is pretty comprehensive just on the case though, and marks a vast improvement on the suite of features to be found in JBL's past screen-toting buds. On top of what I’ve already mentioned, you can activate a smart talk option — similar to Sony’s speak-to-chat feature. You can also customize touch controls, and alter how your voice — and the person on the other side — sounds on phone calls (there’s a natural, powerful, or bright version).\nSign up for breaking news, reviews, opinion, top tech deals, and more.\nGiven that JBL has also included ‘Perfect Calls 2.0’ tech, which harnesses an AI-trained algorithm, you may expect primo call quality here, but unfortunately, I found it to be a touch underwhelming. It was by no means bad, but during a call, my colleague noted how my voice lacked the clarity and definition they’d expected. When making a voice recording I found this to be accurate — my speech was clear enough, but there was a fair bit of sibilance, and background noises were pretty prominent too.\nBut let’s talk about arguably the most important feature of all — active noise cancellation. You can choose to have this on, off, or use a transparency mode, all of which are easy to cycle through in-app or on the smart charging case.\nBut just how good is the ANC on these earbuds? Well… it’s decent. We weren’t blown away by noise the cancelling on the JBL Live Beam 3, and despite some improvement, I still found them a little prone to letting external noise creep in. Whether it was passing by traffic, typing in my office, or chatter on buses, I could still hear a fair bit, even when listening at around 70% volume. The Beam 4 still dulled sounds effectively, and kept me focused on my tunes, but if you’re expecting the silence-inducing ANC of the Bose QuietComfort Ultra Earbuds Gen 2, for instance, then you may be disappointed.\nAnyway, I think it’s about time I started giving the JBL Beam 4 their flowers, because they impressed me in almost every other area — including what is arguably the most crucial of all: sound quality. These are a fantastic-sounding pair of wireless buds, especially with LDAC activated for higher-resolution Bluetooth streaming.\nRight out of the box, the Live Beam 4 offer exciting, impactful, yet detailed audio. As is usually the case with JBL audio gear, the bass is fairly forward, but not to the point that it’s distasteful or ‘boomy’.\nFor instance, tuning into Soul Mass Transit System’s Feel Da Vibe, I was struck by the deep, hypnotic bassline, which rippled throughout the track effortlessly, never sounding lethargic or unrefined. In addition, synths in the mid-range and drums up high never sounded drowned out or obscured by the hard-hitting bass.\nMore stripped-back, acoustic tracks sound great on the Live Beam 4 too. For example, in 78 Rotações by Jards Macalé, fingerpicked guitars sounded expressive and vibrant, and the natural tonality of the raw playing was replicated with finesse. Sure, vocals could have been separated in the mix a bit more effectively, but I still really enjoyed the listening experience overall — subtle percussive elements and guitar licks sounded were positioned brilliantly, and the buds’ broad soundstage made for a surprisingly immersive presentation.\nAll in all, I’d argue that these are some of the best-sounding earbuds in their price range. Treble is controlled yet vivid enough to capture the excitement of more energetic tracks, mids are detailed and clean-sounding, and bass is impactful yet disciplined. One thing, though: keep Spatial Sound off. This setting only made my music sound compressed, and vocals sound artificial and shrill. You also have to turn LDAC off to use it, so I’d steer clear, personally.\nSomething else I really liked about the Live Beam 4 is their design. Their stems make them easy to remove from your ears, and the glossy finish on the exterior is pretty enticing. The case itself is pretty neat-looking too and is built to a very high standard. The display itself is fairly bright, and although the case is on the bulky side overall, it’s still small enough to slot into just about any pocket.\nIn the ear, the buds also feel phenomenally comfortable and secure. I never felt the need to take them out, even when listening to music for hours on end — something I honestly can’t say about a lot of competitors. They’re also IP55 dust and waterproof-rated, meaning I was able to use the Beam 4 even if it was pouring with rain outside.\nSo are the JBL Live Beam 4 worth their £179.99 / AU$269.95 (about $240) asking price? For me, it’s a resounding yes. They offer genuinely sophisticated sound, a really comfortable in-ear fit, and plenty of features to play around with. The smart case — though admittedly a bit of a gimmick — is responsive and a fun inclusion, and I enjoyed setting a custom background, using the built-in applications, and having swift access to EQ settings, for example.\nThe battery life — although unchanged from the previous gen — is still plentiful too, 12 hours for the buds, and up to 48 hours with the charging case is better than you’re getting from Apple and Bose’s flagship earbuds.\nSo, even if the ANC and call quality could be better on the JBL Live Beam 4, they still earn my hearty recommendation, and if you want wireless earbuds that come with a unique twist, they’re well worth checking out.\nJBL Live Beam 4 review: price & release date\n- List price of £179.99 / AU$269.95 (about $240)\n- Revealed in May 2026\nThe JBL Live Beam 4 were revealed in May 2026, about two years after their predecessor, the JBL Live Beam 3. They come in at £179.99 / AU$269.95 (about $240), meaning they cost the same as the previous gen model did at launch (in the UK). We’re yet to receive any information about pricing or a launch in the US, but we’ll update this review if and when we hear more.\nThey’re priced competitively, coming in at a lower cost than top-tier rivals like the AirPods Pro 3 and the Bose QuietComfort Ultra Earbuds Gen 2.\nJBL Live Beam 4 review: specs\n| Drivers | 10mm dynamic | \n| Active noise cancellation | Yes | \n| Battery life | 12 hours (buds), 48 hours (with charging case) | \n| Weight | 4.9g (per bud) | \n| Connectivity | Bluetooth 6.0 | \n| Frequency range | 20Hz-40kHz | \n| Waterproofing | IP55 | \nShould I buy the JBL Live Beam 4?\n| Attribute | Notes | Score | \n|---|---|---|\n| Features | Broad feature-set within the charging case and app, but ANC and call quality could be better. | 4/5 | \n| Sound quality | Impactful bass, rich mids, and vibrant treble and broad soundstage make for excellent audio. | 4.5/5 | \n| Design | Ultra-comfortable in-ear fit, IP55-rated, and case is well-built, albeit bulky. | 4.5/5 | \n| Value | A good price for earbuds that are this feature-rich and sound this good. | 4.5/5 | \nBuy them if…\nYou want the best sound-per-pound audio quality\nFor a pair of wireless earbuds in this price bracket, the JBL Live Beam 4 are excellent performers. They’re a little bass forward, but that doesn’t come at the expense of rich mids or vibrant treble. And with LDAC on, you can enjoy an even more detailed listen time after time.\nYou’re drawn in by the screen-fitted charging case\nIf you’re tempted by the Beam 4’s touchscreen case, then I have good news for you — it works like a dream. With a revamped UI, plenty of customization options, and tools like a timer and flashlight, there’s plenty of functionality to play around with. I also enjoyed selecting a photo from my phone’s library as a custom background — it’s such a thoughtful touch.\nDon’t buy them if…\nYou want top-tier ANC\nWhile the ANC on these earbuds is pretty decent, it’s not exactly class leading. If you want top-of-the-range noise-crushing capabilities, I’d still recommend checking out Bose’s flagship in-ears.\nYou want spectacular spatial audio\nThe spatial audio setting on JBL’s new Live Beam earbuds is by no means spectacular — in fact, it’s barely serviceable. Vocals lose clarity and songs will generally have a more compressed quality, meaning they deliver disappointing spatial sound.\nJBL Live Beam 4 review: also consider\n| Row 0 - Cell 0 | JBL Live Beam 4 | Apple AirPods Pro 3 | Cambridge Audio Melomania A100 | \n| Price | £179.99 / AU$269.95 (about $240) | $249 / £219 / AU$429 | $149 / £119 / AU$199 | \n| Drivers | 10mm dynamic | Custom high-excursion | 10mm dynamic | \n| Active noise cancellation | Yes | Yes | Yes | \n| Battery life | 12 hours (buds), 48 hours (with charging case) | 8 hours (buds), 24 hours (with charging case) | 11 hours (buds), 39 hours (with charging case) | \n| Weight | 4.9g (per bud) | 5.6g (per bud) | 4.7g (per bud) | \n| Connectivity | Bluetooth 6.0 | Bluetooth 5.3 | Bluetooth 5.4 | \n| Frequency range | 20Hz-40kHz | 20Hz-20kHz | 20Hz-20kHz | \n| Waterproofing | IP55 | IP57 | IPX5 | \nApple AirPods Pro 3\nApple’s flagship noise-cancelling earbuds are a little pricier than this JBL model, but they’re occasionally on-sale for less than $230 / £200 / AU$350. And they offer excellent quality, with emotive, spacious sound, awesome ANC, and an ultra-comfortable fit. They could have better battery life, but otherwise, they’re a great pair of in-ears. Read our full Apple AirPods Pro 3 review.\nCambridge Audio Melomania A100\nWe were huge fans of the Cambridge Audio Melomania A100 earbuds when we tested them last year. With impeccable sound quality, impressive noise cancelling, and a tempting low price, they really are the full package. Their bass output is a little less striking than that of the Live Beam 4, but we’d still recommend them without question. Read our full Cambridge Audio Melomania A100 review.\nHow I tested the JBL Live Beam 4\n- I tested over the course of multiple weeks\n- Mainly used Tidal when assessing audio quality\n- Took with me on walks, public transport, and to the office\nI spent multiple weeks testing the JBL Live Beam 4, and took them with me just about everywhere I went. This meant that I was able to test the buds’ noise cancelling in a wide variety of environments, including buses, the office, and while on walks in my city.\nWhen assessing audio quality, I mainly used the music streaming app, Tidal, which supports higher-resolution streaming. I did also dip into Spotify from time to time, however. During my time with the Beam 4, I made sure to cycle through the TechRadar testing playlist, which features tracks from a wide variety of genres, but I also bumped a ton of tunes from my personal library as well.\nMore generally, I’ve spent years testing audio gear here at TechRadar, and have reviewed everything from hulking party speakers to audiophile-friendly wired and wireless headphones. I’ve also tested several pairs of wireless earbuds from the likes of LG and Sony, so I know exactly what it takes for a product to stand out in this competitive segment of the market.\n- Read TechRadar’s reviews guarantee\n- First reviewed: August 2026\nHarry is a Senior Reviews Writer for TechRadar. He reviews everything from party speakers to wall chargers and has a particular interest in the worlds of audio and gaming. Harry has a background in business tech journalism, particularly around the telecoms industry.\nYou must confirm your public display name before commenting\nPlease logout and then login again, you will then be prompted to enter your display name.",
        "content": "TechRadar Verdict\nWith stellar sound quality, a quirky charging case, and an ultra-comfortable fit, the JBL Live Beam 4 might just be my favorite wireless earbuds of 2026 so far. Yes, the ANC could be a little better — as could call quality — but their expansive set of features, impressive battery life, and fair price still help them to challenge some of the best wireless earbuds around.\nPros\n- +E...",
