        "title": "Workaround for MySQL’s “can’t specify target table for update in FROM clause” Error",
        "link": "https://blog.jooq.org/workaround-for-mysqls-cant-specify-target-table-for-update-in-from-clause-error/",
        "source": "Java, SQL and jOOQ.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.474769+00:00",
        "image": "https://s0.wp.com/_si/?t=eyJpbWciOiJodHRwczpcL1wvaTAud3AuY29tXC9ibG9nLmpvb3Eub3JnXC93cC1jb250ZW50XC91cGxvYWRzXC8yMDE5XC8wNVwvY3JvcHBlZC1qb29xLWxvZ28td2hpdGUtdHJhbnNwYXJlbnQtNzUweDc1MC1wYWRkZWQtMi5wbmc_Zml0PTc1MCUyQzc1MCZzc2w9MSIsInR4dCI6IkphdmEsIFNRTCBhbmQgak9PUS4iLCJ0ZW1wbGF0ZSI6ImVkZ2UiLCJmb250IjoiIiwiYmxvZ19pZCI6MjUzNTI4NzB9.hU9kWcZI5TuUnIUVMNNtGeZHJwUtik_q343GZPVDA9UMQ",
        "full_content": "In MySQL, you cannot do this:\ncreate table t (i int primary key, j int);\ninsert into t values (1, 1);\nupdate t\nset j = (select max(j) from t) + 1;\nThe UPDATE statement will raise an error as follows:\nSQL Error [1093] [HY000]: You can’t specify target table ‘t’ for update in FROM clause\nPeople have considered this to be a bug in MySQL for ages, as most other RDBMS can do this without any issues, including MySQL clones:\n- MariaDB 10.2\n- SingleStore 6 (previously known as MemSQL)\nLuckily, jOOQ can easily transform such queries for you, whenever you’re trying to UPDATE or DELETE a target table, with a predicate that depends on the target table itself. In those cases, jOOQ will just apply the following workaround:\nupdate t\nset j = (\n  select *\n  from (\n    select max(j) from t\n  ) t\n) + 1;\nNow, the query works without any syntactic issues. Similar workarounds are documented in the MySQL docs, but with jOOQ, you simply don’t have to think about this limitation.",
        "content": "In MySQL, you cannot do this:\ncreate table t (i int primary key, j int);\ninsert into t values (1, 1);\nupdate t\nset j = (select max(j) from t) + 1;\nThe UPDATE statement will raise an error as follows:\nSQL Error [1093] [HY000]: You can’t specify target table ‘t’ for update in FROM clause\nPeople have considered this to be a bug in MySQL for ages, as most other RDBMS can do this without any issues, in...",
