        "image": "https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/FiJXf8gEJidRrjWaxKEUWR-2000-80.jpg",
        "full_content": "Google simplifies Pixel Watch 5 software with one unified build\nFlashing Pixel Watch 5 firmware still requires an exclusive debug adapter.\nWhat you need to know\n- Google just posted the official factory and OTA images for the Pixel Watch 5, introducing a unified build strategy.\n- The Pixel Watch 5 now uses a single software build (CD5A.260611.00) for both Wi-Fi and LTE models.\n- Flashing factory images still requires Google’s invite-only debug adapter.\nGoogle has released factory and OTA images for the Pixel Watch 5, and it seems the new smartwatch uses a single software build for both Bluetooth/Wi-Fi and LTE models, which could make future updates easier.\nThe Pixel Watch 5 is listed with the codename “goteria” and build CD5A.260611.00. Interestingly, it’s not split between Bluetooth/Wi-Fi and LTE models like previous generations (via 9to5Google).\nThat sounds like a small developer-facing change, but it could simplify Google’s internal software process by consolidating the builds and may reduce the complexity of future updates. The Pixel Watch 5 runs on Wear OS 7, which is based on Android 17.\nThere’s one more catch for anyone who thinks about tinkering with the software. As with the Pixel Watch 4, flashing the factory image still requires a debug adapter that Google only ships by invite. Google also notes that unlocking the bootloader will make the watch less secure and that installing a factory image will wipe it.\nThe Pixel Watch 5 is already receiving new software features like Gemini Intelligence and At a Glance along with hardware upgrades such as 3GB of RAM and 64GB of storage.\nWith the software foundation now in place, the September update should give us a better idea of how quickly Google can extend that experience to older Pixel Watches.\nGet the latest news from Android Central, your trusted companion in the world of Android\nJay Bonggolto always keeps a nose for news. He has been writing about consumer tech and apps for as long as he can remember, and he has used a variety of Android phones since falling in love with Jelly Bean. Send him a direct message via X or LinkedIn.\nYou must confirm your public display name before commenting\nPlease logout and then login again, you will then be prompted to enter your display name.",
