        "title": "ChatGPT's new Mac plugin analyzed my iMessages - and I found it surprisingly useful",
        "link": "https://www.zdnet.com/article/chatgpts-new-mac-plugin-analyzed-my-imessages-and-i-found-it-surprisingly-useful/",
        "source": "Latest news",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.250895+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.zdnet.com/a/img/resize/6765eafa7c972c7d6ed5da1574adef1ac529898c/2026/08/21/a1154655-5774-419d-87b7-9631adbf566d/figure-top-how-i-use-chatgpt-to-give-me-relationship-advice-based-on-my-text-messages.jpg?auto=webp&fit=crop&height=675&width=1200",
        "full_content": "ChatGPT's new Mac plugin analyzed my iMessages - and I found it surprisingly useful\n Follow ZDNET: Add us as a preferred source on Google.\nZDNET's key takeaways\n- ChatGPT can now connect to your text messages on your Mac.\n- You can ask ChatGPT to send a text or search your existing message.\n- Even cooler, you can ask the AI to analyze your texts.\nLike many people, I exchange frequent text messages with family, relatives, friends, and other individuals in my life. But I don't necessarily pause to understand how I'm communicating with them. Now I can ask ChatGPT for some armchair analysis based on those messages.\nOn Thursday, OpenAI rolled out a new plugin for ChatGPT that connects to your Apple iMessages, but only on the Mac. With this new skill, you can ask ChatGPT to send a text to someone on your behalf. OK, that's not a big deal, as you can simply use the Messages app to send the text yourself. You can also tell the AI to find and summarize specific texts based on name, topic, and other criteria. OK, that sounds useful.\n(Disclosure: Ziff Davis, ZDNET's parent company, filed an April 2025 lawsuit against OpenAI, alleging it infringed Ziff Davis copyrights in training and operating its AI systems.)\nBut the coolest part is asking ChatGPT to analyze your messages, either in general or with a specific person. In response, the AI suggests ways to communicate more effectively.\nHere's how this works.\nTo get started on your Mac, make sure you've updated ChatGPT to the latest version. In the app, click the ChatGPT menu at the top, select \"Check for Update,\" and then allow the latest one to download and install.\nNext, click your account name at the bottom of the left pane and select Settings. At the Settings screen, look for the Integrations section and select Plugins. Click the Browse directory button, then start typing the word \"Messages.\" Apple's Messages should appear among the results. Click the Install button next to its entry to add it.\nAlso: Using AI for therapy? Don't - it's bad for your mental health, APA warns\nAh, but there's more. You'll next be asked to grant ChatGPT the necessary permissions on your Mac to access your text messages. Yes, there are some privacy implications, as the AI needs to read your texts to deliver its results. If you're OK with this, then allow those permissions, and you're set.\nSend a message\nAs the first step, you might want to simply compose and send a text message to someone. At the prompt, type \"Send the following message to [name of person] using @.\" After you type the @, start typing the word Messages and then choose Apple Messages from the list of plugins. Type the message you wish to send and submit the prompt.\nYou then have to allow the message to be sent, as ChatGPT won't send it on its own. However, you can tell it to always allow sending to this chat to avoid the confirmation in the future.\nOK, as I said, that's not so amazing, as you can just as easily use the Messages app yourself to send a text. So let's try something else.\nSearch for and summarize messages\nHow about searching for particular messages? At the prompt, type \"Search for and summarize all the messages in @Messages that mention [topic]\" or \"Search for and summarize all messages in @Messages to [name of person.]\" In return, ChatGPT delivers those results for you to review. OK, that's interesting.\nAlso: Even OpenAI CEO Sam Altman thinks you shouldn't trust AI for therapy\nFor this one, I asked it to find and summarize all the text messages that mention my cat, Mr. Giggles. The response was enlightening. ChatGPT not only revealed the main topics of the various exchanges but also highlighted key results from the vet over the years, along with other valuable information.\nAnalyze messages\nNow let's try the analysis part. Want to see how your exchanges with someone else sound and get some communication advice?\nAt the prompt, type \"Analyze my messages in @Messages with [name of person].\" Here, ChatGPT describes the tone and topics of the messages and other details it notices about the exchanges. It may also offer suggestions on how to improve your communication.\nAlso: How to use ChatGPT: A beginner's guide to mastering OpenAI's chatbot\nFor example, I sent a message to a test account I use, complaining that the person kept canceling our dinner plans. In its analysis, ChatGPT told me that the message as I wrote it could invite defensiveness on the other person's part. The AI suggested how I could have better phrased my text to elicit a more productive response. I found that suggestion both illuminating and helpful.",
        "content": "ChatGPT's new Mac plugin analyzed my iMessages - and I found it surprisingly useful\n Follow ZDNET: Add us as a preferred source on Google.\nZDNET's key takeaways\n- ChatGPT can now connect to your text messages on your Mac.\n- You can ask ChatGPT to send a text or search your existing message.\n- Even cooler, you can ask the AI to analyze your texts.\nLike many people, I exchange frequent text messages...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "6m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "How AWS Marketplace is using AI agents to meet the rising demand for AI agents",
        "link": "https://www.zdnet.com/article/application-marketplaces-aws-ai-agents/",
        "source": "Latest news",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.250891+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.zdnet.com/a/img/resize/7bf3fe5c47d6fce96863ef9d50965a1fdcfaed30/2026/08/21/b84cc694-7397-403a-ba0d-15107736b3c1/tubescubesgettyimages-2269966544.jpg?auto=webp&fit=crop&height=675&width=1200",
        "full_content": "How AWS Marketplace is using AI agents to meet the rising demand for AI agents\nFollow ZDNET: Add us as a preferred source on Google.\nZDNET's key takeaways\n- AWS Marketplace uses AI agents to meet the rising demand for AI agents. \n- Agent-powered marketplaces extend support to due diligence and deployment.\n- Larger enterprise apps still require human sales teams.\nAWS Marketplace, one of the most well-known application marketplaces catering to enterprises of all sizes, is seeing rapid growth of its stable of AI agents, along with a rising number of traditional application offerings.\nA year ago, \"AI agents\" ranked 64th among keyword searches across the marketplace, but now ranks in third place, reported Matt Yanchyshyn, vice president of AWS Marketplace & Partner Services. Last year, the marketplace had about 1,000 agent offerings. This year, there are more than 4,000, he told ZDNET. Overall, there are now almost 40,000 applications.\nAlso: Business adoption of AI agents tripled this year - as measurable ROI emerges\nWhen it comes to application marketplaces, however, the AI agent story goes much deeper, as agents are making your jobs easier in the software development lifecycle. AI agents are embedded in app marketplaces such as AWS, handling the nitty-gritty administrative and due diligence tasks that are part of the software acquisition and deployment process.\nOverall, the global agentic procurement software market was valued at $1.2 billion in 2025, and is expected to see a compound annual growth rate of 24%, reaching nearly $9 billion by 2034, according to Market Intelo projections.\nHow marketplaces reduce IT overhead\n\"Agentic procurement software uses autonomous AI agents to plan, execute, and adapt multi-step procurement tasks without requiring a human to initiate each action,\" the report's authors state. \"Unlike traditional procure-to-pay or strategic sourcing tools that automate individual steps within a human-driven workflow, agentic systems can independently qualify vendors, run negotiation dialogues, apply budget constraints, and execute contracts end-to-end.\"\nLately, agent-powered marketplaces have been \"reducing a surprising amount of IT overhead by helping with application discovery, vendor comparison, licensing, entitlement management, auditing, renewals and even portions of contracting and procurement,\" agreed Bret Greenstein, chief AI officer at West Monroe Partners.\nAlso: 'Specialists aren't required' anymore: How to stay valuable in an AI agent workplace today\nThe ability to extend marketplace services beyond the purchasing stage to the deployment stage is advancing up the food chain from smaller apps to larger-scale enterprise applications. \"We are supporting not just discovery, but deployment,\" Yanchyshyn said. \"Due diligence is an area where we're seeing a lot of adoption, beyond traditional pricing and transaction support.\"\nThe rudimentary app-store approach, the self-service \"click-and-go,\" still covers much of the AWS Marketplace, said Yanchyshyn. However, the addition of agentic capabilities \"is much more about customer success, helping people to deploy with success, private pricing, and support bigger deals.\"\nIndustry observers concur that agentic software acquisition and deployment through agentic-powered marketplaces is a boon to buyers, but add some caveats. \"The ability to start with a natural-language description makes it easy for software buyers to discover the solution they need,\" said Shashi Bellamkonda, principal research director at Info-Tech Research Group. However, he cautioned, AI agents \"may only look at less than 70% of the solutions available. This is not an AWS issue but the way the models work.\"\nTo fully take advantage of agent-powered marketplace searches, Bellamkonda advised engaging in \"more reprompting and adding more details.\" At the same time, always question the output from their marketplace engagements, he added. \"Treat it as a high-quality product but not a finished decision, and study the draft through human eyes and not use it 'as is.'\"\nAlso: The 3 types of people who will excel in the AI agent era, according to tech leaders\nAt the same time, such automated features raise the stakes for governance. \"When software can be discovered, evaluated and provisioned faster, enterprises need clearer policies around approved vendors, security, data access, architecture, licensing and agent authority,\" Greenstein cautioned.\nSimultaneously, agent-powered marketplaces are increasingly helping buyers find their own agents. Increasingly, more customers \"are looking for specific agents, or things that incorporate agents easily,\" said Yanchyshyn. \"Initially, people wanted just an agent, like an Atomic MCP server. And they want agentic capabilities, but don't necessarily want a huge army of standalone agents. People are still looking for SaaS, but they're looking for agentic SaaS that other agents can talk to.\"\nSoftware or SaaS vendors themselves are seeing the advantages of providing agent-powered discovery and follow-up when offering their products. CharmHealth, for example, provides an AI assistant called Ria designed to help prospective buyers evaluate its products while streamlining onboarding.\nNo replacement for human reps\n\"Instead of waiting for a sales representative to schedule a demo, buyers can get the information they need and explore software on their own time and at their own pace,\" said Pramila Srinivasan, CEO of CharmHealth. \"There's another benefit to this approach: Ria can collect and pass along higher-quality information to our sales professionals. By the time a prospect connects with a salesperson, they have a better understanding of what the prospect is looking for, what they've already explored, and where they may have questions.\"\nAlso: The autonomous business is coming. Why that's good news for professionals\nAWS' Yanchyshyn doesn't see agent-powered marketplaces taking the place of live human sales representatives or engineers anytime soon, either.\n\"Medium and lower-level deals are increasingly getting automated, the marketplace serves as a solution-matching engine,\" he noted. \"However, more sophisticated deals with customer configurations and custom pricing with salespeople is not going away. With big deals and three-year private pricing, you need a sophisticated, experienced sales team on one end and procurement team on the other.\"",
        "content": "How AWS Marketplace is using AI agents to meet the rising demand for AI agents\nFollow ZDNET: Add us as a preferred source on Google.\nZDNET's key takeaways\n- AWS Marketplace uses AI agents to meet the rising demand for AI agents. \n- Agent-powered marketplaces extend support to due diligence and deployment.\n- Larger enterprise apps still require human sales teams.\nAWS Marketplace, one of the most we...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "6m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "Walmart will finally accept Apple Pay and Google Pay. Here's what's changing",
        "link": "https://www.zdnet.com/article/walmart-now-accepts-google-pay-apple-pay/",
        "source": "Latest news",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.250888+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.zdnet.com/a/img/resize/b9877f25d45d7fe782df28e1f2bd3545c8222023/2026/08/21/fc5877fc-4ac4-4cd2-a5f1-e0a4e14feb47/walmart-screenshot-2026-08-21-140830.png?auto=webp&fit=crop&height=675&width=1200",
        "full_content": "Walmart will finally accept Apple Pay and Google Pay. Here's what's changing\nFollow ZDNET: Add us as a preferred source on Google.\nZDNET's key takeaways\n- Walmart has officially adopted tap-to-pay with both Apple Pay and Google Pay.\n- It was the last major retailer to accept them, instead pushing its own Walmart Pay.\n- There is a way Walmart can still push its own payment system and reap the benefits.\nThis morning, Walmart announced it will begin accepting tap-to-pay payments from credit cards, Google Pay, Apple Pay, and other NFC systems. Meanwhile, Walmart's QR code payment system is the last major holdout from the tap-to-pay revolution that has swept across the US over the past several years. At this point, about 85% of retailers in the US accept tap-to-pay.\nObviously, tap-to-pay is popular because of its speed at checkout -- it takes less time to tap your phone to the POS than to fish out a credit card (or, God forbid, count out cash). Plus, it adds security by encrypting your payment information into a one-time-use token that only banks can decipher. That way, even if the company suffers a data breach, only the token is compromised -- not your actual payment information.\nAlso: How to use Google Wallet - and why you should already\nAs someone who has proudly not carried cash for almost 10 years and has used my phone for payments, I've been interested in various payment platforms -- including Walmart Pay. I always carry credit cards with me for those rare times I can't use my phone, but they've stayed pretty secure in my wallet for a while now.\nI've used Walmart Pay frequently, but it's never been a great experience. The payment form is buried in the app, and over the years, it has moved to different places within the app, adding another layer of annoyance. Sometimes you can access it by scanning the QR code on the payment terminal with your phone's camera, which will (sometimes) get you to the right place in the app. Other times, it will simply direct you to the Walmart app. With tap-to-pay, it works the same way, the first time, every time.\nWhy does Walmart Pay still exist?\nWalmart Pay has been around for a long time despite the rising popularity of tap-to-pay. Walmart pushed its own payment system so it could avoid processing fees from Apple Pay and Google Pay -- which are minimal, but when you sell billions of dollars in product every year, they add up.\nThe other benefits of Walmart Pay are less financial. For example, when you use Walmart Pay, it defaults to sending an electronic receipt versus a paper one. Again, over billions of dollars of transactions, this adds up. Plus, Walmart gets to keep the data behind the transactions. It's another kind of loyalty program that adds up to a colossal amount of data about your buying habits, and the like.\nAlso: Google Wallet's latest feature is a game-changer for both parents and kids\nSo, the fact that Walmart has caved to the pressure of tap-to-pay speaks volumes. One way the retailer could continue to push its own payment platform is to do with products what it already does with gasoline. Walmart has gas stations across the country, and those who use Walmart Pay enjoy an additional 3 cents off per gallon. That's not a lot, granted, with national gas prices exceeding $5.00 per gallon. However, it's often enough to push the price of gas down below its competitors -- at least in my neighborhood.\nAs for Walmart Pay itself, it's sticking around. Walmart's own announcement says tap-to-pay \"is a great addition to payment options already offered like cash, credit card, or Walmart Pay.\" So, the QR code abides. Even if Walmart decided to deprecate the payment system, it would likely just morph into a loyalty program like Target Circle. There's simply too much customer data at stake to eliminate it entirely.\nAlso: The best TVs of 2026: Expert tested and recommended\nIt's likely that those who use Walmart Pay will continue to use it. People like me, however, will likely switch to tap-to-pay systems because there is less friction and virtually no incentive to use Walmart Pay -- except for gas. If Walmart really wants to keep people on Walmart Pay, it will need to find a way to save consumers a bit of scratch in the immediate future. Would a 1% discount on products for using Walmart's payment system be enough to encourage people to use it?\nIt would be enough for me, even though I'm a strong proponent of tap-to-pay systems. But in this economic environment, even saving a little bit helps, whether you're a regular Joe or Jane on the street or a massive retailer selling billions of dollars' worth of product every year.",
        "content": "Walmart will finally accept Apple Pay and Google Pay. Here's what's changing\nFollow ZDNET: Add us as a preferred source on Google.\nZDNET's key takeaways\n- Walmart has officially adopted tap-to-pay with both Apple Pay and Google Pay.\n- It was the last major retailer to accept them, instead pushing its own Walmart Pay.\n- There is a way Walmart can still push its own payment system and reap the benef...",
