        "image": "https://www.moneycrashers.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/MCLogo-color-800.png",
        "full_content": "Webull\n- Best for: Active options traders; anyone who wants professional grade charting without a platform fee\n- What Sets It Apart: $0 options contract fees where most large brokers charge $0.65; order flow charts and ladder trading on the free tier\n- Cost: $0 commissions and $0 account minimum; Webull Premium $3.99/month or $40/year; $0.50 per index options contract; $75 outgoing account transfer (*Prioprietory index options fees, regulatory fees, exchange fees may apply); new-account bonus 12 fractional shares at $3-$300 each plus one month of Premium on a $100 deposit by October 14, 2026.\n- Features: Stocks, fractional shares from $1, options, index options, futures, 70+ crypto coins, bonds, money market funds, IRAs, 0.20% managed portfolios, prediction markets, 24/5 overnight trading\n- Pros: No options contract fees on equity options; no account minimum; full toolset at no cost; free paperTrade with live data\n- Cons: Standard margin rate is 8.74% at every tier; 0.50% default cash management rate; 1.00% crypto spread\nOpen a Webull account if you trade options. Paying zero contract fees on equity options where Schwab and Fidelity charge $0.65 adds up fast on multi-leg strategies, and the charting and analysis tools come free. Add $40 a year for Premium if you plan to hold cash or use margin.\nEvery large brokerage advertises $0 commissions, which makes the headline useless for telling them apart. The real differences sit one layer down, in the per-contract fees, the margin rate, and what the broker pays on cash you have not invested yet.\nWebull competes on that second layer. It calls itself a technology company first and a brokerage second, and has built the US business on that premise since 2018. The St. Petersburg operation is one piece of a Nasdaq-listed group reporting more than 28 million registered users across 16 markets. Webull Financial LLC is the SEC-registered broker-dealer behind US accounts and a member of FINRA, SIPC, the NYSE, Nasdaq, and Cboe EDGX, with futures under Webull Futures LLC and advisory under the SEC-registered Webull Advisors LLC. SIPC covers securities to $500,000, including a $250,000 cash limit, plus excess coverage above that.\nWebull is also running one of the stronger new-account offers among the large brokers right now: deposit $100 by October 14, 2026, and you receive 12 free fractional shares, each worth $3 to $300, plus one month of Webull Premium. Rewards become claimable within 10 calendar days of the deposit settling. Joint, IRA, managed, entity, and event contract accounts are excluded.\nKey Features\nWebull runs one login across a wide product set, and pricing changes by product. The stock and options side is aggressively free. The rest carries its own numbers.\nWhat You Can Trade\nStocks and ETFs trade commission-free, with fractional shares starting at $1.00, IPO pre-orders, and recurring investments. Options cover the full range of multi-leg strategies, with a screener, probability analysis, and profit-and-loss diagrams. Futures trade through CME Group and Coinbase Derivatives contracts and need a $500 balance to start.\nThe same account holds crypto across more than 70 coins, US Treasury and corporate bonds starting at $100 through fractional bonds, and three money market funds with minimums as low as $1. Retirement savers get traditional, Roth, and rollover IRAs, with 401(k) rollovers handled through Capitalize. Mutual funds are IRA-only and start at $1 on select funds. Webull Advisors manages portfolios for 0.20% a year. Prediction markets add yes-or-no event contracts on sports, economics, and more.\nAccess runs from 4:00 AM to 8:00 PM Eastern, and overnight trading pushes select stocks and ETFs onto a 24-hour, five-day schedule, whole shares only.\nCommissions and Fees\nCommissions are $0 on US-listed stocks, ETFs, and options, and the number worth noticing is the contract fee. Webull charges nothing per options contract. Its own published comparison puts Charles Schwab and Fidelity at $0.65 per contract, which turns 20 iron condors into $52 at those brokers and $0 at Webull.\nIndex options are the exception. Webull adds $0.50 per contract on tickers such as SPX, VIX, and NDX, then passes through the exchange proprietary fee, which runs from $0.10 to $0.90 depending on the ticker and the premium. Nanos, SPIKEs, and XND carry no exchange fee. Orders above 500 contracts cost $0.10 per contract, with index options excepted.\nOther products carry their own pricing. Crypto trades include a 1.00% spread. Bonds carry a 0.1% markup on principal, with a $10 minimum on corporate bonds. Futures cost $1.00 per regular outright contract, $0.60 per mini, and $0.25 per micro.\nMoving money is mostly free. ACH transfers cost nothing either way. Outgoing wires cost $25, debit card withdrawals cost 1.75% of the amount, and transferring your account to another broker costs $75.\nWebull Premium\nWebull Premium costs $3.99 a month or $40 a year, and it is where the best numbers live.\n- Cash rate: 3.35% APY on uninvested cash against 0.50% without it, limited to individual and joint cash accounts\n- Margin: 5.20% on balances up to $25,000, falling to 3.90% above $25 million, against a flat 8.74% standard rate\n- IRA matching: 3.5% on contributions against 1%, plus a limited-time 3% match on transfers and rollovers that standard accounts do not receive\n- Index options: $0.40 per contract above 1,000 contracts a month and $0.30 above 5,000, while standard accounts stay at $0.50\n- Market data: Nasdaq TotalView Level 2 and OPRA real-time quotes included\nWhat Premium Is Worth\nWhether the subscription pays for itself is arithmetic, and the break-even is low.\nOn cash, the gap between the Premium and standard rates is 2.85 percentage points. Dividing the $40 annual fee by that spread puts break-even at roughly $1,400 of idle cash, by my calculation. On margin, the gap at the smallest tier is 3.54 points, or about $1,130 of average borrowing. Anyone with a margin balance or a few thousand in cash clears both bars.\nMarket data shortens the math further. Nasdaq TotalView Level 2 and OPRA real-time options quotes each run $2.99 a month on a standard account and cost nothing with Premium. Anyone who uses both feeds covers the $3.99 monthly subscription on data alone, before the cash and margin rates enter the calculation.\nThe standard margin rate is flat. At 8.74% it applies whether you borrow $1,000 or $25 million, and every tier discount in the published schedule runs through Premium. Webull’s own comparison puts that standard rate below Charles Schwab and E*Trade at every tier they publish, and above Fidelity, so the flat structure matters more than the rate.\nOne alternative sits inside the account. Webull offers three money market funds with minimums as low as $1, which earn a market yield on cash without any subscription. That does nothing for the margin rate or commission discounts, so weigh the subscription on those, not on cash alone.\nThe Platform and Getting Started\nA brokerage account opens with no minimum deposit. Margin approval needs $2,000, futures $500. Funding by ACH or debit card is free.\nThe desktop application runs on Mac, Windows, and Linux, alongside mobile apps and a browser version called WebTrade, with unlimited chart layouts across all three. Orderflow, the footprint and volume profile module, is a separate subscription at $16.99 a month or $169.99 a year. Traders who automate can route orders through Webull’s OpenAPI.\nPractice is free and unusually complete. The paperTrade simulator covers every asset class with live Level 2 data and real-time fills, carries more than 60 technical indicators and 17 charting tools, runs the same Turbo Trader and Ladder Trader interfaces as the live platform, and stays open on weekends for crypto and event contracts. Webull Learn adds more than 500 courses.\nOptions approval unlocks a month of free OPRA quotes, which stay free as long as you trade options at least once a calendar month.\nDay trading rules changed in 2026. The pattern day trader rule ended on June 3, replaced the next day by intraday margin requirements that remove both the day-trade counter and the $25,000 account threshold. Webull supports the new framework, so an account under $25,000 can day trade without the old three-trades-per-five-days limit.\nPros\n- No options contract fee. Stock and ETF options cost nothing per contract, against $0.65 at several large competitors by Webull’s own comparison. On multi-leg strategies that difference compounds fast.\n- No account minimum. A brokerage account opens at $0 and fractional shares start at $1.00, so the platform works from the first dollar.\n- Professional tools at no extra cost. Ladder trading, unlimited chart layouts, a full options analysis suite, and real-time fundamentals come with the account, not a paid tier.\n- A cheap managed option. Webull Advisors charges 0.20% a year, below the 0.25% Webull cites for Robinhood, Wealthfront, and Betterment Digital.\n- A serious practice mode. paperTrade runs live data and the real trading interfaces across every asset class, not a stripped-down demo.\n- Extended and overnight access. Trading runs from 4:00 AM to 8:00 PM Eastern, and select stocks and ETFs trade around the clock five days a week.\nCons\n- Margin pricing does not tier without Premium. The standard rate is a flat 8.74% at every loan size, so a large borrower gets no volume discount without subscribing.\n- The higher cash rate requires Premium. Uninvested cash earns 0.50% by default, and reaching 3.35% means paying $40 a year. That rate covers individual and joint cash accounts only.\n- Index options carry a contract fee. A $0.50 per-contract Webull fee plus the exchange proprietary fee applies to SPX, VIX, and NDX trades, unlike stock and ETF options.\n- Crypto carries a 1.00% spread. That cost is built into every crypto trade, which works out to $10 on a $1,000 purchase.\nFinal Word\nWebull earns its place for the trader who uses options. Zero contract fees on stock and ETF options is the rare fee difference large enough to move annual costs, and the options analysis around it costs nothing extra. Add a real $0 minimum, fractional shares from a dollar, and a complete practice simulator, and the free tier delivers more than the price suggests.\nThe account asks more of you if you want its best rates. Uninvested cash earns 0.50% by default and margin pricing only tiers for subscribers, so the stronger version of each sits behind $40 a year and the account rewards a decision rather than passive use. Long-term investors still get $0 commissions, fractional shares from $1, IRA matching, and 0.20% managed portfolios, though options pricing will not be what draws them. If you trade options, plan to use margin, or expect to hold more than a couple thousand dollars in cash, run the arithmetic on Webull Premium before you fund the account, not after.\nOne factor that sits outside the pricing analysis: new accounts receive 12 free fractional shares worth $3 to $300 each, plus one month of Webull Premium, on a $100 deposit made by October 14, 2026, and a separate referral program pays 20 more per friend for up to 10 friends. The offer applies to individual taxable accounts; joint, IRA, managed, entity, and event contract accounts are excluded. Promotional terms change without notice, so confirm the current offer before you fund.\nWebull Financial LLC, Member SIPC, FINRA. Investing involves risk. More info at webull.com/disclosures. Options are not suitable for everyone. Losses can occur quickly. Review ODD at webull.com/disclosures. Subscription required. Fees may apply. More info at webull.com/disclosures\nEditorial & Advertiser Disclosure: The editorial content on this page is not provided, commissioned, reviewed, approved, or otherwise endorsed by any advertiser. Opinions expressed here are ours alone, not those of any advertiser. The offers that appear on this site are from companies that compensate us. That compensation may influence which products we cover and where and how they appear on a page – including the order in which they appear – but it does not influence our evaluations, ratings, or opinions. We do not include every company or offer available in the marketplace.\nRelated:",
