        "full_content": "INDIA\nMumbai Police have approached the Union Home Ministry to use Interpol to serve proclamation orders against six Pakistani 26/11 accused, including Hafiz Saeed and Zaki-ur-Rehman Lakhvi.\nThe Mumbai Police have approached the Union Home Ministry to facilitate service of court proclamation orders through Interpol against six Pakistani nationals accused in the 26/11 Mumbai terror attacks, paving the way for their possible trial in absentia.\nSpecial public prosecutor Ujjwal Nikam informed the special court on Friday that an interim report had been submitted regarding the proclamation orders issued in July against the absconding accused.\n“We have written to the Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA) for taking necessary steps in serving the proclamation order, through Interpol, against the accused residing in Pakistan,” Nikam said.\nHe added that the MHA acknowledged the request on August 3 and sought time to submit a status report.\nThe proclamation orders have been issued against Lashkar-e-Taiba (LeT) founders Hafiz Saeed and Zaki-ur-Rehman Lakhvi, along with Sajid Mir, Abu Alqama, Asim alias Abu Qahafa and Major Abdur Rehman Pasha.\nAll six are Pakistani nationals and have been accused of having roles in the 2008 Mumbai terror attacks. Their alleged involvement was also mentioned by Ajmal Kasab, the lone surviving Pakistani terrorist captured during the attacks, and David Headley, who later became an approver in the case.\nThe move comes under Section 356 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023, which provides for criminal trials in absentia in cases involving proclaimed offenders who have absconded to evade trial and where there is no immediate possibility of their arrest.\nThe court had issued the proclamation orders against the six accused as part of the process to initiate proceedings for a trial in their absence. Kasab was convicted for mass murder and waging war against India and was sentenced to death. He was hanged at Yerwada Central Jail in Pune on November 21, 2012.\nThe November 2008 Mumbai attacks involved 10 Pakistani terrorists who carried out a coordinated assault across the city for more than 60 hours. A total of 166 people, including six American nationals, were killed in the attacks, which targeted several prominent locations in Mumbai.",
