        "title": "Employers forced to wait 12 months for overseas nurses, teachers",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-22/nurses-teachers-overseas-visa-applicant-delay/107065692",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.248835+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/47e2042e0cb4b88a988f216e6ae2ba15?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2250&cropW=4000&xPos=0&yPos=209&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Nurses and teachers facing 12-month wait to get to Australia under visa overhaul\nIn short:\nOverseas skilled visa applicants not working in defence or law enforcement have seen their wait times blow out as a result of shift in visa priorities by the government.\nBusiness leaders have said the changes have forced offshore nurses and teachers to the \"emergency stopping lane\".\nWhat's next?\nHome Affairs Minister Tony Burke faces pressure to reduce migration and is set to announce a more comprehensive overhaul of the system.\nEmployers wanting to bring nurses, teachers and other skilled workers to Australia are facing waits of up to 12 months, under a far-reaching directive issued by the Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke to reduce overseas arrivals.\nThe directive, issued in July, determines the order in which the department processes skilled and employer-sponsored visas, prioritising applications made by migrant workers already in Australia over offshore applicants.\nWorkers in defence and law enforcement are now at front of the queue for visa processing, or in the first of the five tiers, followed by workers in construction, healthcare and teaching who apply from Australia.\nBut any migrant applying for a skilled visa from overseas — who is not in defence or law enforcement — has been bumped to the back of the queue, in effect pausing applications, as the government seeks to put its foot on the brake to stop people coming to the country.\nBefore the new rules were introduced, Home Affairs was working through a 12-month backlog of applications for onshore permanent and temporary skilled visas, according to sources in the sector who note processing times differ depending on the exact visa type.\nJamie Lingham, CEO of Absolute Immigration Group, criticised the directive as a \"blunt tool\".\n\"The impact is huge and I don't think everyone has grasped the reality of what's happening,\" Mr Lingham said.\n\"Why aren't there priority areas for offshore applications too? It means your marketing manager could get in before your nurse.\"\nBusiness groups have slammed the delays \"being built into the system\" which have seen decisions on visas blow out, in some cases, from \"a few weeks to close to a year\", according to Australian Industry Group CEO Innes Willox.\n\"The longer the pause goes on the more we risk robbing Peter to pay Paul — appearing to soothe public concern about migration at the cost of our long-term development and maintaining living standards,\" Mr Willox said.\n\"We have unfortunately gone from a processing fast lane implemented by the government after COVID to the emergency stopping lane.\"\nAustralian Chamber of Commerce and Industry CEO Andrew McKellar has also expressed alarm at the change, which comes on top of a separate directive which effectively paused the processing of Working Holiday Maker, or backpacker, visas in July.\n\"You now have the government putting in place a sequence of measures to slow the processing of skilled visas, responding to a short-term political imperative rather than focusing on the proper administration of system and the urgent skills needs of business,\" Mr McKellar said.\nWorking rights under cloud\nUnder pressure to reduce migration, Immigration Minister Tony Burke is working on a more comprehensive overhaul of the system which was due to be announced at the National Press Club a fortnight ago, before the minister was forced to postpone.\nCentral to the new policy will be a clampdown on temporary visas to reduce Net Overseas Migration (NOM) from the current 300,000 to Labor's target of 225,000 by 2028.\nThe figure, which represents the number of people coming to live in Australia minus those departing to live overseas, reached a peak of 556,000 when international borders reopened after COVID and has not fallen as fast as forecast.\nAs revealed by the ABC, the government is considering removing the work rights of visa-holders whose claims for protection have been rejected to crack down on the migrants who stay in Australia for years longer than expected on bridging visas.\nWhen asked if the proposal was on the table, Mr Burke said: \"I'm not going to start ruling in and out when we're in the middle of a process.\"\nBackpackers, students targeted\nBusiness leaders, including Mr Willox, have pleaded with the minister to announce the details of the new migration policy, and said, \"the quicker the minister can outline future policy and intake settings the better\".\n\"Given that there is a global war for talent, we hope the current settings are in place for a very short time,\" Mr Willox told ABC, referring to the new directives.\nMr Burke would not give any hints when asked today, but defended the process so far.\n\"I think the silliest thing is to get into the exact situation where you haven't completed the consultation and you give the speech anyway and that's exactly what Andrew Bragg did,\" Mr Burke said.\nSenator Bragg was counselled by Opposition Leader Angus Taylor for using a speech to suggest the Coalition should bring NOM down to 180,000 a year by cutting both student and backpacker visa numbers.\nThe comments exposed a rift with the Nationals who want to cut overall migration numbers but are refusing to touch the backpacker visas many farmers and regional communities rely on.\nOne Nation leader Pauline Hanson has also vowed to ring fence backpacker visas from any cuts, instead calling for international student numbers to slashed.\nMore than 528,000 migrants are currently in Australia on student visas, up from 383,910 in 2017, while the number of backpacker visas has risen from 146,340 to 225,650 in the same period.\nThe number of people on a bridging visa has more than tripled since 2017 from 130,450 to 413,000.",
