    {
        "title": "Batteries send power prices up in midnight charging mystery",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-22/batteries-pushed-power-prices-up-in-midnight-charging-surge/107047470",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.248821+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/4349ebcfda076dc6b7b00e7b399e24c0?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=1688&cropW=3000&xPos=0&yPos=552&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Batteries pushed power prices sky-high in WA's midnight charging surge\nIn the wee hours of a cold night last month, as most West Australians slept in their beds, a strange thing happened in the state's biggest and most important electricity system.\nAbout 1am, prices on the spot market started to soar.\nThey had been hovering at an elevated level of roughly $120 per megawatt-hour but suddenly began to climb to extreme highs of more than $350/MWh.\nDriving that jump in prices was no catastrophic loss of a high-voltage power line or a major coal-fired plant.\nIt was a spike in demand and it was coming from an unexpected source.\nThe surge was coming from batteries, the very things supposedly designed to prevent just such a breakout in prices.\nWhy it happened — in the middle of the night when there was seemingly no obvious reason — is still unclear.\nBut experts say the episode highlights how the way batteries are managed will determine their impact on prices as Australia transitions away from fossil fuels.\nA midnight mystery\nOne person who has been seeking answers is Marcus Freese, an analyst at Modo Energy.\nHe says a few things are known based on publicly available data.\nFor one, the batteries that charged between 1am and 2am on July 12 were all owned by the state-owned WA power provider Synergy.\nFreese says the effect was significant.\nCombined, Synergy's four big batteries added more than 30 per cent to demand for power from the grid at a time when prices were already high.\n\"The market was not tight because of demand or renewable output,\" Freese wrote in a report.\n\"Underlying demand was unremarkable for the time of day, in line with the July average for that hour, and wind generation was close to its July average.\n\"The price rise instead coincided with Synergy increasing charging from 229 (megawatts) to 640MW, which added around a third to system demand at the overnight trough.\"\nFreese says it is not the first time Synergy has materially pushed up prices in the market through the \"coordinated charging\" of its battery fleet.\nHe says it appears to make little sense as a tactic.\nNot only does it push up prices that will ultimately be borne by everyone, Freese says it is not even profitable for Synergy's batteries.\nIn this case, on July 12, he says Synergy's battery fleet lost $383,000 across the day.\nOver the last 13 months, Freese says Synergy has lost more than $100/MWh on 54 days, while it's earned more than $100/MWh on just 31 days.\nBy contrast, the company which owns WA's other two mega batteries, French firm Neoen, has made money much more often than not.\n\"We've now observed this dynamic across April, June, and July,\" Freese noted.\n\"Synergy's coordinated charging adds material load (demand) at the wrong time, lifts prices, and then buys into the spike it helped create.\"\nBig bets on batteries\nThe events in WA are being watched closely by energy observers across the country.\nMore than anywhere else in Australia — and, by some measures, the world — the state has led the charge into big batteries as a way to balance an electricity system turning towards renewable energy.\nFrom practically nothing three years ago, the amount of large-scale battery capacity in WA's main grid has risen to about 1400 megawatts.\nCollectively, those batteries regularly meet more than 20 per cent of demand for power at peak times and can sometimes provide more than a third.\nMichael Sarich, a senior vice president of global energy consultancy Rystad, says WA has bet big on batteries, partly because of a very real vulnerability.\nIts main electricity grid is an island, cut off from the rest of the world and unable to tap imports when times get tough.\nThose batteries, he says, are aimed at safeguarding that system.\nSarich says the daily flood of rooftop solar was previously pushing daytime prices — and demand for power from the grid — to ever lower levels.\nBut when the sun set and the solar panels stopped generating, he says prices were rising to highs that were even more extreme.\nSarich says batteries had flattened this price curve by soaking up excess renewable energy at times of surplus and discharging in times of scarcity.\n\"The role the batteries have done really well is meet that evening demand, which is why you're seeing the price curve is a lot flatter across the day,\" he explains.\n\"But the other thing you need to remember is batteries are not really generators. They're really just moving demand around throughout the day.\"\nBattery behaviour compounds winter woes\nThe strengths and weaknesses of batteries have been brought into sharp relief in WA in recent months.\nIn its most recent snapshot of WA's \"wholesale electricity market\", or WEM, the market operator noted batteries had indeed smoothed prices in the three months to June 30.\nThose prices, however, had flattened out at record high levels of almost $120/MWh.\nAccording to the Australian Energy Market Operator, coal plant and wind farm outages partly caused the price pain.\nBut so too did increased battery charging during the middle of the day and overnight, AEMO noted.\nSarich says these factors ran headlong into other inconveniences.\nHe says demand for electricity in winter is simply higher, while output from wind and solar is almost invariably lower.\nFor Sarich, winter, not summer, is now the biggest test of electricity systems and the people who run them.\nHe says WA, like the rest of the country, is moving to a system powered by renewable energy.\nAs such, it needs to be able to cope with extended periods in winter where the wind does not blow and the sun does not shine.\n\"Our view is over the next few years that wholesale prices are going to remain elevated because fundamentally it's all about supply and demand,\" Sarich says.\n\"You need to have enough supply from whatever source you've got.\"\nSarich says WA's safety net at the moment is gas-fired power, a relatively pricey form of power.\nBut he also notes that batteries are increasingly \"mirroring\" the behaviour of gas plants when they offer to supply their power to the market.\nAnd at times of scarce supply, as has been the case in WA over much of the winter, this meant the prices offered had more often than not been high.\nBizarrely, energy traders not authorised to speak to the ABC also noted gas generators had fired up at times to meet the demand created by Synergy's batteries.\nOne such energy trader described the events as \"crazy\".\n\"The other key thing is there's only a very small number of these batteries,\" Sarich says.\n\"So, they've got this sort of dominant position.\"\nWith Synergy's two remaining coal plants due to be retired next year and in 2029, Sarich says the need to build enough capacity to replace those units is becoming urgent.\nHe's optimistic it will, pointing to a slew of wind projects under construction or in the pipeline.\nSynergy would not be drawn on why its batteries were all charged at times when spot prices were high. A spokeswoman said they were a relatively new technology whose capabilities would be monitored and refined.\nShe said \"safe, reliable and lowest-cost\" energy systems needed diverse sources of supply.\nBatteries offered flexibility that would allow greater amounts of renewable energy \"while enhancing system reliability through rapid response to changing system conditions\".\nGrowing pains, growing tensions\nWA's Energy Minister, Amber-Jade Sanderson, also declined to comment on Synergy's trading behaviour.\nHowever, she did not back away from the state's plans to close its coal plants and make the switch to a heavy dependence on green power.\n\"We've always said that the most reliable and affordable energy mix for the future is renewables backed by batteries and gas,\" Ms Sanderson said.\nThe minister blamed outages — planned and unplanned — at coal plants and a wind farm undergoing expansion for the high prices in recent months.\n\"This demonstrates why we need more renewables to keep prices down,\" Ms Sanderson said.\nShe stressed that households and small businesses in WA would be shielded from any pain in the wholesale market because the government regulated prices for those customers.\nAmid the upheaval, Marcus Freese from Modo Energy says scrutiny needs to be applied to a battery fleet that now in a powerful position.\nHe notes batteries are now setting the prices in WA's main grid, whether charging or discharging, more than 90 per cent of the time.\nGiven this power and the inadequacy of current regulations, Freese says there is a risk Synergy could use batteries to push up wholesale prices to benefit the utility's fleet of other coal, gas and wind generators.\nHe argues this may have been the case in recent months, saying Synergy's batteries \"paid $7.3 million to charge whilst its generation earned $18.4 million\" across more than 70 high-price charging days.\n\"A high charging offer increases the price paid by the battery, but it can also increase the price earned by generation in the same portfolio,\" Freese wrote.\nLooking ahead, he says WA's battery fleet and the way it is managed will evolve.\nIndeed, he says it has already changed dramatically in the past year.\nRevenues from batteries trading energy had plunged, he notes, led by Synergy's losses.\nSo, too, had revenues for system strength and security services.\nNowadays, he says, WA batteries earned most of their money for simply being there through so-called capacity payments.\nThese are paid to generators or batteries as a type of insurance for making reliable capacity available when the system needs it.\nWhile a capacity market exists in WA, it does not in the national electricity market, which covers Australia's eastern seaboard.\nOn the eastern seaboard, batteries earn the lion's share of their revenues by trading energy — or the \"arbitrage\" between low and high prices — in the wholesale market.\nFreese says WA's experience suggests batteries could have a counterproductive effect on prices if they are not managed carefully.\n\"In a market with wide spreads and a clear daily arbitrage window, how you charge matters as much as when you discharge,\" Freese wrote.\nSarich says batteries are playing a crucial role in helping to keep the lights on by storing energy as the share of renewable generation grows.\nBut their role in driving down prices is a different matter.\n\"I don't necessarily believe they will lower prices,\" Sarich says.\n\"And the reason for that is they're not providing more generation.\n\"You kind of need to think of them as an enabler.\n\"It's sort of like the cost you need to pay to enable more wind and solar, which is adding more generation, and that will push the price down.\n\"Once you add these sort of batteries, they provide the role of making the whole system work better.\"",
