    {
        "title": "Israel's impunity has been virtually unaffected by collective concern",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-22/israel-gaza-west-bank-zomi-frankcom-mike-huckabee-netanyahu/107058398",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.248817+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/fd200e54effc2690504aab011dee1e9b?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=2131&cropW=3788&xPos=0&yPos=197&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "analysis\nIsraeli's increasing settler violence highlights the futility of concerned statements\nRecords of the village of Qusra, just south of Nablus in the Occupied West Bank, go back to Ottoman tax registers of 1596. There has been documentation of its existence and that of its inhabitants ever since.\nDocumenting a place, and a community, and the events that occur there, is always important.\nBut documenting Qusra has made it one of the stand-outs in the wider story of Israeli actions against Palestinian people which, amid the noise of Donald Trump's chaotic war against Iran, have only escalated in 2026.\nEarlier this month, Israeli settlers besieged three houses in Qusra telling the families that lived there they had to leave because the settlers were going to take their land.\nThe settlers cut off the water and electricity to the houses. They blocked the roads.\nSuch actions are not unusual in the West Bank. Nor are they by any means the worst that happen there.\nCoverage of Qusra easier than of Gaza\nLast month settlers set fire to the village's newly constructed mosque after an earlier clash in another village in which four Palestinians and two Israeli soldiers died, after a group of settlers entered an area of the West Bank for an \"unauthorised hike\", according to the Israeli military.\nBut the besieging of the three houses and their 15 inhabitants — which has continued with the UN allowed to deliver supplies — struck a chord in the international community, and particularly in the United States.\nThat was possibly because one of the houses that has now come under siege belongs to a Palestinian-American, Loai Ridi.\nBut it is mostly because the international media have been able to document what has been going on in real time, in real pictures.\nThe contrast with Gaza could not be more stark.\nIn Gaza, where the international media has not been allowed to independently enter since October 7, 2023, the killing of more than 70,000 people according to the Gaza Health Ministry, the wounding of many more, and the continuing appalling living conditions of around two million people on a small strip of land where the majority of buildings have been flattened, occupies a strange space in history where what we see and what are told bear little resemblance to each other.\nThe lack of international media access leaves it in a situation in which almost every event is contested, but the Israeli ban prevents the media from testing and filtering out false claims and information.\nThese Israeli statements reduce horrific events to the language of bureaucratic failure. Whether incidents are investigated at all, or go on to be a criminal investigation, they rarely result in any real accountability even if there is a fault admitted.\nIsrael clears soldiers over Australian aid worker\nThis week Australia expressed outrage at the findings of an internal IDF report which found \"serious failures\" in the process that led to the killings of Australian aid worker Zomi Frankcom and six colleagues in April 2024.\nThe IDF said \"the decisions of the commanders did not raise reasonable suspicion of criminal misconduct\".\nThe humanitarian aid group for which Frankcom worked, World Central Kitchen, said that \"at the time of the lethal attacks, the IDF knew that our team in Gaza was unarmed and posing a threat to nobody\".\n\"It had full visual clarity on our clearly marked humanitarian vehicles during the extended, multiple airstrikes. It knew our team's movements, identities and activities in advance,\" it said.\n\"The military investigation conflates different timelines and events, muddies the story and shifts the blame for the IDF's illegal conduct.\"\nIsraeli Ambassador to Australia Hillel Newman said there was no intention to kill innocent civilians and that while he could \"express deep sorrow and grief\" for the families he would not apologise over the deaths as that would have \"legal ramifications\".\n\"There are a lot of haters of Israel in Australia,\" he added. \"We have seen this time and time again.\"\nThe credibility of the IDF account was not helped by a reminder of two other notorious incidents at the time the findings were released.\nThe IDF announced it would be launching criminal investigations into the now globally famous incident in which a five year old girl, Hind Rajab, was left talking to emergency services in January 2024 from a car where she was surrounded by the bodies of her six dead relatives, after an IDF strike, only to eventually die, along with two paramedics killed by Israeli forces in an ambulance that was supposed to be on a negotiated path to rescue her.\nThe IDF said it would also launch a criminal investigation into the killing of 15 Palestinian paramedics in March 2025 in Rafah.\nPostmortems of the dead paramedics, whose bodies had been retrieved from a mass grave that had also buried their vehicles, revealed they had been killed at close-range with \"specific and intentional\" bullet wounds in heads and hearts.\nBut given the IDF's record of failure to bring people to account, and the time that has already passed since these events took place, there is already deep scepticism about the likely outcome.\nFor example, The Hind Rajab Foundation (HRF) — set up in the little girl's name to pursue legal action against those responsible for violence against Palestinians — said in a statement that has \"no confidence in the Israeli military's reported decision to open criminal investigations into the murder of Hind Rajab, six members of her family, and the two Palestinian Red Crescent paramedics\" or into the targeted killing of the 15 Palestinian paramedics.\n\"These reported investigations, even if they do occur, should not be mistaken for a genuine step toward justice\", the Foundation said.\n\"As numerous Palestinian and international legal and human rights organisations have documented for years, Israel's internal investigations into the criminal conduct of its own soldiers are fundamentally flawed and cannot be regarded as credible mechanisms of accountability.\"\nUS criticises Israel over West Bank\nBack in the West Bank this week, the IDF has played what would be politely called an ineffective role in ending the siege in Qusra.\nThe events seem to have been one of those lightning rod moments to galvanise public criticism of Israel and its actions in the West Bank.\nThat most notably came from the US Ambassador to Israel Mike Huckabee.\nThe evangelist is a longstanding supporter of Israeli settlements in the West Bank and follows the Israeli practice of calling the area by the biblical names of \"Judea and Samaria\" — to assert the traditional Hebrew ownership of the lands.\nYet Huckabee this week condemned the besieging settlers at Qusra as \"Israeli terrorists\" and said the siege was inexcusable.\nBut as the siege was occurring in front of the world's media, Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and his cabinet ministers attended ceremonies in the West Bank to promote massive new settlements across the illegally occupied territory.\nAustralia was one of 21 countries, along with Britain, France, Italy, Canada and Germany to sign a joint statement calling Israel's particular approval of a major settlement project which will effectively cut the West Bank in half — and further separate it from east Jerusalem \"unacceptable and a violation of international law\".\nA group that monitors and maps conflicts and protests, ACLED (Armed Conflict Location & Event Data) says it has recorded more than 80 incidents involving settlers in Qusra since the beginning of 2026, \"making it one of the three locations most affected by settler violence in the West Bank\".\n\"These incidents included property theft and destruction, razing lands, physical clashes with Palestinians, and shootings at Palestinians — one of these shootings killed a Palestinian on 14 March,\" the group said in a statement.\nAcross the West Bank, it says it has recorded \"300 incidents of land seizures between 1 January and 14 August, which is already higher than the 267 incidents recorded throughout 2025\".\n\"In such incidents, settlers established outposts, built roads, placed tents and mobile rooms on Palestinian land, or seized homes, leading to more daily friction and violence between settlers and Palestinians.\"\nThe United Nations recorded more than 2,200 Palestinians displaced by settler violence and access restrictions in the first half of 2026 alone.\nAll these actions by Israel, a country whose ambassador to Australia told the National Press Club in March this year \"will always be a beacon of hope\".\nIsrael's enemies, Newman said, \"seek its destruction for what it represents; a modern democracy, Western civilisation, and a national home for the Jewish people. There is no other country in the Middle East that upholds the same democratic ideals of Australia as Israel does.\"\nNetanyahu goes from Trump whisperer to Trump ignorer\nAre any of Israel's actions likely to change?\nThe answer to this has usually been that it is really only the United States that can apply any external pressure to Israel to change its actions.\nBut there is an election coming in Israel.\nDonald Trump also now shows little interest in the fate of the Palestinians, particularly with his Board of Peace plan blown up by the Israelis.\nNetanyahu long boasted to his country that he was the Trump Whisperer. Now he is just the Trump Ignorer.\nThere are still actions taken by the US which are clearly read as sops to Israel.\nThe US, for example this week sanctioned the president of the International Criminal Court and a prosecutor tasked with investigating crimes committed by Israel during its war on Gaza.\nBut it was the language used by the US secretary of state Marco Rubio in unveiling the new sanctions that was the most troubling.\nIn another time, it would have sounded positively unhinged for a senior American official to describe an international court as a \"corrupt and fatally politicised supranational court that has maliciously abused its authority and exceeded its mandate\".\nBut not in these times.\nLoading\nIsrael's ongoing impunity\nIsrael's actions in Gaza and the West Bank reflect a sense of impunity which only continues to grow.\nIt is equally reflected in its actions in the region.\nThis week Israel launched air strikes on a Syrian air base just 70 kilometres from the Turkish border.\nThe Syrian foreign ministry condemned the attacks and described them as an \"unjustified act of aggression, a flagrant violation of Syria's sovereignty and territorial integrity, and a dangerous escalation that threatens security and stability in the region\".\nBut, beyond the 'buffer zones' and incursions in Syrian territory that Israel has been making for some time (along with those which have effectively claimed about 20 per cent of Lebanon), the target of the strike on the air base was actually Turkiye, which Israel claims has breached an agreed status quo by taking steps to establish a military presence in Syria by letting Turkish troops deploy near the air base.\nOn Wednesday, Netanyahu said in a political podcast that Israel will \"not tolerate a Turkish military presence, which moves to the south, because it threatens us\".\nLike actions in the West Bank, the US has condemned the Israeli air strikes on the base as an \"unnecessary escalation\" and insisted the new Syrian government is not the threat the Netanyahu government argues it is.\nOnce again it is being ignored.\nOnce again that raises the question of what other countries, like Australia, really need to collectively do — rather than just speak firmly to ambassadors.\nLaura Tingle is the ABC's Global Affairs Editor.",
