    {
        "title": "How much does it cost to fight Pauline Hanson? Labor has options",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-22/labor-advertisements-one-nation-coalition-superannuation/107058402",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.248814+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/a0237e9d9b7798cc1228c51d075b821b?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=900&cropW=1600&xPos=0&yPos=0&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "analysis\nOne Nation's fickleness goes unpunished but Labor is figuring out how to fight it\nIf you have a spare $5, $10 or even $42, the Labor Party has a proposition for you: help stop Pauline Hanson.\nThe varying price tags are attached to a stream of digital fundraising pitches that offer a window into how Labor is approaching the electoral threat posed by One Nation.\nBut a look beyond the requests for loose change and a more sophisticated strategy is emerging.\nLabor is increasingly trying not to simply attack One Nation, but to bind Hanson and the Coalition together in voters' minds as partners in a shared agenda.\n\"One Nation and the Coalition are on a unity ticket to undermine universal super,\" one ad featured on Instagram asking for a $10 donation this week begins.\nFor the more spendthrift Labor supporter, a sponsored post claims One Nation is \"rising\" and the \"hard truth\" is the Liberals can \"only form government with Pauline Hanson\".\n\"If everyone seeing this ad donated just $5 today, we'd have the resources to take on One Nation and the Coalition,\" it says.\nA different post addressed to \"everyone who wants to stop One Nation\" asks for a $30 donation to Labor to help train party volunteers.\nLabor's digital marketing strategy is clear.\nFirst highlight Hanson's record on issues of workers' rights, pay and conditions.\nThen, warn the Coalition does not have the numbers to govern without One Nation. Increasingly, Labor is trying to argue its two opponents have a shared policy agenda.\nA shifting political agenda\nThis all played out in a fundraising text message sent to ALP supporters ostensibly addressed from Treasurer Jim Chalmers asking for a $42 donation because Liberal frontbencher Andrew Bragg \"wants to scrap your super\" and Hanson is \"backing him\".\nThis missive caught the attention of Hanson, who replied on X with the rebuttal Chalmers was \"full of shit\" as One Nation was not looking to end super.\nWhen this column asked Chalmers for a response, he said he was \"genuinely worried\" about the future of super \"in the hands of the Liberals, the Nationals and One Nation\".\n\"They do not support universal superannuation and preservation at its core,\" he said.\nBragg's appearance at the National Press Club last week provided the impetus for this latest example of the time-tested practice of a political party seizing on a news story to animate their supporters and raise funds.\nHanson's suggestion people be allowed to dip into their super when struggling with cost of living only galvanised Labor's argument its opponents wanted to raid Australians' retirement savings.\nThis month Labor's digital ads were framed around superannuation, last month it was Hanson's uncomplimentary comments about paid parental leave.\nBut as politically potent as those issues may be, One Nation hasn't taken these attacks lying down.\nAfter Labor asked supporters for a $27 donation to help fight Hanson in June, One Nation launched its \"Fire the Liar\" campaign in response and raised more than $4 million in just five days.\nThe amount of what's known as \"earned media\" — ie free publicity — Hanson's fundraising campaign attracted was quite unprecedented by Australian political standards.\nDespite this, Hanson has either plateaued or slipped a little in the polls following scrutiny on some of her policy positions.\nBut as Labor moves against one populist idea, another rises in its place.\nYesterday superannuation, today slashing the tobacco excise under the highly contested notion it would quash the illicit cigarette market.\nA test around the corner\nWhile many are looking to the Victorian state election in November as the first real world contest between Labor and a historically popular One Nation, there is another test coming sooner.\nNext Saturday residents in the WA state seat of Secret Harbour will vote in a by-election triggered by the retirement of the local Labor member.\nThe outer-suburban string of coastal towns south of Perth form exactly the sort of electorate where a mix of mortgage pressure, rapid population growth and voters willing to look beyond the major parties could prove a winning combination for One Nation.\nLiberal frontbencher Andrew Hastie, whose own federal seat of Canning overlaps with parts of Secret Harbour, certainly thinks so, criticising what he has felt was a lack of effort from his state colleagues.\n\"We should always strive to win, and we should signal that ambition to our voters,\" he told the Australian Financial Review this week.\nHanson's key advantage\nLabor is at an incredibly high water mark in WA and increasingly sound far from confident about retaining Secret Harbour themselves.\nThe not-so-subtle pivot from Labor sources in recent weeks has been to talk up the by-election as the source of an important \"lesson\" for how preferences might flow in future contests with the Liberals and One Nation.\nThough not totally ruling out their chances, the shifting view inside Labor that the seat could fall to One Nation also raises the stakes for Hanson.\nOne Labor source told this column Secret Harbour was \"exactly the type of seat\" One Nation could win.\n\"Where if not there?\" they said.\nPerhaps that pressure was a factor in Hanson's recent staunch defence of WA's GST deal, which has been quite the change in tune to her critical remarks of the past.\nHanson's GST conversion is a reminder of a key advantage she holds compared to arguably most other politicians.\nShe is often able to jettison a previous position no longer deemed politically useful without any blowback from voters.\nLabor is clearly getting more deliberate about how it fights One Nation, from the issues it chooses to prosecute to the way it increasingly links Hanson to the Coalition.\nBut Hanson is adapting too and historically has had considerable freedom to tailor her pitch to the political moment.\nLoading",
