    {
        "title": "The FNM’s New Blueprint: Is Dr. Rollins Worth the Political Gamble?",
        "link": "https://www.bahamaspress.com/the-fnms-new-blueprint-is-dr-rollins-worth-the-political-gamble/",
        "source": "Bahamaspress.com",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.248557+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.bahamaspress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/image-2-13.jpg",
        "full_content": "‘FNM needs to find a leader who can win’ – PINTARD LOST THREE ELECTIONS SINCE 2023! MICHAEL PINTARD CAN COUNT HIS POLITICAL DAYS AS FNM LEADER!!!\nDear Editor,\nThe fluorescent lights of the Free National Movement headquarters hummed, casting a sterile glow over the portraits of past leaders lining the boardroom wall. To a casual observer, it was a gallery of honor. To those who knew the party’s DNA, it was a graveyard of the “One-Yard Line.”\nCecil Wallace-Whitfield. Kendal Isaacs. Janet Bostwick. Tommy Turnquest. All titans. All brilliant. Yet, all of them had driven the ball ninety-nine yards down the gridiron, only to freeze at the goal line. They were architects of an institution, but they lacked the raw, destructive power needed to break the PLP’s defensive front.\nSitting at the end of the mahogany table, an elder of the party—a man who had marched in 1972—stared at the empty chair at the head of the room.\n“We are doing it again,” the elder muttered, his voice thick with the gravel of time. “We are trying to breed a gentleman leader from within a house built on infighting.”\nThe younger operatives looked up from their tablets. One spoke up, defending the current status quo. “Michael Pintard is fighting hard. Dr. Minnis has the base. Dr. Sands has the clinical prestige. We have options for the future.”\nThe elder let out a dry, mocking laugh that cut through the room like a cold front over the Great Bahama Bank.\n“You think the Bahamian electorate cares about prestige?” The old man leaned forward, slamming a weathered hand on the table. “The Bahamian electorate is a unique creature. When they are done with you, they are done. Right now, looking at Pintard, Minnis, and Sands is like a beautiful Bahamian woman watching an ugly, cheating man try to make a pass at her outside a shack in Over-the-Hill. It doesn’t matter how expensive his suit is, or how smooth his words are. She’s going to look at her friends, suck her teeth, and say, ‘I ain’t checkin’ fa him!’ The brand is burned. The faces are tired.”\nThe room went dead silent. The truth was heavy, and it was accurate.\n“So what do we do?” the young operative asked quietly. “Who punches it through?”\n“History doesn’t lie, it echoes,” the elder said, pointing a finger at a portrait of a young, fierce Hubert Alexander Ingraham. “In 1992, we couldn’t win with our own. We were too busy cutting each other’s throats. We had to take a man born of the PLP—a man who knew their playbook, knew their weaknesses, and possessed the unvarnished, raw audacity to carry the entire FNM on his back, smashing through the PLP line with every tackler hanging onto his jersey. Ingraham was a political bulldozer. And when he stepped away? We went right back to the playground fighting, forcing ‘Alexander’ to come back in 2007 to save us from ourselves.”\nThe elder stood up, walking to the window overlooking Nassau. The year was moving steadily toward 2031. The political landscape was parched, craving something explosive.\n“We don’t need a diplomat,” the old man continued. “We need a disruptor. We need Dr. Andre Rollins.”\nA murmur rippled through the room. Rollins. The firebrand. The man who had walked the halls of the PLP, stood up to his own leadership when he saw wrongdoing, and later shook the foundations of the FNM with an unapologetic, fiercely independent streak. He wasn’t a product of the FNM’s internal grooming machine, which meant the machine hadn’t broken his spirit.\n“Rollins is reckless,” someone whispered.\n“Rollins is fearless,” the elder corrected sharply. “The Bahamian people respect a man who doesn’t bow. They remember him as the man who spoke truth to power, regardless of the color of the shirt he wore. He has the Ingraham factor. He isn’t afraid to get blood on his uniform to win the game. He understands the PLP’s vulnerabilities because he stood in their councils, and he understands our flaws because he’s challenged them.”\nThe elder turned back to the room, his eyes flashing with the urgency of a man who wanted to see one more victory before his time was up.\n“In 2031, the public will be tired of the same old political dance. They won’t want the polite establishment. They will want a quarterback who sees the one-yard line and doesn’t look for a referee to make a call—they will want a man who will lower his shoulder, invite the collision, and drag the whole country into the end zone with him. Andre Rollins isn’t standard FNM. And that is exactly why he is the only thing that can save us.”\nHe picked up his hat, leaving the young operatives staring at the empty head of the table, suddenly seeing the ghost of 1992, and the wild, unpredictable promise of 2031.",
        "content": "‘FNM needs to find a leader who can win’ – PINTARD LOST THREE ELECTIONS SINCE 2023! MICHAEL PINTARD CAN COUNT HIS POLITICAL DAYS AS FNM LEADER!!!\nDear Editor,\nThe fluorescent lights of the Free National Movement headquarters hummed, casting a sterile glow over the portraits of past leaders lining the boardroom wall. To a casual observer, it was a gallery of honor. To those who knew the party’s DNA...",
