        "title": "Josh Brolin Nearly Quit The Dog Stars After One Day, “I’ve Got To Get The F— Out Of Here”",
        "link": "https://thoughtcatalog.com/xstockton/2026/08/josh-brolin-nearly-quit-the-dog-stars-after-one-day-ive-got-to-get-the-f-out-of-here/",
        "source": "Thought Catalog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.248332+00:00",
        "image": "https://thoughtcatalog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/josh-brolin-nearly-quit-the-dog-stars-after-one-day-i-ve-got-to-get-the-f-out-of.jpg?w=1536&h=768&crop=1",
        "full_content": "Josh Brolin Nearly Quit The Dog Stars After One Day, “I’ve Got To Get The F— Out Of Here”\nRidley Scott’s process spooked Josh Brolin so badly that he panicked just one day into filming “The Dog Stars.” He eventually made his peace with it, but he still describes the director’s method as “stratospherically creative and stratospherically dangerous.”\nBrolin told Empire Magazine he “got really scared” during his first day on the post-apocalyptic sci-fi, scared enough to phone his agent and ask to be let go. In his own words: “I remember calling my agent the first day and going, ‘Something’s really wrong and I’ve got to get the f— out of here.'” Things eventually settled, and he stayed with the film.\nThat reaction carries some weight coming from Brolin, who is far from a nervous newcomer. Across a Hollywood career that stretches back to the 1980s and “The Goonies,” he has worked with some of the industry’s most demanding filmmakers — from the Coen brothers on “No Country for Old Men” to Denis Villeneuve on “Sicario” and “Dune” — and earned an Oscar nomination along the way. When a veteran of that many exacting sets says a director’s approach rattled him, it lands as more than first-day jitters.\n“The Dog Stars” comes from Ridley Scott and Disney’s 20th Century Studios, adapted by Mark L. Smith from Peter Heller’s 2012 novel. It’s set after a flu pandemic wipes out most of humanity, following a former civilian pilot and an ex Marine.\nJacob Elordi took over the lead after Paul Mescal, Scott’s original pick, dropped out over scheduling conflicts with the Beatles biopics. Josh Brolin, Margaret Qualley and Guy Pearce round out the cast.\nThe film hits theaters Aug. 28.\nStay in the loop with Thought Catalog — follow us on Facebook or visit our website.",
