        "title": "What laws are there to protect tenants in Denmark?",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20241128/what-laws-are-there-to-protect-tenants-in-denmark",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473698+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/watermarks-logo-20241113-152559-7-1920x1279web-1.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "The two main laws you need to know about in Denmark are the Danish Rent Act (Lejeloven) and the Housing Regulation Act (Boligreguleringsloven).\nThe purpose of the Rent Act is to protect tenants through standardisation of things like rental contracts, rules for when landlords can give notices and what is expected of tenants when they move out.\nThe Housing Regulation Act gives tenants additional protections by regulating rent as well as things like renovation and maintenance of rental properties.\nThe two laws overlap somewhat in terms of the areas in which they offer protection. A table at the bottom of this article gives a general summary of this.\nContracts, rent and rent increases\nIf you want to know which rules apply to your rental contract, you should refer to the Rent Act. This law states that tenants are entitled to receive a copy of the rental contract (lease agreement), which should outline all terms including rent, duration, and obligations.\nUnder the Housing Regulation Act, there are limits to how much rent landlords can charge as well as how much they are allowed to increase it by. Rent is usually based on the value of the property.\nIn 2022, when interest rates in Denmark soared to over 10 percent, the government introduced a temporary rule capping rent increases at 4 percent, whereas it can normally rise in line with inflation and wage increases.\nLandlords must provide at least three months' notice of rent increases, which must comply with rent control laws. Tenants can dispute rent increases through rent tribunals (more detail on this below).\nDeposits and returns\nLandlords in Denmark generally demand three months’ rent in deposit, three months’ rent in advance and rent for the current month when you move into the property. This eyewateringly-high advance payment can certainly feel unfair, but it is legal.\nThe deposit must be refunded by the landlord upon termination of the lease, minus any legitimate deductions for damages. Conflicts can arise around whether deductions applied by the landlord are reasonable.\nTenants should not be expected to pay for fair wear and tear (like minor scuffs on floors or carpet wear due to normal usage), but if something has been damaged as a result of misligholdelse or poor upkeep during the tenancy, it may be deducted from the deposit.\nYou must leave the property in a clean condition when you move out. The landlord is entitled to deduct additional cleaning costs.\nLandlords cannot deduct for damage that was present before the tenant moved in. This is why documenting the property's condition at the start of the lease is crucial.\nMany landlords, including housing associations, carry out indflyttersyn or moving-in inspections which document existing damage and which tenants agree to. Alternatively, it’s a good idea to photograph the apartment in detail when you move in.\nMinor repairs or maintenance may be the tenant’s responsibility if this is specified in the rental agreement.\nLandlords must return any unused portion of the deposit promptly after deductions, typically within 1-2 months of the lease ending. Failure to do so can result in the tenant filing a claim against the landlord.\nNotice periods and eviction protections\nTenants can typically terminate the lease with three months’ notice unless otherwise stated in the rental contract.\nLandlords can terminate a lease only under specific conditions, such as the landlord or their family needs to use the property, and must provide extended notice periods depending on the duration of the tenancy.\nHowever, landlords are allowed to specify a duration of the rental agreement at its outset. For example, you may be offered a one-year lease on a property, in which the date on which the lease expires is stated in the contract.\nTenants can only be evicted for valid reasons, such as non-payment of rent, severe breach of contract, or illegal activities. Eviction requires a court order, and tenants can challenge the eviction in housing tribunals.\nHow do housing tribunals work?\nIf there is a disagreement about deposit deductions, tenants can file a complaint with the local housing tribunal (Huslejenævn), which handles disputes related to deposits and damages.\nLandlords must provide detailed documentation such as receipts, invoices, and photos to justify the deductions.\nThe tribunal board is not limited to cases related to deposits. You can also file a complaint if, for example, you believe the rent or an increase to it breaches regulations by being too high. You can also appeal if you feel the landlord is failing to fulfil their obligations under the law and your rental contract, for example by failing to make necessary repairs.\nAs of 2025, it will be possible to file a complaint on the national Huslejenævn website. Up to now tenants have had to contact the board in the municipality in which they live.\nThere are also anti-discrimination rules in Denmark’s tenancy laws. Under the law, landlords cannot refuse to rent based on ethnicity, religion, sexual orientation, gender, or other protected characteristics.\nIn addition to the housing tribunal (Huslejenævn) system, renters can seek assistance from tenant associations such as Lejernes Landsorganisation (LLO) if they believe their rights are being violated.\nIn some cases, the housing tribunal will decide that it is not within its remit to rule on the dispute, and will refer to or recommend the case be brought to the Boligretten or the Housing Court. This is likely is material evidence or witness testimonies are needed, or in major disputes relating to contract terminations.\n| Protection | Lejeloven | Boligreguleringsloven | \n|---|---|---|\n| Rental agreements | ✔️ |  | \n| Rent control |  | ✔️ | \n| Rent increases |  | ✔️ | \n| Deposits and advance rent | ✔️ |  | \n| Maintenance and repairs | ✔️ |  | \n| Notice periods | ✔️ |  | \n| Eviction protections | ✔️ |  | \n| Subletting | ✔️ |  | \n| Anti-discrimination protections | ✔️ (plus civil laws) |  | \n| Housing tribunals |  | ✔️ | \nSources: borger.dk, Huslejenævn.dk, Bolius, Retsinformation",
