        "title": "Today in Denmark: A roundup of the latest news on Friday",
        "link": "https://www.thelocal.dk/20260821/today-in-denmark-a-roundup-of-the-latest-news-on-friday-58",
        "source": "The Local Denmark",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473672+00:00",
        "image": "https://assets.thelocal.com/cdn-cgi/rs:fit:1200/quality:75/plain/https://apiwp.thelocal.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/watermarks-logo-20240818-182005-L-1920x1280web.jpg@webp",
        "full_content": "Pending citizenship applications cause rift in government\nMore than 2,000 people are still waiting for their Danish citizenship applications to be approved after processing of cases was put on pause before the Danish election in March.\nDespite the fact that Denmark has a new government (after record-long negotiations), these 2,055 applicants' cases are still in processing limbo.\nNow, the Moderates, Social Liberals and the Green Left, three of the governing parties, are putting pressure on the fourth governing party, the Social Democrats, to conclude outstanding cases.\n\"I think this is unreasonable. They've met the requirements which have previously been part of the citizenship agreement and for that reason I don't think it's worthy for a democracy and a state where the rule-of-law is applied that these people are being left in a huge vacuum,\" the Social Liberals' citizenship spokesperson, Magnus Georg Jensen, told public broadcaster DR.\nCitizenship applications are approved by the Danish parliament, meaning that each applicant's name has to be included on a citizenship bill passed in parliament before they become citizens.\nApplications were put on pause before the election in part due to discussions as to whether interviews should be put in place to screen prospective citizens to see if they hold \"Danish and democratic\" views.\nThe Social Democrats' citizenship spokesperson, Katrine Evelyn Jensen, has said she wants to investigate the possibilities for introducing a screening system before processing of applications can be resumed. She also rejected the other parties' claims that the situation is unreasonable.\n\"I think it's fair that we are looking into whether we can introduce this screening model,\" she told DR.\nDanish vocabulary: statsborgerskab – citizenship\nCopenhagen Ironman sells out in record time\nMore than 3,000 people bought bibs for the 2027 edition of the Ironman sports event, set to take place in Copenhagen on August 22nd.\nAccording to Ironman Copenhagen, bibs sold out in an hour, which is a new record. Last year, it took 46 hours for them to sell out and in 2025 it took 92 days.\nThe organisers said that they were surprised by the increased demand.\n\"The event in 2026 sold out in less than two days but we were not expecting 2027 to sell out so quickly,\" Jacob Nørgaard, country head for Ironman Denmark told the Ritzau news wire.\nIronman consists of 3.8 kilometres of swimming, 180 kilometres of cycling followed by a marathon – a 42.2 kilometre run.\n\"Signing up for an Ironman is a huge decision and a massive commitment, so the record interest only makes it more significant,\" Nørgaard said.\nTickets for a half Ironman in Helsingør, which involves 1.9 kilometres of running, 90 kilometres of cycling and a 21.1 kilometres of running, also sold out. That is set to be held on June 27th, 2027.\nDanish vocabulary: en enorm beslutning – a huge decision\nDanish government refused to assist German police with interrogations in Nord Stream case\nAccording to an indictment, Danish authorities refused to help Germany regarding the sabotage of the Nord Stream gas pipelines two years ago due to the potential threat it posed to the Danish state.\nSpecifically, Danish authorities refused to question harbour masters and recreational sailors on behalf of Germany. This is according to a German indictment, obtained by public broadcaster DR, dated June 26th.\n\"Danish authorities refused to conduct investigations on Bornholm and Christiansø due to a potential threat to the well-being of the state,\" German prosecutors wrote in an overview of the case, according to DR.\nAccording to the indictment, this occurred on February 14th, 2024.\nIn September 2022, explosions were recorded on the Nord Stream 1 and 2 gas pipelines which run through the Baltic Sea between Russia and Germany. The first was recorded about 20 kilometres south of Dueodde on the Danish island of Bornholm.\nDanish vocabulary: trussel mod statens ve og vel – a threat to the state's well-being\nSun and rain in store for this weekend\nThe weather this weekend looks similar to what we've seen so far this week, with sun and rain both forecast.\n\"Far from everyone will get rain showers but you could definitely catch one,\" Anja Bodholdt, on-duty meteorologist at Danish weather agency DMI, told the Ritzau news wire.\nOn Friday, it looks like it will be dry for most of the country in the afternoon and evening, with temperatures of between 19 and 20 degrees. Despite this, Bodholdt recommends taking an umbrella with you just in case.\nOn Saturday and Sunday, it might be slightly wetter, although there will be sunshine at times and temperatures just below 20 degrees.\n\"Unlike on Friday, on Saturday and Sunday you might need a thin jacket over your clothes. And on Sunday you should probably swap that jacket for a raincoat, especially in the east of the country,\" Bodholdt said.\nAs far as next week goes, she predicts that conditions will improve and we'll see something closer to \"late summer\" weather.\nDanish vocabulary: et paraply – an umbrella\nWith reporting from Ritzau",
