        "full_content": "By continuing to browse our site you agree to our use of cookies, revised Privacy Policy and Terms of Use. You can change your cookie settings through your browser.\nAerial view of the Xiling Gorge section of the Yangtze River in Yiling District, Yichang, central China's Hubei Province, May 20, 2026. /VCG\nAerial view of the Xiling Gorge section of the Yangtze River in Yiling District, Yichang, central China's Hubei Province, May 20, 2026. /VCG\nChina took a major step towards green modernization Saturday as its landmark Ecological and Environmental Code came into force, strengthening the legal foundation for harmonious coexistence between humanity and nature.\nAdopted by China's top legislature in March, it is the country's second formal statutory code after the Civil Code.\nThe code comprises 1,242 articles in five chapters, covering pollution control, ecological protection and green, low-carbon development, among other areas.\nIt was formulated through systematic integration, compilation, revision and refinement of over 30 ecological and environmental laws, over 100 administrative regulations and more than 1,000 local laws and regulations.\nChina also celebrated its fourth National Ecology Day Saturday under the theme \"Building a green foundation for Chinese modernization.\"",
