        "title": "China's fishing boats return to sea as summer moratorium ends",
        "link": "https://news.cgtn.com/news/2026-08-17/China-s-fishing-boats-return-to-sea-as-summer-moratorium-ends-1PG6iwhzGg0/p.html?UTM_Source=cgtn&UTM_Medium=rss&UTM_Campaign=China",
        "source": "China",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473590+00:00",
        "image": "https://news.cgtn.com/news/2026-08-17/China-s-fishing-boats-return-to-sea-as-summer-moratorium-ends-1PG6iwhzGg0/img/0e397e9c00da426e8a3bac5307b5286b/0e397e9c00da426e8a3bac5307b5286b-750.jpeg",
        "full_content": "By continuing to browse our site you agree to our use of cookies, revised Privacy Policy and Terms of Use. You can change your cookie settings through your browser.\nFishing ports along China's eastern and southern coasts came alive on Sunday as tens of thousands of fishing vessels set sail for a new fishing season following the end of the South China Sea's three-and-a-half-month summer fishing moratorium. Specially licensed boats have also resumed operations in parts of the East China Sea.\nIn Yangjiang City, south China's Guangdong Province, more than 3,400 fishing vessels departed after the official lifting of the fishing ban, heading toward fishing grounds in the South China Sea. As one of Guangdong's major marine fishing hubs and a key supply base for aquatic products, Yangjiang has for years ranked among the province's leading spots for marine-captured production.\nAhead of the reopening, local maritime, fisheries and transport authorities cleared navigation channels, inspected navigation markers and introduced temporary traffic controls in key waters to give priority to fishing vessels.\nFishing boats depart ports in Yangjiang City, south China's Guangdong Province, August 16, 2026. /VCG\nFishing boats depart ports in Yangjiang City, south China's Guangdong Province, August 16, 2026. /VCG\nDuring the first week of the fishing season, more than 2,000 Yangjiang fishing boats are expected to return to port with an estimated 30,000 tonnes of seafood. Local authorities said cold-chain logistics will transport the seafood nationwide, while expanded deep-sea aquaculture and seafood processing industries will help extend the marine industry chain and increase fishermen's incomes.\nMore than 1,100 fishing boats left the Qisha fishing port in Fangchenggang City, south China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, on Sunday, heading to the Beibu Gulf. Maritime authorities launched a special escort operation, deploying patrol vessels and implementing temporary traffic controls to ensure safe and orderly departures.\nFishermen unload their fresh catch from the first returning fishing boats at a fishing port in Qionghai City, south China's Hainan Province, August 17, 2026. /VCG\nFishermen unload their fresh catch from the first returning fishing boats at a fishing port in Qionghai City, south China's Hainan Province, August 17, 2026. /VCG\nSouth China's Hainan Province saw more than 29,000 fishing vessels gradually depart from various ports. In Sansha City, additional fishing support vessels will assist boats operating in deep-sea fishing areas of the South China Sea.\nThis year's fishing season has also featured cultural activities celebrating China's centuries-old fishing traditions, including intangible cultural heritage performances in Hainan and Guangxi, which not only preserve maritime culture, but also promote integration between fisheries and tourism.\nChina's summer fishing moratorium is a key conservation measure implemented in order to restore stocks, protect ecosystems and increase fishermen's incomes. The annual ban began on May 1, covering the Bohai Sea, the Yellow Sea, the East China Sea and the waters north of 12 degrees north latitude in the South China Sea, including the Beibu Gulf.\nDuring this year's moratorium, about 120,000 marine fishing and support vessels returned to port. Authorities, including the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, the Ministry of Public Security and the China Coast Guard, carried out strict enforcement against illegal fishing activities, safeguarding the order of the fishing moratorium.",
        "content": "By continuing to browse our site you agree to our use of cookies, revised Privacy Policy and Terms of Use. You can change your cookie settings through your browser.\nFishing ports along China's eastern and southern coasts came alive on Sunday as tens of thousands of fishing vessels set sail for a new fishing season following the end of the South China Sea's three-and-a-half-month summer fishing mor...",
