    {
        "title": "Top British scientist steps down from WHO",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/global-health/science-and-disease/top-uk-scientist-steps-down-from-who/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473300+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/global-health/2026/08/18/TELEMMGLPICT000247643036_17870360503410_trans_NvBQzQNjv4Bq-s-7wrOmwzHH88MNjIzm6xxuncOdM3ovusrCW3L9KzA.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "One of Britain’s best-known scientists has resigned as assistant director-general of the World Health Organization.\nSir Jeremy Farrar became a household name during the pandemic when he served on the UK’s Scientific Advisory Group for Emergencies (Sage).\nSources inside the WHO and the broader scientific community told The Telegraph he resigned his position on Monday.\nWhile it is not clear what prompted his resignation, colleagues told The Telegraph he had been unhappy within the organisation for some time. One speculated that he had become increasingly frustrated with its internal politics and bureaucracy.\nSir Jeremy, 64, who was also a former director of the Wellcome Trust, could not immediately be reached for comment.\nThe surprise move comes at a difficult time for the WHO, which is undergoing a major restructuring after suffering cuts and a massive funding shortfall following the United States’s withdrawal from it in January. It is also fighting a major outbreak of Ebola in the Democratic Republic of Congo.\nDr Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, the WHO’s Director-General, announced Dr Farrar’s decision in an email to all staff on Tuesday morning, saying that the British scientist will “retire” from the WHO, effective September 30.\nIn the email, seen by The Telegraph, Dr Tedros said: “Dr Farrar has demonstrated an exceptional commitment to science and public health at the highest levels, and we are grateful that he chose to bring his experience, energy and conviction to WHO.\n“On behalf of the entire organisation, I extend my sincere thanks to Dr Farrar... [and wish] him every success in his next chapter and a well-deserved retirement.”\nHe added that Dr Bruce Aylward will take over as the new Assistant Director-General for Health Promotion, Disease Prevention and Care.",
