        "title": "Robinson demolishes Pakistan to cement importance to new regime",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/cricket/2026/08/19/england-vs-pakistan-live-score-latest-first-test-day-1/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473279+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/cricket/2026/08/19/TELEMMGLPICT000498866991_17871669316910_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqKyHt9LyDGHZG95j7JY85mOil7a1KV2STY3xRqqFt_No.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Live",
        "full_content": "Ollie Robinson (right) and Josh Tongue shared the wickets, taking five each on the opening day of the first Test against Pakistan\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nOllie Robinson and Joe Root are two men with reputations to rebuild and they could not have wished for a better start than an old-school day of Headingley Test cricket.\nRobinson spent nearly three years exiled in the county game, his face not fitting in with the Bazball vibes, while Root’s standing as an England captain sank with every Ben Stokes funky field.\nBoth have outlived the previous regime and if Root is to make a success of his second crack at captaincy then squeezing every drop out of Robinson in home conditions will be imperative.\nThis was not a bad first stab with Robinson claiming five wickets and Root unbeaten at the end of a long, rain-affected day that stretched into a sunny Leeds evening.\nRobinson’s five for 51 included becoming the first man to take a wicket with the first ball of a Test series in England, and his standards did not drop throughout 18 overs of high-quality seam bowling that outwitted Pakistan’s batsmen.\nRoot did not have to think too hard after winning the toss under overcast skies at a ground where he grew up. He just went through the card, relying on two seamers to demolish Pakistan, although he did show a decisiveness when he whipped Jofra Archer off after only two overs with the new ball. Josh Tongue backed up Robinson with a pacy performance, smoothly transitioning from the Hundred final three days ago to finish with five for 46, cleaning up quivering tail-enders as Pakistan were bowled out for 171 in 49 overs.\nIt was a day for a diesel Volvo Estate rather than a Porsche as Mohammad Abbas also bowled at a pace that would only just earn him three points on his licence as he bamboozled Ben Duckett leg before as England’s opening stand lasted fewer than nine overs.\nMohammad Ali had Emilio Gay flashing to slip for 33 before Root ushered England to the close at 112 for two, partnered by a solid-looking No 3 in Jordan Cox. Whereas England had more gears with the ball, Pakistan’s attack appeared pop gun.\nFor all the empathy and light-heartedness of the Brendon McCullum-Stokes era, there was a ruthlessness and Robinson felt the full force of their rejection for nearly three years.\nEven after a stunning seven-wicket comeback at Lord’s at the start of June he was not trusted in the heatwave of the Trent Bridge Test against New Zealand only for bowlers of his ilk to prosper.\nYou can understand the reluctance. Robinson let England down, none more so than in his last Headingley Test three years ago when he limped out of the Ashes at 2-0 down with a back spasm.\nRobinson proved why England were right to give him another chance with a special five-wicket performance\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nEngland also became fixated on pace. Robinson was not quick or fit enough for five-day cricket, the theory went. It was only after the Ashes disaster that they reluctantly pivoted back to Robinson in June, and even then they insisted he had changed and was bowling quicker at about 83-84mph.\nThis time he averaged 77mph – and was not much quicker at Lord’s. Robinson averages 18 when he bowls below 80mph and with his performance here, it again brought to mind Rob Key’s throwaway comment that bowling “75mph keeper up, dobbing it on a length doesn’t work in Test cricket”. Even on a good day, Key takes a kicking.\nHis point was county-cricket trundlers do not cut it at Test level, but Robinson has exceptional skills combined with a command of accuracy, potent combinations in these conditions. He hit the exact fuller length for Headingley and nibbled the ball both ways, bowling wobble seam deliveries that left batsmen groping.\nHe also has that edge, the grunt of an angry fast bowler, and his competitiveness was just what Root needed considering his other quicks were still in Hundred mode. Archer bowled too short, Gus Atkinson grew into the innings but was wicketless and it took a while for Tongue to click, although once he did he was unplayable against the right-handers with his pace and angle.\nRobinson’s lack of pace gives them the keeper-standing-up option. It worked a treat when he bowled Pakistan’s top-scorer, Abdullah Shafique for 61, his footwork messed up by Jamie Smith breathing down his neck.\nPakistan would have batted first had they won the toss and it is a good pitch that will improve as the Test goes on, although with rain around all week the overheads will have a say on the game. Pakistan have far more recent red-ball exposure than England’s batsmen but they were ill-equipped to deal with Robinson and Tongue.\nMost supporters were still walking into the ground when the former struck first ball, Azan Awais lbw to a nip-backer. Shan Masood’s stilted footwork was ripe for exposing and Robinson’s first ball over the wicket to the left-hander was full and straight to beat the loose drive.\nImam-ul-Haq had done the hard work, seeing off Robinson only to flash a drive to second slip off a wide ball from Tongue to end Pakistan’s only stand of substance of the innings: 93 for the third wicket.\nRobinson cracked the innings open further with Saud Shakeel leg before, who was too slow to call for the review that would have saved him. Hawk-Eye showed the ball would have gone over the top.\nRobinson then bowled Shafique in his next over and Pakistan were toppling. Tongue cranked up the pace, bowled full and the perpendicular angle into the right-handers did the rest.\nMuhammad Rizwan was leg before to a ball tailing in and, first ball, Ali Usman was lbw to a yorker. Tongue was on a hat-trick over tea and for the delivery Root stationed two short-legs and four slips. Tongue served up a wide half-volley that was smacked for four by Khurram Shahzad, who slogged 14 before becoming Robinson’s fifth wicket, dollying a catch to mid-on where he was caught by Atkinson, who had dropped a similar chance two balls earlier.\nTongue finished it off with a fast yorker as Abbas backed away. The two seamers held the innings ball aloft to the crowd as they led England off, with Root right behind them slapping both on the back. He will not have many easier days than this.\nA very, very good day for England, not least because of the 50-odd Root shared with the rookie Cox at the end there. Important partnership. The weather is iffy tomorrow but England have a huge opportunity to put this game to bed.\nRoot defends, leaves and gets up the other end off the fifth ball of Mohammad Ali’s seventh over. Cox shoulders arms to it and England walk off 59 runs behind with eight wickets in hand after great contributions from Ollie Robinson, Josh Tongue and a very promising partnership between Jordan Cox and Joe Root, the returning captain.\nRoot’s done his thing again, of stealth accumulating. You look up and he’s motored along to 20 or so more runs than you though he had. He adds seven in this over, cutting the spinner for four and then drives the slow left-armer for four through mid-off. His former Yorkshire captain Shan Masood saves the boundary.\nNelson! One over to come.\nVery early days of course, as it is his second Test, but Jordan Cox has the air – the presence -– of a Test batsman. All sorts of caveats of course. What’s he like when the ball is around his ears, what’s he like facing spinners in Asia? But there is, on the evidence so far, plenty on which to build. I’d go so far as to say that Cox is more likely to be a long-term Test middle-order batsman, perhaps at five or six (or even as a wicketkeeper-batsman) than Emilio Gay is to become a long-term Test opener.\nRoot brings up England’s hundred with a false shot off Ali as he seemed to be blinded by the glare of the setting sun off the sightscreen. It was full, about fifth-stump line and Root opened the face late to nick it through gully for four. He leaves a couple on that line after that then plays and misses at the final ball, a near miss that has Salman Aghan rubbing his beard in exasperation.\nSpin to come. Usman Ali with four minutes’ play left.\nKhurram’s radar has been wonky. He looks useful if he can find a consistent line but he’s spraying it around and gives Root a freebie on his pads which the England captain tickles for four. Why isn’t Naseem Shah here? I know we’ve entered the Darren Stevens phase of Test cricket but genuine pace and an action as sublime as his always has a place in a team, particularly one that is struggling.\nMohammad Ali switches to the Football Stand End and immediately looks better, finding Root’s edge with a bit of extra pace. The ball flies past gully and they run two. Long old stint this. Three hours of play between lunch and tea and two-and-a-half between tea and close.\nBetter from Khurram now he’s loose and Root defends three, leaves two then takes the strike off the last ball with the shot that will be forever associated with him, the dab/ back-cut through third man for a single.\nFair play to Mohammad Abbas, saddling up for his 10th over in a row. The ground is about half-empty now, and it gets quite dark when the sun slips behind clouds. At the other end Pakistan have two bowlers who, really, are inferior versions of Abbas.\nEven if he can’t run without a ball in his hand, Abbas is still capable of delivering a peach. Early in the over Cox had played a handsome back-foot drive through mid-off for four but is then gulled by the old magician’s nip as the ball hits the pitch on middle then jags past his edge and thumps into the keeper’s gloves.\nSalman, the captain, slaps himself on the forehead when Khurram starts his second spell with a long hop outside off and Root cuts it with his bat halfway between the vertical and horizontal to send it behind point for four. He’ll injure himself if that’s his usual response to sloppiness because the next ball is fired on to Root’s pads and although he misses out with his flick, he adds four leg-byes off his thigh pad. Abbas, at long leg, almost waves it to the boundary as he tiptoes half-heartedly after it. The next ball is again sprayed on to Root’s pads and this time he times the pants off a glance. Again Abbas gives up the ghost and lets it go for four. He looks like an arthritic pensioner. Nine overs off the reel have taken their toll. No, hang on! He’s going to have a 10th.\nA ninth over in this spell for Abbas. Cox reads the one that nips away, waits for the movement and pushes it through cover off the back foot for two. He doesn’t pick the next one though, which is fuller, and whistles past the edge as he pokes forward.\nAli seems to be coming to terms with running down the hill from the KLE. In his first few overs he was slowing down, wary of overstriding, but he now is hitting the crease at full pelt. Nonetheless, after five decent deliveries to Root, testing his defencewith nip, bounce and accuracy, he hangs a long hop outside off and Root drives it off the back foot for four, the ball blazing a trail to the fence at extra cover.\nCox keeps out Abbas but only just when the bowler goes wider on the crease to arrow one into Cox who is squared up a second time as the ball pitches and whistles past the edge as it sears away.\nEmilio Gay can barely believe he has lost his wicket\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nMohammad Ali turns Cox into the dreaded S as he rides the bounce of one that s fired in and then jags away. He fends it away between third slip and gully for four. The next ball is short and doesn’t shapeshift so Cox tucks his napkin into his collar and gobbles it up, smacking it off the back foot through cover for four. Earlier in the over Root, at home, gets his feet under the table with his famous dab, opening the face to glide three through third man.\nGame on as Rizwan comes up to the stumps for Joe Root and makes a terrific take when Abbas shapes it on to off and middle then fizzes it away from Root’s defensive push. The next ball is the nip-backer and forces Root to adjust late and not very smoothly to chisel it out to point. A very probing maiden and Abbas has found his groove.\nA fair few fans are filing out, because it’s after 630pm, ur this is the best weather of the day. A gorgeous evening as the shadows lengthen. Rizwan will spend much of the evening session standing up, you would think.\nCox gets off the mark thanks to a generous gift from Abbas, a floaty wide one that he dispatches through cover point for four with a graceful swing of the bat. Two balls later he serves one up on Cox’s pads and the batsman uses his wrists to whip it through the onside for two more.\nNo rumble in the jungle for Mohammed Ali who bleeds in Leeds at the start of his first over. Too full at first he gives Gay a half-volley to squirt past point for four, goes wide again and invites a blistering cover drive then serves up another pie on Gay’s pads who clips him confidently for a third four.\nBut after all that he mines gold with a beauty, the perfect ball to exploit Gay’s weakness.\nMohammad Ali: floats like a butterfly, stings like a... butterfly.\nGay can’t believe he’s gone. It was the first ball from Ali that wasn’t a generous “hit me” delivery, and he’s edged to second slip. Game on.\nGay c Masood b Ali 33 Squared up after hitting three fours off Mohammad Ali’s first four balls and nicks it through to second slip. It’s been a feature of his short Test career so far. He looks vulnerable to balls from over the wicket that angle across towards off and middle and surprise him with some extra bounce. FOW 48/2\nAn artist at work. The previous ball jagged away from Duckett’s outside edge as he prodded forward, the next ragged the other way. Enter Jordan Cox at No 3, at least a place too high, and he too is beaten by one that nips away first up.\nDuckett lbw b Abbas 15 Yes. Another dreadful review from England, their only Achilles heel today. Would have hit middle stump about two-thirds if the way up after pitching outside off and zipping in. FOW 36/1\nDuckett got lucky in Abbas’s first over, but this always looked out. At the other end, Gay is looking as assured as he has at any point in his short Test career. He battled hard for fifties at Lord’s and the Oval, but this is pure batting in nice conditions.\nMaybe Gay should have fought a bit harder to get Duckett not to review that; a few words exchanged with the Pakistan fielders as the opener trudges off.\nKhurram recovers with five dot balls after opening with a short and wide one, back from round the wicket, to Gay who demolishes it, belting it through cover point for four. Tested by a couple of probing and hooping inswingers, Gay defends them solidly.\nDuckett’s timing has been a bit off\nCredit: DARREN STAPLES/AFP\nNice tempo for the start of England’s reply. Neither opener was determined to strike his first ball of this series before, like Zak Crawley did to launch the 2023 Ashes. Ben Duckett was looking to score but that is not the same as being hell-bent on boundaries. He and Emilio Gay got a neat start of 36 in nine overs before the first wicket fell.\nNo cloud cover for Abbas to work with and he is straining for the magic delivery, overpitching on a couple of occasions. Gay opens the face to dab three behind point and Duckett crouches back to a good length ball and slaps it through extra-cover for two.\nAround 3pm, with Robinson wheeling away from the Football Stand End, the Western Terrace got going and was pretty lively. Largely, though, it’s been becalmed down there. Certainly, as the clock drifts past six and there is still, in theory, 90 minutes to play, there is not much excitement, just polite applause as England’s score ticks up.\nKhurram comes over the wicket. In this bowler’s first over Duckett had gone for a square cut and didn’t middle it, toeing it tamely to cover and he finds the cue end again as he followed one angled across him and spooned it just past a man placed at catching, shortish mid-off. They run two and add two more with a midwicket flick.\nWhen Khurram drops too short, Duckett works two more with a twist of the wrist through midwicket.\nAbbas comes round the wicket to Gay who uses the angle to whisk a full one off his toes for four. The next ball is hung outside off, equally full, and Gay creams it through the covers for four in a shot that instantly evokes memories of Alastair Cook.\nLooks like being pretty sunny for the rest of today’s play ie there isn’t any cloud cover for Pakistan’s seamers to exploit. And as we all know Headingley is the ground where you look up not down to see what the ball will do.\nAwais throws down the stumps from short leg as Gay wandered out of his crease. The Durham opener scrambled back in time and then added insult to injury by filching an overthrow when it rebounded into the legside. Khurram trespasses on to the red zone in his followthrough from round the wicket and is given an unofficial ticking off. He should come over. Duckett defends a two with positive intent, looking but not finding a gap, then leaves one. Or as they used to do on Grandstand for freak occurrences: DUCKETT LEAVES ONE.\nAbbas is bowling over the wicket to the left-handers and beats Gay with one that nips away from the left-hander having pitched on off and middle leaving Gay groping after it. But when he adopts a wider line not by design, Gay cashes in with a thumping square drive for four.\nKhurram is a skiddy swing bowler, RFM you’d say, and starts round the wicket to Gay with shape. The commentators, Messrs Hussain and Karthik, suggest he should bowl over the wicket at the two left-handers to exploit his inswing and bring leg-before into play. Gay gets off the mark with a tight single pushed into the offside and Duckett adds a third two with an uppish flick, just out of short leg’s reach.\nRizwan questions why the umpire kept his finger holstered\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nPakistan will rail against that call from umpire Paleker who gave Rizwan out on two umpire’s calls and didn’t raise the finger for Duckett who was pinned on the back thighpad. Duckett had got off the mark by steering a two through point then, after being diddled by the nip-backer, plays and misses outside off. Pakistan are ticking. Duckett uses his feet to work two to square leg.\nAbbas b Tongue 2 Perfect ball for a rabbit, fast, straight and full, angling in. Tongue is no Elmer Fudd. He always bags his bunnies. Knocks back off stump and now Tongue has his five-for. Who keeps the ball? FOW 171 all out.\nKhurram is going throw the bat, has a big, gyroscope whoosh at a short one and misses then carts the next through midwicket for four. Robinson, who has four wickets, is sanguine about missing out on his fifth when the batsman plinks one towards mid-on and Atkinson tips it over the bar. Two balls later he makes amends.\nKhurram c Atkinson b Robinson 14 Bags his fifth wicket two balls after watching Atkinson drop him at mid-on. Another swish, another cloth and this time Atkinson gets under the high ball to take it. FOW 171/9\nVery odd dismissal that in the sense that Rizwan was off the ground as he flapped at the late movement. He was beaten, the shot was hideous but even a trajectory eliciting two umpire’s calls out of three would rarely prompt the raising of the finger.\nTongue welcomes Abbas with a blistering sandshoe crusher that he hops over and gratefully watches as it screeches past Smith’s dive for four byes. That would have hobbled him had it hit him and quite possibly deflected it into the stumps.\nRizwan doesn’t mind exposing the No 9 bat to Robinson and takes a single off the first ball with a nurdley push into the offside. Robinson almost immediately finds the edge of Khurram’s bat but it scuttles away low through the slips for four. A big waft outside off interests Duckett who says he heard a noise and there was a sound on the TV broadcast but England do not review and there has been no replay with Snicko so far. Maybe it was the old creaky handle.\nWith the way he bowls, fast, aggressive and targeting the stumps, feels like Josh Tongue might have a Test hat-trick in him, even if it’s not today.\nKhurram greets the hat-trick ball with a sparkling cover drive for four as Tongue went for the yorker but served up a half-volley. Tongue polishes off the over with a bouncer and Khurram drops to his haunches to evade it.\nRobinson will resume after his two-over breather before tea.\nTongue’s hat-trick ball: four slips, two leg slips, and two short legs. Whacked through the covers for four!\nJosh Tongue goes into tea on a hat-trick and England on top in the first Test against Pakistan. Led by the superb Ollie Robinson, England have made the most of good bowling conditions to give Joe Root’s captaincy the perfect start. Nobody is missing Ben Stokes just yet with Robinson taking four for 39 and Tongue, three for 41, after Root won the toss and his bowlers did the rest. Robinson’s second spell of two for 17 sparked a collapse of four for 33 as England took control of the match. Root has been understated in the field, no funkiness with the tactics. It has simply been about line and length on an old fashioned day of Headingley Test cricket.\nTongue starts with a short ball on leg stump that Rizwan collars on the pull through fine leg for four. As Stuart Broad says, he played in the Hundred final on Sunday evening and last bowled in a red-ball match on June 28, so it will take time to find his rhythm. He can still find the magic ball among some dross, though, and shatters off stump with a rapid nip-backer that lured Agha on to the drive then gated him.\nIn walks Ali Usman who lasts one delivery and burns a review after being trapped by the inswinging quasi-yorker.\nThere will be two balls left of the 45th over to complete after tea. Why take a break when Tongue is on a hat-trick. Again, cricket marches to the beat of its own drum. Ridiculous, though, these immovable feasts.\nUsman lbw b Tongue 0 First ball. The second golden duck of the innings. He’s going to review but it looks plumb. Fired in the inswinger at 91mph and hit him on the ankle. Would have hit leg stump. Two in two. Did him for pace as well as movement. Tongue will be on a hat-trick straight after tea. FOW 150/7\nSalman Agha b Tongue 21 Superb from over the wicket, angling in and kept going off the pitch rather than jagging away, chipping the top off off stump. FOW 150/6\nTongue smashes Agha’s off stump\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nArcher keeps plugging away but a stat that shows only eight per cent of his deliveries would have hit the stumps tells you everything about his precision. Two singles.\nThere is a lot of scrutiny on Jamie Smith, especially regarding his keeping. But he’s done a very good job standing up to Ollie Robinson there. Good to see England using the strategy that has contributed to their downfall over the last year.\nAgha cashes in on Tongue losing his line, filleting point with a blistering cut stroke for four, then chucking everything but the kitchen sink at a drive and flashing it wide of third slip for another. The captain is on the counter-attack.\nDouble change. Archer replaces Robinson. The first ball is on a good length, straight and wallops Agha on the thighpad but the next is looser, a fast long hop really and Agha carves it through point for four. The thing about Archer is he seems to be a pretty luckless bowler. Some of his best deliveries do not seem to take wickets. Here he beats Agha by squaring him up with one that arrows in, pitches, hits the seam and sears away from the edge.\nA bit of chatter about the discrepancy between Josh Tongue’s numbers to right and left-handers, notwithstanding his dismissal of Imam-ul-Haq today. He averages 16 more to left-handers: the sort of discrepancy we’d normally see among spinners. Anyway, he will be happy that he has two right-handers to bowl to now as England hope to limit Pakistan to under 200.\nTongue returns for a third spell and Rizwan walks across to the offside to flip a single off his legs. Aghan defends a couple then opens the face to run a single down to third man.\nRobinson takes his fourth wicket\nCredit: Lee Smith/Action Images via Reuter\nOllie Robinson had taken only seven first-class wickets at 25 each at Headingley before this Test, but he had also played a bit for Yorkshire here in white-ball cricket in his youth. In any event he has maintained the correct full length, and has bowled straight, and wobbled it both ways. Difficult to think who might have bowled better here. Pat Cummins? Jasprit Bumrah? Scott Boland and the South Africans might have pitched a touch shorter. Oh yes, and Mohammad Abbas. He could be just as accurate and penetrative if tomorrow is cloudy.\nRobinson beats Salman Agha with two that nip away, the captain playing and missing. Robinson is bowling so well with the keeper up that he can smile when Agha chips a boundary through mid-on for four, a far from convincing stroke. Robinson’s control is extraordinary.\nAtkinson continues with his 12th over. Salman Agha, the caretaker captain, gets off the mark with a Harrow drive off the Surrey quick down to long leg, Rizwan flicks a single off his bootlaces and Salman elongates a defensive push wide of the bowler to farm the strike.\nPerfectly fine over but it feel likes filler in between the box-office bowling of Robinson.\nEnter Rizwan who keeps out another nip-backer that jagged in like an off-cutter and then tries to slog sweep the fuller one, finds the tope edge as he helicopters through the stroke and launches it high over the slips for two down to third man.\nWicket-to-wicket bowling with the keeper standing up: worthy of Nathan Smith or Matt Henry in New Zealand’s Test series victory over England. Now, England are in a fine position at Headingley.\nShafique b Robinson 61 Impressive innings ended by an excellent delivery. The previous delivery of this masterly interrogation fizzed past the edge, this one nipped back through the gate and knocked back middle and leg. A glorious sight for the bowler only trumped by the glorious sound of the death rattle. Unplayable. FOW 118/5\nAs so often, one of England’s most effective wicket-takers is the drinks break. But that was a spawny one. It was missing the stumps but Saud did not have the confidence to make the call himself and wsted time discussing it with Shafique.\nWowzer. Saud tried to review, but was too late. And the ball was going over. Robinson, down the hill, gets a little extra bounce. It always looked adjacent, but I didn’t realise quite how high it had hit him.\nSaud lbw b Robinson 5 He ran out of time to review even though Allahuddien Paleker ought to have run out of time to give him out. He took an age. Angling in from round the wicket it struck him in front but the only question was height. He asked Shafique whether his stride might have saved hm but by the time he made the sign the clock read zero. The replay shows it was going over leg stump. FOW 117/4\nAfter a spell of control, Atkinson serves up one of Shafique’s pads and he gobbles it up, whisking it through square leg for four. Time for the second drinks break of this 175 minute session.\nRobinson has purveyed more peaches and jaffas today than Trumpton’s Mr Clamp. (Ask your parentsgrandparents). His third maiden includes another from round the wicket to Saud. You can guess the trajectory, angling in and nibbling away, but reading it and playing it are entirely different things.\nSaud leaves the first three as Atkinson gets progressively closer to the stumps, the last of them so tight he needed to look around for reassurance. He plays at the next and again demonstrates the benefits of a soft grip, guiding it down in front of third slip. They pinch a single.\nAbdullah Shafique moves to fifty as he waits for a side one and squirts it past point with a thick outside-edge. Robinson is bowling beautifully, moving the ball in and away, beating him seemingly routinely. He pins Saud above the knee roll from round the wicket but England again decide not to gamble with their remaining review, wisely so as it pitched outside leg-stump before straightening.\nAtkinson replaces Tongue and immediately finds Saud’s edge. His soft hands guide it about a foot short of Duckett who makes a painful stop with his thumb. DK, a keeper, thinks Jamie Smith should have gone for it he left it for first slip. Two balls later Atkinson beats the left-hander with a clone of that delivery, angling in from round the wicket, jagging away off a good length, only this time it beats the edge.\nBig appeal from Robinson when he jags the last ball of the over in to Shafique and strikes him on the right thighpad.Too high. The ball before Shafique had tucked in when Robinson strayed down leg, flicking it fine for two.\nTongue makes the breakthrough\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nTongue must have been working on his line of attack for left-handers because no sooner had he sent Imam packing after his gutsy innings, he fires a 90mph inswinger into Saud’s shin. England ponder a review but having wasted two decide not to bother, mercifully so as there was an inside edge. Staying full, Tonge seeks the edge again but Saud opens the face and steers it past gully for four.\nA classic Josh Tongue wicket, reward for pitching the ball up and swinging the ball. But not a classic Josh Tongue victim: he averages 39 against left-handers in Tests, compared to 23.3 against right-handers.\nIman c Brook b Tongue 42 Having barely troubled the left-hander while torturing the right-hander with his unusual point of delivery and skill at moving the ball away, Tongue goes into the left-hander with a full one, invites the cover drive and shapes it away to take a thick edge. Brook takes a good, low catch to his left at second slip. FOW 97/3\nI have long felt that they will miss Stokes’ captaincy, Trent Bridge notwithstanding. But more than anything they will miss his bowling. He’d be on now.\nPointless pining for Bonnie Prince Benny, though, And here’s Robinson with Smith standing up. Imam squeezes a single through gully.\nThere we have it: Robinson bowling 76mph with Jamie Smith up to the stumps. It’s the tactic Rob Key famously said no longer works in Test cricket. Let’s see how England deal with it when Abbas is bowling with Rizwan up to the sticks.\nTongue replaces Archer from the Kirkstall Lane End. He took four for 86 and three for 72 in England’s victory over India there last year.\nShafique nails another elegant on-drive, drilling it between bowler and the fielder’s right hand for four. After Shafique pushes a sharp single to cover, Imam chips a legside flick just short of a diving Robinson at midwicket. The ball goes underneath the fielder and they sprint a single. They run even faster for the next run when Shafique drops one into the crease, loses sight of it but Imam calls him through with the cordon on their heels.\nRobinson starts with a loosener in terms of pace, at 72mph, but it still moves away off the seam and beats Imam. After three balls of the over, Imam calls on the physio after seemingly popping his calf. The medical staff apply some strapping.\nAfter treatment Imam resumes and plays out the last three balls, the final one with Smith up at the stumps. The ball crashes into his gloves as the left-hander leaves it.\nThere is less energy out there on the field than there was in the good old days when Ben Stokes was captain. Buzzing around at mid-off or similar, Stokes was of course the best outfielder in the England side by a street, setting the example. And he would be talking more to the bowler than Joe Root seems to be doing at mid-off.\nArcher gives Imam bounce and width at 90mph and he RSVPs by scything a square cut over point for four. If that delivery causes self-chastisement, the next has Archer cursing his luck as he beats the left-hander from round the wicket with one that straightens to snake past the edge.\nImam hares back for three after clipping one off his toes. Tongue chased it down, slid on his right knee and clawed it back from the rope. Hate to see a fast bowler land on one knee in a sliding stop. Feels too risky. Still, I suppose the rain may have smoothed his landing.\nRobinson is coming back on, this time up the hill.\nHad Babar Azam been fit, Imam-ul-Haq probably wouldn’t have played this Test match. But he’s looking very well-organised and compact, making himself hard to drop for Lord’s.\nTongue makes Shafique contort himself into a capital S with his classic delivery, angling in and jagging away from the outside edge and off stump by a gnat’s whisker. But far from being fazed by it, Shafique ends the over before drinks with a pair of fours when Tongue overpitches, the first tickled fine for four, the next harpooned through mid-on.\nJust the leg-bye off Jofra’s sixth over though there was a loose pull at a legside bouncer that could have resulted in a strangle but fortunately for Shafique the bball evaded the glove.\nMichael Atherton and Nasser Hussain are talking about Scyld’s piece ranking the Test grounds with Headingley in fifth.\nIts super-strength is the quality of the pitches, far removed from the uneven bounce that used to encourage seamers, whether steaming down the slope from the Kirkstall Lane End like Bob Willis or running up it from the Football Stand End. The Western Terrace is another asset, when it is not bored and beer-snaking: it is as patriotic as any ground in England, especially when a northerner such as Ben Stokes or Joe Root is putting opponents to the sword.\nWhat the ground lacks is a feeling of history: all the planning has been higgledy-piggledy over the decades, as the pavilion has oscillated around the boundary, and nothing of Yorkshire cricket’s unique past has been captured. It is also difficult to walk round the ground at an interval, so packed are the concourses. That said, it should have been awarded an Ashes Test next summer, whatever the merits of Headingley’s bid or even if there was none. Cricket’s governing body, the ECB, should have made sure of it.\nThey disagree. Atherton would have it top and Hussain agrees in a toss up with Edgbaston..\nTongue is so tricky to cope with because of the angle and his high pace, He is the only bowler who has made Shafique look two-bob rather than a million dollars, beating him with movement away off the pitch. He cops a blow on the thighpad, keeps another out with the inside edge then slices a check-drive along the ground to gully.\nBaz may ne gone but his coaching staff, Jeetan Patel, Tim Southee, Marcus Trescothick and Sarah Taylor (with Shoaib Bashir) are in charge for the interim\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nA quick run to cover invites Lawrence to swoop and shy underarm at the non-striker’s stumps, demolishing them. Shafique had made it home for the only run of the over but that’s encouraging given how inaccurate England’s outfielding has been recently.\nA couple of singles, both skelped off the pads, sandwich a jaffa from Tongue to Shafique with his extraordinary angle of delivery, from five past on the clock face rather than on the hour. It arrows in to middle, hits the seam and climbs past the edge and over middle stump.\nAs Tim points out, Joe Root had to contend with Anderson and Broad, two powerful personalities, when he was captain. We had Jimmy as a columnist for that Ashes series that Tim referenced. This was his response to the captain’s words: “We can’t just go after the game, we should have bowled fuller. If we are bowling too short at lunch we need information back saying we need to push our lengths up. The data is live. We can run messages out during the innings to say your length needs to go a touch fuller or whatever it might be.” Not sure Gus Atkinson is going to go public like that.\nFirst runs off Archer today and it’s a handsome stroke to break the drought, a classy on-drive from Shafique for four that brings up the fifty partnership.\nShafique plays tip and run for a single to cover and Archer comes round the wicket to Imam and beats him with a beauty that angles in to off and middle then jags away to whistle past the edge. He likes that and the length so goes full again twice more, too full both times, and Imam smears a sweetly timed cover drive for four then drives through mid-off with less power and precision for three runs, Robinson chasing and diving to save a run.\nImam-ul-Haq raises the right elbow to nail a drive\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nAtkinson goes shorter to the bespectacled Imam who leans back and crashes a square cut through backward point for four. It’s been bowlers who have worn glasses recently – Messrs Vettori and Leach – and few batsmen since the heyday of Zaheer and Clive Lloyd. Four eyes but one vision for Imam who clips two off his toes as Atkinson again errs on the straight side.\nArcher starts at 85mph and cranks it up thereon in his first dart down the hill from the Kirkstall Lane End. There’s swing, too, too much of it, ducking in past Shafique’s backside and forcing headlong dives from Smith to save byes. Shafique defends three of them, leaves the other three. The ones he is forced to play are the ones that push him on to the back foot. That’s a third maiden in three overs from Archer but none of them have much virtue other than the lack of runs given how many balls the batsmen have been able to let sail past.\nPitching the ball up at a line that is almost impossible to leave: Ollie Robinson is doing what Joe Root wanted James Anderson and Stuart Broad to do at the end of his first stint as captain. Remember what Root said about England’s new ball bowling after defeat in Adelaide in 2021?\n“With ball in hand, we didn’t bowl the right lengths,” Root said. “We needed to bowl fuller. As soon as we did in the second innings, we created chances. That’s frustrating. We did it four years ago and didn’t learn from it. We have to be better.”\nDinesh Karthik, as astute a pundit as he is a natty dresser, says England are diligently pitching it up but need to throw in the odd bouncer to keep the batsmen on their toes. Atkinson overpitches on middle and off and Imam takes a big stride to the pitch and strokes it through mid-on for four.\nPerhaps Root has heeded the call for some short stuff. Archer will replace Robinson.\nShafique plays Robinson off his legs twice, once with the middle and once with the inside edge but neither earn him any runs. In fact there’s only a single to add to the score, from another flick off middle and leg as Robinson continues to target the front knee-roll. Imam blocks the last two stoutly.\nImam has the soft hands required to steer one that spits up from round the wicket by Atkinson and guide it past gully for four. He defends two and leaves another three. Robinson has made the batsmen play at 93 per cent of his deliveries. Atkinson and Archer have not been as naggingly accurate.\nShafique climbs into a Robinson half-volley and creams it through cover for four. Lovely shot. He’s a class act even if the width of his stance would offend the purist.\nRobinson pushes one wider and Shafique opens his wrists to glide two runs down past gully. Root is jollying Robinson along from fourth slip. He’s been there, at mid-off and extra-cover so far today. Steve Smith may well edge ahead as the record outfield catcher in Test cricket if he carries on keeping himself away from first slip. They both currently have 218.\nAbdullah Shafique drives\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nWhile the length has been consistently probing, none of that Anderson and Broad 2008-22 habitual reluctance to pitch it up in their opening spells, the line has strayed and now Imam picks Atkinson up off his toes to whip it behind square for two. The next ball is shorter and jumps. Imam fends it away off bat and pad, spooning it wide of the leg gully.\nAtkinson ends the over with a wobble-seam beauty that straightens past Shafique’s edge.\nGiven the interruption and how dangerous Robinson has been with the new ball, no reason that he shouldn’t bowl 10 overs unchanged with the new ball. There have been many questions about Robinson in his Test career; his potency with the new ball has never been one.\nRobinson is a proper handful, moving the ball both ways to the left-hander. Imam manages to jam the bat down in time to keep out the nip-backer that was arrowing towards the top of middle but is beaten when crouching back by the one that tails away outside off. When Robinson goes fuller still from round the wicket and too straight, Imam picks him off with an elegant on-drive, pressed through mid-on for three.\nIn theory, we can now play until 7.30pm. Although I can’t see the light lasting beyond around 6.30 based on last night.\nAtkinson continues with the decent, fuller length you need at Headingley but a tad too wide and Shafique opens the face to squeeze two runs past point.\nRobinson will continue and, given his rest, could have another five-over spell up his sleeve.\nEngland players are out on the field and warming up\nLink copied to clipboard\nThere are still six minutes to go before the umpires shout ‘Play’ but no discernible reason why they cannot be playing now. There are two balls to go of Gus Atkinson’s third over with Abdullah Shafique facing. Shafique averages 44.62 from 25 Tests with six hundreds and six fifties but this is only his fifth Test outside Asia. He made 160* and 24* last time out against West Indies in Trinidad at the start of the month.\nThis session will be juts shy of three hours with tea scheduled for 4.40pm and a final session of 5pm-7pm with theextra half hour available if the light is good enough.\nThere’s a shower looming to the west on the radar for later in the afternoon but we should be OK to restart in 14 minutes.\nExhibit 387 of cricket not helping itself, and being far too lax about maximising the amount of play in a day. Everyone is a bit perplexed about why we aren’t playing at the moment.\nEnglish cricket marches to the beat of its own drum part 1,769\nLink copied to clipboard\nIf a pitch inspection for 1.30 had not been announced an hour ago, I reckon the umpires would have been out there now – and ordering a restart of play. None of the ground staff is doing anything except standing and waiting.\nRob Bagchi here, taking over for the last two sessions of day one. The groundstaff are working like Trojans to mop up but a resumption at 1.10pm still seems touch and go. There would have to be an inspection first.\nImran Khan, the former Pakistan prime minister and World Cup-winning cricket captain, will be moved from his “death cell” in prison to hospital amid concerns about his health.\nThe Supreme Court of Pakistan ordered on Tuesday that Khan, 73, should be moved to hospital, having been in jail for the last three years for a string of cases following his ousting as prime minister in 2022.\nHe will be moved to Shifa International Hospital in Islamabad within 48 hours, and will remain there until at least September 16, Naeem Haider Panjutha, Khan’s spokesperson, said on X that this would be the longest period Khan has spent outside jail since his imprisonment.\nSir Geoffrey Boycott: Time for England to grow up and change their attitude\nLink copied to clipboard\nA change in captain and coach by England is only cosmetic. What is really required is a change in attitude and culture by the players.\nSelf-discipline on and off the field is what is needed, not waiting for a captain or coach to tell them how to behave or how to bat and bowl. Grow up and take control of your own actions.\nHow you perform on the cricket field and how much alcohol you drink in your free time is up to you. When the great Don Bradman was captain of Australia, one day he told his team to be in bed by midnight. Just after midnight there was a knock on his door. Keith Miller stood there and said: “Captain I’ve been to bed and now I’m going out.”\nKeith lived life to the full but did not get into any nightclub incidents and he always performed next day. In fact, he is one of Australia’s greatest cricketers.\nIts super-strength is the quality of the pitches, far removed from the uneven bounce that used to encourage seamers, whether steaming down the slope from the Kirkstall Lane End like Bob Willis or running up it from the Football Stand End. The Western Terrace is another asset, when it is not bored and beer-snaking: it is as patriotic as any ground in England, especially when a northerner such as Ben Stokes or Joe Root is putting opponents to the sword.\nWhat the ground lacks is a feeling of history: all the planning has been higgledy-piggledy over the decades, as the pavilion has oscillated around the boundary, and nothing of Yorkshire cricket’s unique past has been captured. It is also difficult to walk round the ground at an interval, so packed are the concourses. That said, it should have been awarded an Ashes Test next summer, whatever the merits of Headingley’s bid or even if there was none. Cricket’s governing body, the ECB, should have made sure of it.\n“It felt like quite a nice day to bat about 40 minutes before play but as we got closer to the first ball the clouds started to roll in. All the numbers suggest that Headingley is a place to bowl first; the last seven Tests have been won by the team batting second. You still have to bowl well and [Ollie] Robinson did just that. Robinson’s accuracy and skill make him perfectly suited to Headingley.\n“If you are a bowler who finds movement off the pitch and in the air, Headingley is a good place to bowl, provided you pitch the ball full. His skill has never bene in doubt. What has been in question is his durability long-term and being able to do it time and time again. This summer is a case in point. He came back at Lord’s to great fanfare and took seven wickets, and that was the last time we saw him!”\nThere has been a heavy burst of rain, as expected, but it is looking brighter in the skies above Headingley. There are still some showers around but the heaviest of the rain may have passed.\nGood news: all five unused members of England’s Test squad will be available for their County Championship sides tomorrow. Ollie Pope and Matt Fisher for Surrey, Sam Cook for Essex, Brydon Carse for Durham and Shoaib Bashir for Derbyshire.\nThe rain is coming! I am surprised the lights are not on yet as it is getting gloomier. Those who are sat in the Western Terrace may not know what is behind them but should be prepared to get very wet! England need to try and bowl as many balls as possible before the rain strikes.\nAtkinson offers up some width and Shafique drives through the covers for four.\nEngland think they have Shafique but umpire Allahudien Paleker does not agree. Was there an inside edge on that? There was not and it may be going over. It strikes the back thigh pad and was going over so England have lost their second review inside the opening hour. A superb decision from Paleker.\nThe rain has now started to fall and the umpires are taking the players off. This is going to be a very heavy shower so we wait to see if we get any more play before lunch or if the interval is taken early.\nRobinson continues into his fifth over. He has changed angles a few times, both coming over and around the wicket, and has caused the Pakistan batsmen all sorts of problems.\nHe is not far off getting his third wicket of the morning as he finds Imam’s outside edge but the ball goes past Cox’s left hand at third slip and runs away for four.\nOllie Robinson, in his previous Test appearances in England, has been accustomed to bowl for the first hour, have a drink, then one more over after drinks - seven overs in all for his opening spell.\nThe rain is on its way\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nShafique gets one on his hips from Atkinson and flicks away through square leg for a couple. It is safe to say the weather in the distance looks horrific and that is exactly the direction which the weather is coming from. We are almost certain to get a heavy shower at some point in the next hour.\nAtkinson then bowls a few beauties that beat Shafique’s outside edge.\nThe slip cordon is packed and Robinson finds the outside edge of Imam’s bat but it drops just short of Gay in the gully region. The clouds that are rolling in are very nasty so England will now they have to capitalise on this period before the inevitable rain hits.\nA maiden over from Robinson, who has been magnificent this morning.\nThe sun is poking through, but there are some very ominous crowds hovering...\nWe have out first change of bowling as Gus Atkinson comes on to replace Archer. That is the shot of the day and it brings about the first boundary of the game. Shafique drives brilliantly past Tongue’s left hand at mid-on for four. Classy from Shafique.\nAbdullah Shafique joins Imam in the middle and gets off the mark with an inside edge down to fine leg. I have just heard one of the England fielders, potentially Duckett, telling his teammates to get around quickly, knowing a shower is coming.\nOllie Robinson’s brilliant new ball spell is also making his omission from the last two Tests against New Zealand seem more curious. The selection for the last Test, at Trent Bridge, was particularly difficult; Robinson was left out on account of not being quick enough.\nMasood LBW Robinson 5 Robinson was denied a LBW earlier in the over but not this time. Robinson now comes over the wicket having mostly bowled around the wicket thus far and that decision brings him a wicket. Masood misses a full, straight one and he is trapped plumb in front. Masood does not think long about a review and sensibly opts against it. FOW 6/2\nArcher sends down his second straight maiden to start his day. We wondered how Archer, Tongue and Atkinson would fare transitioning from the Hundred to Test cricket but so far Archer has been on it.\nAmazing stat from Andy Zaltzman - that is the first time ever that a wicket has fallen to the first ball of a series in England. It’s the 12th time overall, with the most recent being Rory Burns in the 2021/22 Ashes, of course...\nThis is a real test for these Pakistan left-handers as both Robinson and Archer have settled into a rhythm quickly. Like his first over, Robinson nearly bowls a maiden but Masood drops into the offside and takes a quick single from the final ball.\nJoe Root’s last Test win as captain was here at Headingley in August 2021. Their key bowler in that game? Ollie Robinson.\nJofra Archer will open the bowling alongside Robinson. Third ball he finds the outside edge of Imam’s bat from around the wicket but it drops short of Cox at third slip.\nIt is a probing maiden over to start with from Archer.\nAnother pair of Sussex pace bowlers, Maurice Tate and Arthur Gilligan, jointly dismissed South Africa for 30 back in 1924. Ollie Robinson and Jofra Archer have started well by pitching the ball very full. A pleasant change from the bouncer barrage which Joe Root unleashed in the Oval Test against NZ’s Glenn Phillips, catastrophically.\nShan Masood, who has plenty of experience on this ground and in this country, joins Imam in the middle. It is nearly a wicket maiden from Robinson but Masood gets Pakistan going with a clip through wide mid-on for a couple.\nThat was Robinson’s 500th first-class wicket.\nIt was the third ball of that first over from Robinson, rather than the wicket, that showed it was a great toss to win. Extravagant movement away from the left hander Masood off the pitch as if the ball had hit a crack (there aren’t any). It is damp below the surface, and this is going to be a tough morning to bat.\nAzan LBW Robinson 0 Ollie Robinson, who did not play in the Hundred like the other England seamers, will start proceedings from the Kirkstall Lane End. He starts around the wicket to Azan and first ball wraps him on the pads. There are loud appeals from England and umpire Chris Gaffaney’s finger goes up. Azan opts to review and the only thing that will save him is whether the impact was outside off stump. Three reds and Azan has to go. England take a wicket with the very first ball of the series. FOW 0/1\nAn incredible start to this Test match. Robinson pins Azan lbw first ball. They say you need to pitch the ball a metre fuller at Headingley, and he was instantly on the money. Robinson started with a triple-wicket maiden in his only Test against New Zealand...\nThe perfect start for England\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nJoe Root leads his men out to the middle, followed by the Pakistan opening pair of 21-year-old Azan Awais and Imam-ul-Haq. This will not be an easy task for the Pakistan openers in these conditions. The sun is shining for now but there are plenty of clouds surrounding Headingley.\nBoth sides emerge from the dressing room and it is time for the national anthems. Pāk Sarzamīn Shād Bād (Blessed Be the Sacred Land) followed by God Save The King. The sun is trying to peek through the clouds but it is significantly cooler than it has been of late.\n“The art of Test cricket is staying patient, particularly here at Headingley where you get rewarded for holding length; banging away in a good area and bringing slips into play.\n“What you do not want to do is go chasing; go fuller, go shorter, change pace, so the discussion in the bowling group will have been about keeping minds patient and getting in and out of overs.\n“They would hope to get 10 wickets in a day with this cloud cover but the workload is not what the bowlers are used to after a couple of months of white-ball cricket.”\nOne aspect of modern cricket that is always intriguing: the rapport between commentators and players who were formerly opponents. Shan Masood had a long chat with Stuart Broad just before the toss. I suspect Masood, as a left-hander, will be glad that he doesn’t have to contend with late career Broad bowling around the wicket.\n“It was last minute I find out I am captaining. Babar’s [Azam] absence is unfortunate. We have been playing a lot of red-ball cricket. We are well prepared.\n“Whenever you to go into any series it is an opportunity to win. We want to win a series. Today is the first day; we just have to have a good day.\n“We wanted to bat first. We have seven proper batsmen, three seamers, and one spinner. I can turn my arm over if needed.”\n“We are going to bowl first. There are some good overheads to work with throughout the day, a bit of top moisture, hopefully the lads can exploit that. Nerves are always there as a player; it is time to finish if they no longer linger, but there is certainly a huge amount of excitement. It is an exciting group of players and the fact we get to bowl here, those talented bowlers get to show what they can do. There will be a continuation of [Bazball] and hopefully an evolution of that over time, understanding certain ebbs and flows.”\nJoe Root back at the helm\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nLet’s sum up the McCullum Era of England’s Test cricket, which metamorphosed into the McCullum Error.\nThe quality which Brendon McCullum brought as England’s head coach was boldness or fearlessness: he gave the players a sense of security and confidence which they had never had before, scaling the heights of scoring 500 runs in one day in Pakistan.\nThen came the excess of this virtue. Opponents, taken aback at first, came to realise that England could play only one way - up and at ‘em. England’s one-size-fits-all approach to batting made them vulnerable, and soon brittle, in bowler-friendly conditions. There was no Plan B, until Ben Stokes decided to defend. England kept on being bowled out cheaply.\nThe fearlessness turned to gormlessness. Having won all but one of their first ten Tests, McCullum’s team won two of their last ten. Probably not since 1988, the season of four Test captains, have England been so shambolic as they were in the last Test at Trent Bridge against New Zealand, which culminated in that lemming rush. Time for change that was over-due after the last Ashes.\nWe will see a new cordon. Yesterday in training Ben Duckett was at first slip, Harry Brook second and Dan Lawrence third (although this morning Jordan Cox was at third in catching drills). It allows Joe Root to move to mid-off, where he can chat to his bowlers, which suggests a more hands on approach than in his first stint. Good toss to win, clouds will hang around all day.\nIt goes the way of Joe Root on his home ground and he chooses to bowl first. Root cites the overhead conditions today and the moisture on the top of the surface as reasons for his decision.\nThey say look up, not down at Headingley. Currently the sun is out but some showers could hit in the coming hours so it may not be the easiest of decisions at the toss. Maybe it might not be a bad toss to lose.\nWell, this was quite the moment. In the days building up to the Test, news came out that Pakistan captain Babar Azam had been ruled out of this game with a hand injury. However, just moments ago, Babar emerged in full whites with his blazer on and the thought was that Babar might actually be playing and going out for the toss. However, it was just for a photoshoot with the trophy alongside England skipper Joe Root. That would have been quite the turnaround for Babar!\nIs that Babar Azam about to toss after all? He’s lining up to pose for the series trophy, but now he’s walking off. So doesn’t look like he’s playing after all.\nBabar Azam out for a photo but is not playing today\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\n“This is another reset for England. We have had quite a few in the last 18 months. They have tried to reset by playing smarter cricket but not been able to do that, so now the captain has retired and the coach sacked. I hope it is just a reset as it would be wrong to to dismiss the previous regime as failure. You have to remember that [Ben] Stokes and [Brendon] McCullum gave us a couple of years of exhilarating cricket.\n“In the last 18 months, though, they have come across as an unserious team, with misdemeanours off the field. That has translated to results going in the wrong direction. I want the good stuff from the previous regime but a reset to understand that winning is important. I think the previous regime got carried away with brand and style of cricket. [Joe] Root is not bothered by that.”\nEngland interim coach Marcus Trescothick speaking to Sky Sports\nLink copied to clipboard\n“I have always said I would love an opportunity to step up and lead the group. There have been challenges but we are focused on the present now; this Test series against Pakistan. Let’s take the positives from what has happened and move on to the next phase.\n“We understand we have not been as consistently good as we want but now it is about trying to tweak that to make it better in the short-term before Stephen Fleming comes in.\n“I want to let Joe Root focus on batting and leading the team on the field.”\nLast Headingley Test for Joe Root? Has to be a chance of that. Headingley is not on the Test rota again until 2029, the India series, and he will be 38 by then. Next year it hosts a women’s Ashes Test but the men are not playing further north than Trent Bridge. The ECB passed over Headingley, a ground soaked in rich Ashes memories, for Southampton. It will be a big talking point next year.\nWill Macpherson: Dan Lawrence has become England’s unlikely replacement for Ben Stokes\nLink copied to clipboard\nReplacing the many dimensions of Ben Stokes in England’s Test team will be the job of more than one cricketer.\nYet when England enter the post-Stokes world at Headingley on Wednesday, the man tasked with taking his place in the XI is not a seam-bowling all-rounder offering talismanic leadership, but Dan Lawrence, a batsman who bowls some improving, unusual off-spin.\nLawrence has not been seen on the Test stage for almost two years, when an ill-fated stint stepping in for the injured Zak Crawley as an opener ended with three scores in the 30s and three in single figures against Sri Lanka.\nBen Stokes’ retirement has left a huge void in this England side\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nHeadingley has consistently produced terrific Test pitches that have led to excellent games in recent years. Think of the 2023 Ashes Test and last year’s Test against India, when England chased down 371 to win. Hoping for more of the same, and a full day’s play. A short shower is expected at lunch, but it’s meant to be brief.\nTim Wigmore: Pakistan have become cricket’s basket case\nLink copied to clipboard\nAt the Oval in August 2016, Pakistan ended their tour of England with a triumphant lap of honour. The 10-wicket victory earned a 2-2 series draw, and Misbah-ul-Haq led his team around the ground on their independence day. A week later, Pakistan were named the world’s No 1 Test side.\nFor Pakistan, such heights have never seemed farther away. They came bottom of the nine teams in the 2023-25 World Test Championship, and are eighth in the current table.\nBangladesh, born from the country that was East Pakistan, defeated Pakistan 2-0 in series in 2024 and this year, and clinched a seismic Test victory in Australia last week. White-ball cricket, far from being an escape from Pakistan’s other woes, has reinforced them: the team have not reached the knockout stages in the last four International Cricket Council (ICC) events.\nFormer Pakistan wicketkeeper Sarfaraz Ahmed is now their head coach\nCredit: Bradley Collyer/PA\nWe gave two cricketers from The Hundred a bat from 100 years ago – here’s what happened next\nLink copied to clipboard\nIn 1902, Gilbert Jessop scored the fastest-ever century by an English test match cricketer, reaching the milestone in just 77 balls.\nDespite the rise of Bazball and the reign of Ben Stokes, this record still holds today.\nBut, would one of these modern players be able to achieve what Jessop did, with the tools he did it with?\nWe brought in James Coles and Kiran Carlson from The Hundred’s London Spirit to put a replica of the original bat to the test.\nIsn’t it just typical that we have had heatwave after heatwave and no international cricket for the last month yet just as England return to action there is rain around!\nGood morning from Leeds. There’s been some overnight rain, and there’s an autumnal feel in the air, with cooler temperatures and an even split of blue skies and clouds. They say look up, not down here...\nI am sure, even though he is very experienced with the media, that one aspect of becoming captain once more Joe Root will not enjoy is the sheer volume of media duties he has to do now he is back as skipper:\n“You always want to represent your country in the best possible light. We might have fallen short of that on a few occasions in the recent past. The only [way] to rectify that is be really good role models, good human beings and put in really strong performances on the field. That does take time and the first opportunity is this week.\n“One of the big challenges is not trying to be Ben Stokes. He is a unique player with unbelievable qualities that I don’t think are easy to replicate. I don’t think there is any point trying to. Understanding things have changed, it is going to have a slightly different look to it but that is OK.\n“There has been a lot of good stuff that has happened in the past four years that will serve us really well. It is about not ripping it up, just fine-tuning and evolving and taking that to the next level. He did a phenomenal job throughout his tenure and will go down as a brilliant Test player. We have got to find a way of evolving the team without him.\n“I don’t think there is a huge amount we need to change. It is small things that will make a big difference. It’s not about ripping it up. It’s about keeping a lot of the good stuff Ben and Baz built for a long while, but making small adjustments that will make a big difference.”\nIt is something new and something old up in Leeds this week with a new era beginning for English Test cricket begins up in Yorkshire as England take on Pakistan at Headingley in the first of a three-match series. In the last Test England played all the way back in the latter stages of June, former captain Ben Stokes announced his retirement mid-Test against New Zealand at Trent Bridge. Joe Root is back as captain, four years on from his five-year stint as England Test captain coming to an end, with his first game back as skipper being on his home ground. This will be Root’s 66th Test as England captain, which will extend his own record, but he won just one of his final 17 Tests as skipper during his first stint in the role.\nNot only has the captain changed but so has the coach. Since Stokes’ retirement, England have decided to move on from Brendon McCullum as Test coach, although the New Zealander will remain the white-ball coach. McCullum’s compatriot and good friend Stephen Fleming has been appointed as McCullum’s successor but he will not start his new role until later this year for the tour to South Africa starting in December. That means Marcus Trescothick will be in interim charge for this series. Fleming has no experience as an international head coach but has extensive experience on the franchise circuit having been a long-term head coach of the Chennai Super Kings in the Indian Premier League.\nJoe Root is back at the helm\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nOpener Emilio Gay, who made his debut in the New Zealand series, was a doubt for this series having injured his shoulder but is fit to play, although he did register a pair last week for a Counties XI against Pakistan. Jacob Bethell has been ruled out with a knee injury, which means Jordan Cox is set to take his place in the side at number three. England going forward will have to address the Stokes void and for the time being it will be Dan Lawrence who will be at six, all-be-it not a like-for-like replacement for Stokes.\nPakistan were last in Test action earlier this month over in the Caribbean. They lost the first match of that series against the West Indies but won the second game by eight wickets to draw the series 1-1. These sides last met in a Test series back in October 2024; England won the first game of the series at a canter before Pakistan came back to win the next two matches to claim a 2-1 series victory. The tourists have been dealt a huge blow on the eve of the first Test, with captain Babar Azam ruled out of this game with a hand injury. Salman Ali Agha will act as interim skipper this week.\nPakistan will have to cope without the services of their captain for this match\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nPlay from Headingley is set to get under way at 11am but there are some showers in the forecast.",
