        "full_content": "Harry Brook falls nine short of a hundred on his home ground where he made 99 last year\nCredit: Craig Brough/Reuters\nA half-full Headingley fell silent and Harry Brook put his hand on his head when he was bowled for 91 as a home-ground Test hundred went begging once again.\nBrook has not scored a Test century at Headingley and this was a golden opportunity before the ground enters the deep freeze for England until 2029, dumped off the rota for grounds further south.\nThe faithful that braved the dark and cold long after 7pm deserved to see Brook go one better than his 99 here against India last year which was an innings that ended in a Bazball ego trip: a hook down to deep square leg as he walked into the bouncer trap.\nHe cut out the risky shots this time as England built a 195-run lead over Pakistan at 366 for eight. The loss of three wickets for eight runs in the gloom of the last 20 minutes salvaged some pride for Pakistan but England are in control and the prospect of a day three finish hangs in the damp Leeds air.\nBrook still scored at almost a run a ball, stroking 14 fours, but there was nothing outrageous, just a determination to take his chance and wait for the steady supply of poor deliveries until he was bowled by an arm ball from Usman Ali, via a deflection off his back leg.\nImran’s Tigers are long gone. This is a toothless Pakistan attack and a shambles of a fielding side. They even gave Brook a helping hand, dropping him at slip on one. It was a costly mistake by captain Salman Ali Agha, fumbling a catch to his right just after his team had dismissed the well-set Joe Root and Jordan Cox in five balls to give themselves a chance. Had he taken the catch, England would have been 209 for five, under 40 ahead and with plenty of work to do.\n— SHIVAM RAJPUT 🏏 INDIA 🇮🇳 | WORLD CRICKET 🌍 LIV (@shivamr75742206) August 20, 2026\nBrook also averages 84 against Pakistan so hardly needs an opportunity to take them apart. After the let off he eased into his rhythm and the game was gone for Pakistan as he put on 120 with Lawrence.\nUsman also whipped out Gus Atkinson and Ollie Robinson in successive balls after removing Brook to finish with five for 92 on a curious day of at times fairly average Test cricket.\nFor England, Brook’s dismissal summed up a day of steady work without the A star on GCSE results day that a hundred would have merited. Cox made 73 in his third Test innings, out-batting Root in a 150 run stand. Root scored 66 and knows he may have missed his chance for one more Headingley hundred. He will be 38 in 2029 and with his team so far ahead in the game, there is unlikely to be a chance for a hundred in the second innings.\nJoe Root, pictured, made 66 runs off 112 balls as his partnership with Jordan Cox surpassed 100 runs\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nLawrence settled back into Test cricket in his role as Ben Stokes’s replacement at No 6 with a half century, but a top edged slog sweep off Usman was a waste of an opportunity to really nail down a place.\nIt is hard to draw any conclusions because Pakistan are so poor but there are places going in this side and Stephen Fleming, watching at home in New Zealand, will be encouraged by Cox and Lawrence’s runs even though he will want to see more.\nPakistan fed a diet of half volleys or 78mph powder puff balls and England never really had to break stride, scoring at 4.46 an over. Mohammad Abbas was the only bowler to bowl at under four an over as England paid his skills the respect they deserve. Usman bowled some good deliveries with the ball gripping and turning on day two but was inconsistent while the rest were anonymous.\nThe ground fielding was abysmal. Bowlers were either too lazy to dive on the boundary or too unathletic to get down quick enough. The peak of incompetence was Usman trying to put a boot on a ball to stop a four at fine leg only to get his feet in a tangle and kick it over the rope.\nBrook was dropped when he dangled his bat without moving his feet and a dolly of a caught-and-bowled off Cox on 57 was fluffed by Khurram Shahzad. Pakistan teams always have a fire in the belly even if they lack world class talent but there seems to be an air of apathy from this team.\nUnlike Ollie Pope say, Cox has a certain zen at the crease and played some excellent drives through the off side with controlled aggression. He saw off the danger of Abbas on a cloudy afternoon, after rain delayed the start until 2.45pm. The pitch was still offering plenty of assistance to the seamers but Cox played late and assuredly. The lack of pace in the Pakistan attack meant that he was never put off his stride or faced any short stuff; that test awaits further down the line.\nCox batted patiently, making 14 boundaries, before he was bowled out by Usman Ali, caught by Muhammad Rizwan\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nHe fell to Usman, nicking a ball that turned outside off stump, and in the following over Root was lbw to Shahzad trying to play to the onside and pick the gap in a seven two off side field. Both reviewed, wasting lives for others, although Cox later said he thought the ball had struck his pad, not the outside edge.\nPakistan tried the bouncer theory to Brook but it did not last long as he put away a 78mph lollipop from Mohammad Ali. Pakistan tested Lawrence’s patience by bowling wide of off stump but he did not take the bait. The concentration did not last, and that will be costly against better teams. The mistake from Lawrence gave Pakistan a glimmer as 322 for four became 364 for eight in a sloppy end.\nCox ‘thrilled’ to make 73 but regrets missing century\nJordan Cox rued missing out on a maiden Test century as he looks to “bang the door down” and secure a place in England’s first-choice batting lineup.\nEngland’s new No 3, Cox made 73 in his second Test, having made a long-awaited debut at No 7 at the Oval earlier this summer and described himself as “thrilled”, but mildly regretful that he had failed to kick on to three figures.\nLike Cox, Dan Lawrence passed fifty but failed to reach a first Test ton, while Joe Root and Harry Brook also failed to convert on their home ground. Between them, they forged a superb position for England, who lead by 195 with two wickets in hand.\nCox is standing in for Jacob Bethell at No 3, one place higher than he bats for his county, Essex, but there are places up for grabs in a transitional England batting lineup. A strong showing in this series could lock him in for the winter tour to South Africa.\nThough neither Harry Brook (left) or Dan Lawrence reached a century at Headingley, they helped put England in a strong position ahead of day three\nCredit: Craig Brough/Reuters\n“Yeah, of course I did,” said Cox, when asked if he was frustrated to miss out on three figures. Cox was caught behind off spinner Ali Usman, but believed he had hit his pad with his bat, not the ball.\n“Once I got to fifty, I moved to sixty quickly, and thought ‘there’s a chance here’. Just keep being disciplined, ball by ball. I had it at the back of my mind, thinking you have worked so hard, do not give this opportunity up. That is exactly what I do in county cricket. I get to fifty, see how many runs I need to score, and work down from there.”\nAsked if his team-mates were feeling the same emotion, he said: “Oh yeah there is. Every team, every player wants to score Test hundreds. Four of us got fifties, so we were hoping one would kick on, and instead of making it just a hundred, make it 150 or 170.\n“I am thrilled. I would have taken it at the start of the Test match. I let myself down a tiny bit, but at the end of the day, you take 70.”\nCox resumed on day two on 23, then was dropped by the bowler Khurram Shahzad on 57. In the course of his innings, he was compared to AB de Villiers by the former England captain Michael Vaughan.\nCox is likely to get a good run across formats. England had hoped to call on Sam Curran for the third Test of this series, but his summer is over due to a groin injury. Curran will undergo surgery on Friday.\nIt is likely England will be without Curran, Bethell and Tom Banton for September’s white-ball series against Sri Lanka, which should mean greater opportunity for the likes of Cox and Sussex’s James Coles, and could even see a recall for Zak Crawley, who made an assured 81 for Kent against Middlesex on Thursday. Will Jacks is currently sidelined with appendicitis, but is expected to have recovered in time for the series.\nGood contributions from Messrs Cox, Root, Brook and Lawrence have put England firmly in control though the late flurry of wickets and Brook’s disappointment of being out in the nineties for the second year running on his home ground took some of the gloss off an otherwise pretty dominant two days. Good to see Ali Usman showing that spin is still a valuable weapon in Tests in England, too.\nNick’s match report will be here shortly but please join us tomorrow for day three which may well be the last day given the pitch and the quality of England’s seam attack.\nSmith plays out five dot balls from Ali Usman before taking an single with a firm push to cover off the last ball and that’s stumps. England lead by 195.\nIs Smith batting for tomorrow? Seems to be but is beaten by Abbas when he manages to hit a crack and the ball nibbles away from the edge late. He takes a single off the fourth ball by working it through midwicket. Archer defends one and is beaten by the last one that also hits a crack which will give Jofra and pals something to work with on Friday.\nNo hat-trick as Archer defends. England make it to 80 overs for only the seventh time in their last 17 innings since the start of the Ashes.\nUsman Ali has shown he was the right pick after all. Sajid has the superior record but the left-armer has bowled some terrific deliveries in his five for 91.\nRobinson b Usman 0 A five-for and he’s on a hat-trick! This one arced in and turned sharply away to castle Robinson who departs for a golden duck. FOW 364/8\nSmith takes a pair of singles off Abbas, one with the outside edge, one off the inside while Atkinson jogs a leg-bye after being pinned by a big inswinger that was angling down leg.\nBrook experiences deja vu at Headingley\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nMaiden for Ali Usman to Atkinson, stringing together six decent deliveries for the first time. He has 21-1-90-3 which tells its own tale about how he has mixed jewels and dross.\nAbbas replaces Khurram and beats Smith with a classic delivery that arrows in then nips away. But Smith also takes a single off a thick edge through point and Atkinson shuffles across to whip through midwicket for three.\nBrook was given a life on one and added 90 bonus runs. Yes, he’ll be miffed at missing a century but in perspective he made an excellent contribution. Poor Rizwan was struck in the right eye by a bail which flew througfh the top slit of the grille when Brook was castled but seems to have shut it in the nick of time.\nBrook b Usman 91 Goes back to work it between midwicket and mid-on but it slides on and beats the outside edge, crashing into off and middle as Brook turned his wrists. A second ninety for the vice-captain at Headingley in successive Tests here. FOW 356/6\nMohammed Ali betrays his exhaustion by diving over Brook’s on-drive and letting it scuttle under him for four. The worst fielders yesterday and today have been the bowlers. No brotherhood of the ball here. Usman has kicked one over the rope, Ali has been sloppy a couple of times and Abbas has given up the ghost when a sprint may have saved runs on more than one occasion.\nBrook, closing in on something significant on his home ground, also picks Khurram off for a pair of twos – a back-cut and a midwicket flick – then pinches the strike with another whisk through the legside.\nThe sub Sajid saves a single with a wholehearted chase to the midwicket fence, diving to drag Brook’s pull back inches in front of the rope. They run three and milk another couple of singles.\nSmith is such a destructive batsman and looks a million dollars at times like this – creaming Khurram through extra-cover for four after a big stride to the pitch of the ball. Towards the end of the over, Smith gets up on his toes to steer another boundary, this one down through gully. Pakistan cannot maintain any form of control on the run rate.\nSmith works the spinner off his pads to get off the mark. Brook hits his 12th four with a dainty, deft late cut as England keep trucking at nearly five an over, building their lead to 160.\nBrook is pinned by Khurram’s nip-backer on the back thighpad. Pakistan appeal but the umpire shakes his head. The captain asks the bowler and keeper whether a review, their last, should be deployed but they indicate that it was too high and he opts not to bother, wisely as it proves.\nAli Usman takes a second wicket and Lawrence looks to the heavens as if he cannot believe he’s chucked it away when so well set. Two balls before Brook had pulled long hop for four then nurdled a single to put Lawrence on strike. England lead by 146.\nKhurram replaces Mohammad Ali, drops short and Brook leans back to pull it hard and flat over the non-striker’s head. He dabs the next two balls for two and a single as Ali Usman, after some ham-fisted fielding earlier, gathers both cleanly, much to the delight of the crowd whose enthusiastic cheers make him beam from ear to ear.\nBrook whacks a wide one from Usman to the point sweeper for a single. Lawrence, who made Test fifties at Ahmedabad, Edgbaston, Galle and Barbados, now has a fifth, brought up off a thick inside edge for one when trying to drive the left-arm spinner.\nOh, hang on. Lawrence does take on a slow bouncer and it does draw the mistake as it leaps higher than he expected but he gloves it over the keeper for four nonetheless. Streaky but they all count and he ducks the next four half-trackers as Ali groans with the effort. That four brought the 300 and the hundred partnership up.\nFinally Ali pitches the ball up and Lawrence flogs it through the covers for four with power and panache.\nOn come the drinks for the second time today.\nIs it cocoa in this light? Ovaltine? Horlicks?\nThis role at number six is a far better fit for Dan Lawrence than his three Tests as opener against Sri Lanka in 2024, when he looked completely ill-equipped. But against an old ball and a tiring attack, he can be a very dangerous Test match number six.\nLovely shot from Brook, late cutting Usman wide of the slip for four. Usman has been the best of the bowlers and fires one into Brook’s knee with one that was angling down.\nPakistan send men out on the hook and Ali duly delivers a short ball at 77mph. Brook lets it go but takes on the next one, rolling his wrists and cuffing it for four. Lawrence lets two more slow, loopy bouncers go. What are they doing? Look at the sky? Look how dark it is? This is rubbish. They are swing and seam bowlers, three sub 80mph merchants and they’re trying to buy wickets with filth. I mean, Brook and Lawrence are cavalier but not that cavalier in the first innings of a new approach.\nI can’t remember a worse collective effort fielding on the boundary than Pakistan in this Test. It’s been slap-stick stuff.\nLawrence chops the spinner down through third for three and Brook brings up his 19th Test half-century by also carving a shorter one, this time for a single. He calls on the physio after raising his bat, pointing out that his hands are cramping up. Should try blogging an ODI… After some gel and fluids, he puts fresh gloves on and resumes his innings. No pickle juice from what I could see.\nThe slog sweep has been productive for Lawrence who dispatches Usman to cow corner for four. He misses a full bunger at the end of the over that triggers some farcical cricket from Pakistan in burning a review and missing a run out. It was quite farcical to miss it, too.\nPakistan have a seven-two field for Brook but it doesn’t matter if one the seven is a carthorse as Usman proves himself to be when Brook late cuts Mohammed Ali down through third man, Usman plods after it, sticks out a boot and wellies it over the rope. Ali then strays on to middle and Brook plays the Nat Sciver-Brunt inside-edge drive through his own legs for four more, the so-called ‘Natmeg’.\nEngland take Usman for three singles, Brook’s into the offside, Lawrence a cut to the point sweeper, and then Lawrence devours the slider, slog sweeping it over the square leg umpire for four.\nLawrence keeps walking across his crease and almost squares himself up defending balls down the corridor. Mohammad Ali finds his edge as Lawrence ends up with his right leg coming round so he’s almost face on but he is redeemed by soft hands guiding it down in front of first slip.\nAli keeps plugging away outside off and makes Lawrence stay busy, shuffling across, sometimes leaving, sometimes playing at it. Not a bad ploy to try to bore him out given his unorthodox offside technique.\nMaiden.\nLawrence likes to feel bat on ball\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nBrook playing the spinner off the back foot largely, as is his wont, cuts Usman for a single and, when Lawrence takes a single to cover, sweeps for four. It is getting darker and gloomier. Feels like a typical holiday in Wales circa 1979.\nBrook sweeps a rare loose one from Usman, sprayed on to his pads, fine for four but then misjudges the length of the next ball and defends in a bit of a flap on the back foot when he should have played forward. Opening the face, Brook takes a single. Pakistan send the lbw shout upstairs but the replay not only shows that it would have missed but after it had struck the pad it kissed Lawrence’s glove and had Rizwan caught it, that would have been out.\nMohammad Ali takes the opening over of the evening session. Salman Agha is back in the field. Ali, an 80mph merchant at best, makes one sit up outside off and Brook flayed it uppishly and just past cover for four. He was trying to smack it through point but was on it too soon.\nLawrence, open stance, lets one go outside off and is given one that feeds his strength, nice and full on middle and he, with his tongue out like a child artist, skelps it through midwicket for four.\nDan Lawrence hs made an unorthodox but so far effective return to Test cricket\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nEntertaining if low on quality session of cricket. Fifties for Joe Root and Jordan Cox but some poor bowling and fielding from Pakistan was somehow rescued by two quick wickets. Root and Cox wasted reviews on their dismissals leaving England without any for the rest of the innings. Usman Ali has found grip and turn, which is how he dismissed Cox off the outside edge, while Khurram Shahzad bowled a mixed bag conceding eight fours in five overs but dismissed Root leg-before and had two catches dropped. One was Brook on one at second slip by Salman Ali Agha forcing the Pakistan captain off the field for treatment. Lawrence and Brook have a 68 run lead to build after tea and are not going to hang around.\nLawrence tries to slog sweep Usman but the ball turns away from the right-hander resulting in a top edge that lands safely behind square leg. After that encouragement, the spinner drags the next ball down and Lawrence smears it off the back foot in front of cover point for four. Although Lawrence was beaten and pinned by the arm-ball, the line suggested it was missing leg and Pakistan wisely decide not to review.\nMaiden for Abbas to Brook who once again plays that catching practice pat to the cordon. Can’t see any merit in that shot unless he hits it much harder. More of a deflection than a proper stroke but this time it does not yield a chance. One more over before tea.\nI wouldn’t say that Lawrence will not be an effective Test player but he’ll never be a pretty one with all those moving parts. After mistiming a flick and plinking it off the inside edge and being beaten by one that arced in and turned away, he throws his hands at a drive to another ball that turns away and edges it just wide of slip’s span at head height for four.\nLawrence shuffles across to press a single off Abbas towards point and Brook uses his fast hands and wrists to drive an inswinger behind point for three.\nSalman Ali Agha goes off for treatment after hurting his right hand in the process of dropping Harry Brook\nCredit: Getty Images/Philip Brown\nUsman is bowling well now with guile and flight. Brook takes him for two by opening his wrists and the face of his bat and running the ball down to third man.\nTime to bring back Mohammad Abbas, says Saud Shakeel, the caretaker to the stand-in captain.\nLawrence, batting with a black undershirt, moves across the crease to cream a straight drive off Khurram for four then, despite the unorthodox set-up, constantly moving vertically and horizontally, like Steve Smith in some ways, plays a terrific on-drive for four. Re that undershirt – what would Lord Maclaurin have said? Or PBH May who stopped DI Gower wearing his cardinal’s red socks.\nLawrence, if you’ve seen any of his previous 14 Tests, has a very low grip and is bottom-hand dominant, which gives him power but can occasionally make it seem as if he’s clawing the ball into the legside. He moves busily across his crease in defence and down it, looking to attack, but Usman keeps him quiet until the spinner is let down by Imam’s dozy fielding again which gift-wraps a single off the last ball.\nSalman Ali Agha drops Brook at second slip, low to his right and hurts the ring finger on his right hand. It was an odd shot from Brook, chasing a wide one from Khurram and almost patting it towards the cordon. The ball rattles away for four and the captain jogs off the field with blood pouring from a cut. Brook, as seemingly insouciant as ever, makes the most of his life with a wristy clip off his toes fine of long leg for four more.\nBrook gets Pakistan’s beans jumping with a tight single to cover that exploits Imam’s lackadaisical fielding. After that, though, Usman, now extracting bounce and turn, keeps Lawrence quiet with five dot balls.\nAfter that drama, Lawrence gets off the mark with a shovelly drive to a ball that takes the inside edge and squirts away for two.\nJust as we thought this game was ceasing to be a contest, a wicket pops up from nowhere. Lazy dismissal from Cox, who didn’t realise he hit it – somehow. Anyway, a decent innings and very good partnership have put England right in charge.\nRoot lbw b Khurram66 Stone dead. Would have knocked over leg stump. Root walked across to flick a fuller one down through square leg and missed it. Another burnt review and now England have none left. FOW 202/4\nAfter the orthodox sweep in Usman’s first over, Root treats us to the reverse and nails it for four. Root works a single into the legside, Cox provokes a cry of ‘Catch it!’ when he spoons a drive for two wide of cover but the catch comes the next ball. Another look tells us that Cox thought he hit his pad with his bat, which he did, but he also hit the ball.\nCox c Rizwan b Usman 73 A wonderful delivery out of nowhere with flight and late turn, kissing the edge as Cox tried to force it through mid-off and Rizwan took a very sharp catch. Chris Gaffaney gave it out straightaway and there was a very audible woody sound. Was Cox checking for a fair catch or felt that he hadn’t edged it? FOW 198/3\nCox nicks it through to Rizwan\nCredit: DARREN STAPLES/AFP\nPakistan switch strategy and try to test Cox’s patience with a ring offside field saving the single and Khurram bowling down the corridor. Much better from Khurram, reeling off a maiden, sticking to the plan and even making Cox have an injudicious swipe across the line.\nJordan Cox does his world famous Dalek impression\nCredit: DARREN STAPLES/AFP\nAs Dinesh Karthik says, this is going badly wrong for Pakistan. But the blame lies with the selectors rather than the players. A powder puff pace attack is being flayed and the spinner is being milked. England take Usman for four singles. And yet, other than Abbas, he looks their best bet.\nChance! Khurram starts with a surprise bouncer on the slow side, messing with Cox’s timing. The right-hander, deep in his crease, flat bats it hard back at the bowler who gets his hands up to chest height but spills the opportunity.\nThat seems to encourage Khurram that the short ball might work so he tries two more and Cox hammers them through midwicket with punishing pulls for four.\nCox then plays a gorgeous, punchy drive on his toes through cover point for a third four as Pakistan try to rally round Khurram who had insult added to injury over five balls.\nThis is the worst Pakistan attack I have seen, totally toothless once Abbas is off. The slump in their Test cricket is alarming. Great news for Jordan Cox, who is bedding in nicely at Mo 3 against bowling that lacks any pace to unsettle him. It’s a lovely 50 and what is striking is how calm he has been, compared with Pope.\nAfter some time to think Salman Ali Agha turns to spin. Ali Usman starts with some grip and rip beating Root’s attempted drive on the outside edge, exciting the fielders. He’s bowling orthodox fashion, round the wicket to the right-handers. Root also sweeps in orthodox fashion, cuffing it fine for two to take the lead and England end the over with a couple of easy singles with the field set back.\nLet’s not get carried away but simply on the basis of this session Jordon Cox has batted better than Joe Root as he hasn’t played and missed a couple of times as Root has. And when Cox put away those four fours in an over he didn’t really chase any of them, just put the bad balls away. Not his last Test 50 for England.\nA serene start to the day for England, adding 58 in brisk time. They’re now level with Pakistan; will be aiming to have a lead of 150+ by the close of play.\nNow Root reaches fifty with a flick when Mohammad Ali errs too full and straight, whipping the ball to the boundary between midwicket and square leg. It’s his 68th in total and 28th as captain. After Root rotates the strike with a wristy square drive, Cox nails an off-drive for four as Ali serves up another greasy half-volley.\nAbbas signals his intent to make induckers his stock delivery by posting a leg slip. Cox turns the first of them wide of the man for a single to third man. His next effort starts too straight, swings too much and sails down between leg slip and the keeper for four byes.\nRoot pushes a straight one through point for a single. Rizwan tries to boost morale with some chirpy encouragement. Abbas, after a no-ball, comes wide on the crease and hoops an inswinger on to Cox’s pads and he helps it on its way wide of leg slip for four to bring up a maiden Test fifty.\nRoot pulls a short one from Mohammad Ali for single and Cox cuts another down to third man, ripping a chunk out of the edge of his bat. When the ball is returned to the bowler, he takes it to the umpire as a couple of threads from the seam are hanging off. Out come the scissors for repairs rather than the briefcase of used balls.\nMohammad Abbas’s big inswinger does way too much. Cox shapes to flick it down to fine leg, misses but his thighpad adds four leg-byes to the total. A couple of balls later Cox brings up the hundred partnership with a handsome extra-cover drive for four. Very early days but he looks the part.\nIf you’re at a loose end later, I can commend the discussion between Jonathan Agnew, Steve Finn and Mark Wood that was on TMS from 11am until noon, a fascinating chat about injury, knowing when you’re time is up, inside the dressing room under both Root and Stokes, the 2025-26 Ashes tour and the enduring pain, psychological and physical, of Perth and much else besides.\nA maiden for Mohammad Ali that Root either blocks, leaves or tests the infield. Ali tries the surprise bouncer, bangs it in very short and Root lets it vault over his shoulders, resisting temptation and smiling, possibly contemptuously.\nRizwan has been up to the stumps for Abbas since ball two\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nStuart Broad is on commentary now and uses his expert eye to dissect Pakistan’s failings with the ball. He says their outswingers are being wasted by starting them on fourth stump and allowing the two right-handers to leave them rather than forcing them to play by pitching on off and there are too many attempted inswingers which are fired down leg rather than allowed to move in the air.\nAfter two singles a chap in the crowd is captured coming down the steps carrying four pints on one of those horrible cardboard trays and, having spilt so much beer already to erode the structure, the front half gives way and he loses the front two pints. I was very fortunate to be a spectator at Edgbaston on day one of the 2005 Ashes Test when , after about an hour, the same happened to someone two rows in front as he was negotiating the trip hazards of picnic bags as he shuffled back to his seat. He lost all four but managed to deposit them all over someone in the row in front, absolutely drenching him in Banks’ Bitter. He took it very well…\nAbbas starts down the hill with a maiden to Cox which will look better in the scorebook than it did in actuality. His line was all over the shop and Cox left or defended with ease.\nPakistan are so lucky that they do not have to spend a whole day in the field with a threadbare attack. Sajid Khan, their moustachioed offspinner, has to come into the side for the second Test to give it some ooomph in the field, some animation, even though Lord’s is not a spinning ground. But why not aim for spinning pitches at HQ and make it the ground’s USP?\nTo read Scyld’s view on the Lord’s pitch, click here.\nMohammad Ali replaces Abbas and runs in up the hill as if he’s towing a caravan. Starts with a welcoming half-volley and Root flicks it through square leg for two. He hoops the next three outside off and Root is not tempted to chase them then defends the next two. Abbas is only changing ends. He bowed 10 overs off the reel yesterday from the Football Stand End but will now have a go from the Kirkstall Lane End.\nCox drives loosely but effectively\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nAfter a good start in his opening two overs, Khurram takes the silver cloche off a pie, spraying a half-volley on to Cox’s pads and he greedily clips it fine of long leg for four. After a second no-ball as the runaway train came down the track, he gives Cox some width outside off and the batsman goes for it, spooning a drive through cover point with the outside third of the bat. Two balls later he goes a touch shorter and Cox is surprised by the bounce but manages, not intentionally, to squirt it off the splice down through gully for four.\nAnd then Khurram deals up another half-volley and Cox whosks it slickly through midwicket for four more. He’s catching up with Roo after taking 16 plus the no-ball off the over. Blogger’s curse strikes the bowler.\nJordan Cox looked excellent last night. If he can get through the much harder batting conditions today, that would be a real statement. He opened for a couple of years for Kent in the Championship, so could conceivably slot up to open when Jacob Bethell returns at three.\nJordan Cox is wielding a Kookaburra bat. Can’t remember an England regular since Jos Buttler and before that Graham Thorpe who was sponsored by the Melbourne company. They don’t pick many Poms. I digress, anyway, he uses it to work Abbas of his legs for a single as England start cautiously against the moving ball in 17C temperatures and with pregnant, grey clouds above.\nThis is a markedly better spell from Khurram than he managed yesterday. Cox leaves the big outswinger alone, chops the next down to third man for a single but the right-arm dobber beats Root a second time with the one that wobbles in and nips away and then, after a no-ball for overstepping as he got ahead of himself coming down the hill, forces Root to block in his crease.\nAbbas starts with the keeper back so Root walks down the pitch and clips the ball off middle through square leg for four. After one ball Rizwan comes up to the stumps. That change forces Root back to take guard on the crease line rather than 12 inches outside and he defends three and leaves one of the next four. Abbas strays on to leg stump, Root’s eyes widen but he plays it gently, slightly squared up, and doesn’t beat the infield.\nRoot gets weaving on day two\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\nKhurram, under oyster-shell, grey skies and the floodlights fully on, starts with a beauty, full, wobbling in to off-stump then nipping away from Root’s loose drive.\nThe right-armer tries to swing it the other way, spearing in the yorker but it angles too far across and Root works it to midwicket without return. A nip-backer pins him outside the line, Root defends two into the offside then pinches the strike by opening the face and running the ball behind point for a single.\nMohammad Abbas will obviously take the ball from the Football Stand End.\nHark at Will ‘Kid’ Macpherson here:\nAmazing to think it is 25 years to the day that Mark Butcher made his extraordinary unbeaten 173 here against the Aussies. The first England Ashes Test victory I remember, and a formative cricketing experience for me.\nTo the closing strains of Jerusalem. Joe Root skips on to the outfield then performs those side-jigs he likes to do to get his hips and back loose. Khurram will open the bowling.\nExtra 15mins was needed to prepare the pitch as covers were still on. With delayed start tea will now be taken at 17:00, Final session will be 17:20 - 19:00 with an extra 30mins to bowl the overs There will be a maximum of 58 overs in the day\nTea will be taken at 16:50, Final session will be 17:10 - 19:00 with an extra 30 mins to bowl the overs. There will be a maximum of 63 overs in the day.\nA reminder of episode one and what’s in store in episode two\nLink copied to clipboard\nI’ve spent the morning listening to episode two of Sir Geoffrey Boycott: An extraordinary Innings, our four-part podcast series. In it he discusses being dropped for slow scoring, his 100th first-class hundred and why some people don’t like him. Boycott has divided opinion his entire life but he offers no excuses for being forthright. “I’m not the one to back off in anything. If you stand up for what you believe, there’ll always be opposition. Simple. If you want everybody to like you, then acquiesce. Do what they tell you. Don’t find that very appealing.”\nI understand but don’t agree with their decision to drop Shaheen Shah Afridi and Naseem Shah given recent poor performances but why have they picked Ali Usman, the slow left-armer, to play his third Test rather than Sajid Khan, the off-spinner, who has 77 Test wickets at 28.81 and took eight in the match at Port of Spain a fortnight ago.\nI ranked England’s Test cricket captains from the last 50 years\nExuding authority but not ostentatiously. The cynosure of all eyes when England are in the field. Often in the slips at the heart of decision-making. Michael Brearley, Andrew Strauss, Alastair Cook and Joe Root, who has been reappointed as captain, have stood at first slip – but sometimes elsewhere. Michael Vaughan posted himself at mid-on so he could confer with his bowler. Always appearing to be in control and to have something – usually a fresh bowler – up his sleeve. Jollying the media along and giving them a line.\nThese are the qualities that have distinguished the best England Test captains of the past 50 years, when I have been covering the team. Curiously, leading from the front is not essential, or rather it was the rule to which Brearley was the exception as he never made a Test hundred.\nWinning the Ashes and holding on to them has always been the primary objective of English cricket, and it looks as though it always will be, so long as Test cricket lasts. The one period that was an exception was the 1980s when West Indies set the standard as world champions, and England wanted to compete, but could not, losing 17 Tests while winning none. So Graham Gooch deserves exceptional credit for turning that tide.\nResults come first, but there is a consideration for style too, because panache and aggression help to sell the game to the English public.\nHere are the England Test captains of my time who have led the side in five or more Tests, the pick of them to be bracketed alongside the best before it, such as Raymond Illingworth, Sir Leonard Hutton, Percy Chapman and Douglas Jardine, who all achieved the hardest feat of winning an Ashes series in Australia.\nMust be pretty wet out there underfoot but they are beginning to remove the covers. Almost needless to say that any more rain would delay the 1.45pm inspection.\nThe umpires chat to the groundstaff\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nBut on the wider picture on TV when Sky’s cameras panned out, one could see a fully covered square and fully covered practice pitched in front of the old changing rooms on the east side of the ground.\nI understand that it’s not currently raining which is good given the MetOffice says there is a 90 per cent likelihood of some rain between noon and 2pm, reducing to 80 per cent at 3pm, 60 per cent at 4pm and 30 per cent thereafter. It forecasts showers between 11pm and 2pm tomorrow but a full dry day on Saturday.\nI’ve spent the morning listening to episode two of Geoffrey Boycott: An extraordinary Innings, our four-part podcast series. In it he discusses being dropped for slow scoring, his 100th first-class hundred and why some people don’t like him. “I’m not the one to back off in anything. If you stand up for what you believe, there’ll always be opposition. Simple. If you want everybody to like you, then acquiesce. Do what they tell you. Don’t find that very appealing.”\nThe players are currently enjoying their lunch break, which has been brought forward, and there will be a pitch inspection at 1pm. I suspect though that it will still be a while before we see any action as the mopping up job will take quite some time and it relies on the rain stopping and remaining away.\nI’ve been for a lap of the ground. It’s not currently raining, but is cold, and most people are huddling below the stands, eating and drinking. The NRL is being broadcast at the Football Stand End, and there’s a lot of concourse cricket being played by kids.\nI would say there is some cause for optimism for a start around 3, in very bowler friendly conditions.\nThe Yorkshireman averages a tick over 53 in Test cricket through 38 games with 10 centuries to his name. But could he be doing even better? Maybe if you take out some of his absolutely ridiculous dismissals then his average would be better.\nEngland will not play a home Test match up north next summer in The Ashes. In addition to many other reasons why that is a ridiculous concept, here is England’s record at Headingley and Old Trafford between 2018 and 2026:\nHeadingley: played six, won six Old Trafford: played eight, won five, lost once, drawn twice\nEngland’s former Test captain is in the XI for Durham against Derbyshire as is Brydon Carse, who has been released from the England squad to play for his county this week. Shoaib Bashir has been named in Derbyshire’s side against Durham.\n“Speaking to captain Joe Root and coach Marcus Trescothick before the game, we spoke about me stetting the tone. Before I bowled, I pictured how I would go at the left-handers. It is not my favoured suit coming around the wicket but I decided to go that way straight away and it paid off first ball!\n“Going back into county cricket you have to bowl that bit fuller to hit the stumps and I have taken that into the Test game. Anyone who has looked to hit the top of the stumps has had the greatest success and it is just about being brave. Trying to get the batsmen driving and setting slightly straighter fields that maybe we are used to. On these wickets it is paying off.”\nThe players not selected in England’s XI for this Test are available for their counties. Shoaib Bashir and Brydon Carse are set to go head-to-head as Derbyshire take on Durham but that game has been delayed due to rain. A certain Ben Stokes is also set to play in that match. At The Oval, Matthew Fisher has taken two wickets for Surrey against Nottinghamshire with Ollie Pope taking a couple of catches. Sam Cook has taken a wicket for Essex down at Taunton against Somerset.\nThere is no play currently at Headingley but we are under way in some of the County Championship games. Sam Cook was named in the England squad for this Test but has been released to play for Essex having not been named in the England XI at Headingley. He is already in the wickets at Taunton.\n𝗖𝗢𝗢𝗞 𝗢𝗣𝗘𝗡𝗦 𝗢𝗨𝗥 𝗔𝗖𝗖𝗢𝗨𝗡𝗧!! 🙌\nSam Cook claims the first wicket of the match as he bowls Tom Lammonby, with the ball coming off the batter's thigh pad and onto the stumps!\nCarnage at the Oval on another very green track: Notts are 14 for three, with England squad member Matt Fisher has two wickets and another, Ollie Pope, has two catches.\nFormer Pakistan Women’s player Urooj Mumtaz is part of the Sky Sports team for this series and she has been giving her views on Pakistan:\n“You have to account for the lack of experience, with no one, bar Shan Masood, having much experience in England. But I also feel there was a gulf from the skill required. The way Salman Ali Agha, Saud Shakeel and Mohammad Rizwan, who Pakistan are banking on, got out begs the question of are we there with skills compared to the top teams in the world? I feel we are one step behind.”\nOn their bowlers:\n“If I am being brutally honest, they were a bit toothless. It seemed very placid and timid but that is what we expected on paper as there is no fear factor with a tearaway fast bowler.\n“Pakistan relied far too heavily on Mohammad Abbas to produce magic and I do not see this Pakistan seam or spin attack taking 20 wickets. That is the problem. It is sad to see guys who should be the senior bowlers for Pakistan; Shaheen Shah Afridi, Naseem Shah, fall off completely and not be the spearheads and sad to see that there is no fear factor.”\n“It was a pretty miserable day one for Pakistan. They would have batted first anyway had they won the toss. But I would rather look at it as an excellent day for England.\n“[Ollie] Robinson was so precise, using the new ball in a fantastic way. The percentage of balls that the batsmen were able to leave in his first spell and a half was around seven per cent. He bowled full and brought the stumps into play almost every single delivery. He was so skilful throughout the day, so surgical.”\nGood, if slightly surprising, news: there is a very gentle mop up going on out there, because the rain has stopped. Root and Brook have gone for a bat in the indoor school.\n“There is no doubt that [Joe] Root will have sterner tests of his captaincy over the next year than this Pakistan side. Pakistan look slightly low on quality and had a rough start to the series, not quite getting used to the conditions with bat and ball.\n“Root, with the bat, will not see a huge amount of threat in this bowling line-up, so will be eyeing up a big score. I would not bet against a century.\n“It is up to England to go and get a big score. There is a bit of seam movement but it looks a good pitch. England will look for 350-400. They will want to bat once and then knock over Pakistan again on day three, four or five.”\nWe will have to wait a while for any Test action here, but there’s a full programme of Championship cricket starting today. Some will be delayed no doubt, but Somerset v Essex at Taunton looks a belter, with Sam Cook taking the new ball for the visitors shortly. The clash of last year’s top two, Surrey and Notts, will start on time at the Oval, too. In Division Two, Ben Stokes will play for Durham against Derbyshire.\nA bit of injury news from the Oval, with England interest. Sam Curran is having surgery on a groin injury tomorrow, and is out for the season, ending any hope of prompt Test return. And Will Jacks has had his appendix out; England hopeful he will be fit to face Sri Lanka in T20s and ODIs next month.\nSam Curran will miss the remainder of England's home summer after being ruled out with a groin injury, with surgery scheduled for tomorrow.\nIsn’t it typical that we had a whole month in the middle of summer when we seemingly had heatwave after heatwave and there was no international cricket, yet the moment England are back in action the rain hits? Almost entirely predictable!\nSpectators may be waiting a while for play on day two\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nRemoving Archer and backing Robinson: Root’s ruthless return as England captain\nLink copied to clipboard\nRuthless use of Archer\nRoot’s (over)use of Jofra Archer was among the most scrutinised aspects of his first captaincy stint, especially in New Zealand in 2019.\nArcher had first use of the Rugby Stand End here but after just two overs, he was whipped out of the attack. Both were maiden, but Archer was firing the ball down the leg side and, knowing England had to capitalise on conditions, Archer was replaced by Gus Atkinson. Archer bowled just nine overs, compared to Robinson’s 18 and Atkinson’s 12.\nWith Josh Tongue not used until the ball was older, after 20 overs, the focus for England’s three new-ball options was finding the perfect length, and making batsmen play. Archer clearly had the highest leave percentage of the three, partly because he was bowling much shorter than Robinson.\nEngland cut ball in half after Robinson and Tongue both take five wickets\nLink copied to clipboard\nOllie Robinson and Josh Tongue celebrated their five-wicket hauls by getting the ball cut in half.\nThe England bowlers both took five wickets against Pakistan on the opening day of the Test series, as England bowled the tourists out for 171 before reaching 112 for two at stumps.\nBowlers who take a five-wicket haul in a Test match traditionally keep the ball after as a memento of the feat. Two bowlers taking five wickets in the same innings is exceptionally rare, but Robinson and Tongue found a way to celebrate their joint achievement.\nAfter Tongue took the final wicket of Pakistan’s innings, thereby matching Robinson in registering a five-wicket haul, the two men led England off the field together.\nJosh Tongue and Ollie Robinson have had the ball split in half so they can both have a momento from their five-wicket hauls 🏴🏏\nGood morning from Leeds where I’m afraid it’s been raining steadily for around 10 hours. Not heavy but very persistent, and I’d think we are in for a fairly hefty delay. The forecast it should clear up just after lunch but you sense it’ll still be a bit grey then. Fingers crossed we get some play.\nEngland head into day two of the first Test against Pakistan at Headingley in a commanding position after a strong opening day of the series. After bowling Pakistan out for 171, England closed day one on 112/2, just 59 runs behind with eight first-innings wickets remaining.\nJoe Root, in his first game back as England captain, won the toss and elected to bowl first, with conditions certainly early on day one favouring the bowlers. Ollie Robinson gave Root the perfect start to a new era for English Test cricket, taking a wicket with the first ball of the series by trapping 21-year-old Azan Awais in front. Robinson went on to rip through the Pakistan top order, ending up with his fifth five-for in Test cricket. He now has 88 Test wickets at an average of 21.25 and no England seamer since 1914 has taken as many wickets at a better average.\nJosh Tongue mopped up the Pakistan tail and joined Robinson in taking a five-wicket haul. They became the first pair of England bowlers to claim five wickets in the same Test innings for 20 years. That pair was Steve Harmison and Monty Panesar against the same opposition at Old Trafford back in 2006.\nJosh Tongue and Ollie Robinson did all the damage with the ball for England\nCredit: Gareth Copley/Getty Images\n“We are in a great position now but that first hour tomorrow morning [this morning], it might do a bit more at 11am,” Tongue told Sky Sports at the end of day one. “But if we focus on that first hour, we can get a good lead. It was nipping quite a bit this morning. Some of the balls they played in the evening session, it was playing quite a lot.\n“It is just about trying to be brave with your length, that was crucial on this wicket and trying to keep the stumps into play as much as possible. As a bowling unit, we were very good and [Ollie] Robinson was excellent. It was similar to his performance at Lord’s against New Zealand. He is an exceptional bowler when he hits his lines and lengths and he showed that today [yesterday].”\nAbdullah Shafique top scored for Pakistan, making 61, with Imam-ul-Haq (42) probably the only other Pakistan batsman to show any real resistance. Pakistan lost their final eight wickets for 74 and last five for just 21.\nRoot will resume on 37 not out and will be joined in the middle by Jordan Cox, who is on 23, as England aim to build a strong first-innings lead. Play on day two from Headingley is set to get under way at 11am, although the weather forecast for today does not look the best.",
        "content": "Harry Brook falls nine short of a hundred on his home ground where he made 99 last year\nCredit: Craig Brough/Reuters\nA half-full Headingley fell silent and Harry Brook put his hand on his head when he was bowled for 91 as a home-ground Test hundred went begging once again.\nBrook has not scored a Test century at Headingley and this was a golden opportunity before the ground enters the deep freeze f...",
