        "title": "Soaring welfare bill pushes national debt to almost £3tn",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2026/08/21/tax-surge-leaves-national-debt-on-brink-of-3-trillion/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473269+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/business/2026/08/21/TELEMMGLPICT000495949557_17872923253990_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqpVlberWd9EgFPZtcLiMQf0Rf_Wk3V23H2268P_XkPxc.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Live",
        "full_content": "John Healey, the Chancellor, faces a tax-raising Budget in October\nBritain’s soaring welfare bill forced John Healey to borrow more to balance the books last month.\nThe gap between tax receipts and spending unexpectedly resulted in a £1.8bn deficit in July, taking the national debt to within a whisker of £3tn.\nEconomists had predicted the Government would borrow nothing and blamed the deficit on the rising welfare bill.\nFigures published by the Office for National Statistics (ONS) this morning show total debt rose to £2.985tn in July.\nBenefit payments also climbed by 7.2pc, or £2bn, to £30bn last month.\nThe Chancellor now faces another tax-raising Budget in October, even as strong receipts from self-employed workers provide some respite.\nThe ONS also said Mr Healey spent £7.7bn on debt interest in July alone, almost 10pc higher than a year ago.\nAshley Webb, at Capital Economics said: “The weak performance was mainly due higher social payments.”\nMonthly borrowing was slightly higher than the Office for Budget Responsibility and markets expected, meaning the Government has borrowed £56.7bn this financial year to plug the gap between tax receipts and public spending.\nJulian Jessop, an independent economist and IEA economics fellow, said: “The latest official data highlight the fragility of the public finances as John Healey prepares his first Budget. Government borrowing was £2.3bn above forecast in July, and £0.7bn higher than in the same month a year ago.”\nJuly is traditionally a strong month for receipts from the self-employed, with self-assessed income tax receipts last month coming to £17.1bn, about £1.7bn more than a year earlier.\nHowever, higher inflation triggered by the Iran war means Mr Healey will be forced to use the Budget to find billions of pounds to repair the public finances.\nMr Healey said: “Fiscal discipline is the bedrock of our UK economic stability and national security which is why we are committed to meeting our fiscal rules, with a buffer against global uncertainties.”\nThe Resolution Foundation warned that the Chancellor’s headroom against his fiscal rules - self-imposed guardrails to guide public spending - has shrunk from around £24bn in the Spring Statement to less than £8bn today.\nThe think tank called on the Chancellor to use his first Budget on Oct 28 to put the nation’s finances on a more sustainable path.\nElliott Christensen, senior economist at the Resolution Foundation, said: “The Chancellor’s margin against his fiscal rules is now razor thin.”\nUK’s biggest banks warn Burnham against ‘damaging’ windfall tax\nLink copied to clipboard\nBritain’s biggest banks have warned Andy Burnham against introducing a “damaging” windfall tax on the UK’s finance industry.\nIn a letter on Thursday, lobby group UK Finance warned the Government that increasing the current levies placed on the sector would threaten to undermine Mr Burnham’s plan to deliver “growth in every postcode”.\nThere is fresh speculation that John Healey, the Chancellor, could launch a fresh raid on bank profits in his maiden Budget on Oct 28.\nHowever, UK Finance said it was “concerned that increasing taxes on banks would ultimately risk undermining the very tax base the Government seeks to protect and grow, as well as damaging the UK’s international competitiveness”.\nRise in government borrowing shows the ‘fragility of the public finances’\nLink copied to clipboard\nThe latest rise in government borrowing highlights the vulnerable state of public finances ahead of the Autumn Budget, an economist has warned.\nJulian Jessop, an independent economist and IEA economics fellow, said: “The latest official data highlight the fragility of the public finances as John Healey prepares his first Budget. Government borrowing was £2.3bn above forecast in July, and £0.7bn higher than in the same month a year ago.\n“Most worryingly, spending growth – notably on benefits – outpaced revenues despite a bumper month for income tax receipts.”\nAnother reminder of the fragility of the public finances... 🙄\nGovernment borrowing was £2.3bn above forecast in July, and £0.7bn higher than the same month a year ago.\nMost worryingly, spending growth - notably on benefits - outpaced revenues despite a bumper month for income…\nDespite this, consumer confidence in the UK rose to its highest level in two years, according to GfK, as the national mood improved after Andy Burnham arrived into No 10.\nHowever, Mr Jessop warned the current ‘Burnham bounce’ which has helped the economy this summer is unlikely to survive a “tax-and-spend Budget”.\nChancellor expected to focus on low-cost measures in Budget\nLink copied to clipboard\nThe Chancellor is expected to focus on inexpensive measures to address Britons worries about climbing energy bills and rising inflation as government borrowing costs rise.\nMatt Swannell, chief economic adviser to the ITEM Club, said: “There’s a good chance the Office for Budget Responsibility will revise up its medium-term borrowing forecasts at the Budget. Gilt yields rose this week, as an increase in oil prices stoked inflationary concerns.”\nHe added that the rise in borrowing costs will limit John Healey’s room for manoeuvre as he prepares for the Budget on Oct 28.\nMr Swannell said: “We think that the government will likely continue to follow its playbook since Andy Burnham became Prime Minister, with a focus on low-cost measures to address the cost of living, such as the announced cap on bus fares and upcoming suspension of VAT on electricity bills.”\nHe added that any larger and more costly policies would require spending cuts or tax rises.\nFinancial markets will turn their attention to the Autumn Budget in the coming weeks, but the challenge facing the nation’s public finances remains the same as last year, an economist has warned.\nJake Finney, senior economist at PwC UK, said: “The Prime Minister may have changed, but the fiscal arithmetic has not. The same difficult trade-offs remain, including the unresolved question of how to pay for higher defence spending.”\nHe added that despite the change in leadership financial markets had remained relatively calm.\nMr Finney said: “While there have been some additional spending commitments in recent weeks, these have been small compared with the wider fiscal challenges. However, as we have seen in recent years, markets can become jittery when uncertainty over the public finances rises in the run-up to a Budget.”\nInvestors watching how seriously Burnham takes fiscal discipline\nLink copied to clipboard\nThis week’s rise in government borrowing costs has shone a spotlight on Andy Burnham’s approach to the nation’s public finances as it becomes more expensive for the government to increase borrowing.\nThe yield on 10 year gilts, as UK bonds are known, has climbed from 5pc on Monday to 5.04pc on Friday morning.\nRising debt costs pose a headache for John Healey, the Chancellor, who is preparing to unveil his maiden Budget on Oct 28.\nThomas Pugh, chief economist at RSM UK, said: “Investors are watching every borrowing number for clues about how seriously the new government takes fiscal discipline. That means borrowing more is becoming an increasingly expensive way to solve the government’s problems.”\nHe has forecast that government borrowing will overshoot forecasts from the Office for Budget Responsibility, the UK’s fiscal watchdog, as spending continues to rise.\nMr Pugh added: “Higher gilt yields, stubborn inflation, and a government determined to spend more means borrowing is on course to remain above 4pc of GDP this year, instead of falling to 3.6pc as projected. That risks keeping gilt yields higher for longer and making every pound the government borrows more expensive.”\nRising government borrowing and the economic impact of the Iran war has vastly reduced the Chancellor’s fiscal headroom, the Resolution Foundation has warned.\nEconomists at the left-leaning think tank warned that the headroom against John Healey’s fiscal rules - self-imposed guardrails to guide public spending - has shrunk from around £24bn in the Spring Statement to less than £8bn today.\nMarket turmoil from the Iran war has continued to put pressure on public finances in recent days.\nElliott Christensen, senior economist at the Resolution Foundation, said: “Stronger growth in the first half of the year contributed to strong tax receipts in July. But it has failed to deliver a wider public finances windfall as the economic impact of conflict in the Middle East has taken its toll on borrowing costs.\n“The Chancellor’s margin against his fiscal rules is now razor thin, with the healthy headroom of around £24 billion last spring likely to have fallen below £8bn.”\nMr Christensen called on the Chancellor to use his first Budget on October 28 to put the nation’s finances on a more sustainable path and ensure that any new policy announcements are fully funded.\nLast month’s decline in retail sales comes as households across the UK brace for rising prices and pressure on their incomes, according to Capital Economics.\nThey added that July’s worrying public finance figures show the the Chancellor has “limited scope” for further borrowing.\nAshley Webb, senior UK economist at Capital Economics, said: “July’s fall in retail sales volumes is probably a sign of things to come as the drag on households’ incomes from high inflation is set to intensify.\n“With CPI inflation and unemployment yet to peak, we doubt any rebound in retail spending will last.”\nRetail sales were down in July despite the warm weather and the World Cup, official figures show.\nThe Office for National Statistics said physical and online sales volumes dipped 0.5pc last month.\nIt was partly due to non-food sales dropping, after retailers brought forward summer discounts to capitalise on hot weather in June. Sales rose 1pc that month.\nBy comparison, there were signs shops had “reduced promotions in July”.\nUnsurprisingly, however, one bright spot was alcoholic drinks and beverages which “performed well” as football fans enjoyed the football World Cup at home.\nRetail sales are estimated to have risen by 1.1% in the three months to July 2026 compared with the three months to April 2026.\nBritain’s FTSE 100 is up this morning — but investors are feeling “wary” about rising borrowing.\nThe blue chip index edged up 0.07pc as trading on the London Stock Exchange opened.\nSusannah Streeter, chief investment strategist at Wealth Club, warns that bond markets are nervy as tough headwinds blow on the global economy.\nInvestors are wary at the end of the week, amid a fresh surge in government borrowing costs, as efforts to intervene in bond markets by the Trump administration failed to have their desired effect.\nIt’s clear that consumers are less confident, reining in purchases as higher bills land. The latest snapshot of retail sales showed, as expected, a drop in July, as the jump in the energy price cap pushed up household costs and shoppers grew weary under the intense heat.\nNice to have but not essential expenditure falls by the wayside when consumers are facing an uphill climb to stick to budgets.\nBorrowing in July is usually lower than expected thanks to bumper tax receipts from self-employed workers\nLast month, self-assessed receipts came to a record of £17.1bn, £1.7bn higher than a year ago, according to the Office for National Statistics.\nHowever, this was actually slightly below official forecasts.\nAt the same time, inheritance tax receipts also continue to rise. HMRC has collected £3.2bn so far this year, some £100m higher than over the same period in 2025.\nLabour ‘tapping credit card while bailiffs are at the door’\nLink copied to clipboard\nMel Stride, the Shadow Chancellor, has accused Labour of seeking to “tap the nation’s credit card while the bailiffs are at the door” amid calls for higher public spending.\nThe Conservative frontbencher warned that July’s borrowing figures would pile further pressure on the Government’s already-strained finances. He said:\nThe Office for National Statistics has confirmed the national debt is now almost £3 trillion, and borrowing is running ahead of OBR forecasts by £2.3 billion. That is over £100,000 of debt for every single household in Britain. And we are all paying for it, every day.\nWe spend more on just the interest of our soaring debt than we do on our defence, police, and prisons combined. We simply cannot afford the price of Labour. They already plan to borrow over a quarter of a trillion pounds more than the plans they inherited, tapping the nation’s credit card while the bailiffs are at the door. It is ordinary families, their children and grandchildren who are left to cover the bill.\nOnly the Conservatives have a credible plan to get the national debt down with £50 billion in spending cuts, starting with the welfare bill, and a Golden Economic Rule to ensure the majority of those savings are used to cut the deficit. That is the only way we can fix the public finances and build a stronger economy.\nMartin Beck at WPI Strategy has described this morning’s figures as an “unwelcome surprise for the government”.\nHe warned that a surge in borrowing costs triggered by the war in Iran and fears about debt would pile further pressure on the Chancellor:\nMany of the biggest pressures on public finances increasingly lie ahead.\nTen-year gilt yields are above 5pc, reflecting energy-related inflation concerns, and these will gradually feed through into a larger debt-interest bill as existing debt is refinanced. At the same time, the government faces pressure to loosen inherited spending plans, meet unfunded defence commitments and deliver on its own ambitions for housing, infrastructure and public services.\nBritain already spends more than £100bn a year on debt interest.\nChancellor: Fiscal displine ‘bedrock of UK economy’\nLink copied to clipboard\nJohn Healey, the Chancellor, has just responded to this morning’s figures.\nIn a statement released by the Treasury, he says:\nFiscal discipline is the bedrock of our UK economic stability and national security which is why we are committed to meeting our fiscal rules, with a buffer against global uncertainties.\nWe are cutting the deficit faster than any other G7 economy, while giving people a bit of breathing space with cost of living pressures and focusing support to get young people into work.\nGovernment borrowing in July was £2.3bn above official forecasts, piling pressure on the public finances, according to PwC economist Jake Finney.\nHowever, he says the totals for this year compared to earlier predictions by the Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR) are still “relatively modest” considering they pre-date the Iran war.\nIt means John Healey, the Chancellor, faces tricky arithmetic ahead of October’s Budget.\nMr Finney adds:\nAttention will soon turn to the Budget, when we will get more clarity on the government’s fiscal plans. The Prime Minister may have changed, but the fiscal arithmetic has not. The same difficult trade-offs remain, including the unresolved question of how to pay for higher defence spending.\nFinancial markets have so far been relatively calm about the change in leadership. While there have been some additional spending commitments in recent weeks, these have been small compared with the wider fiscal challenges. However, as we have seen in recent years, markets can become jittery when uncertainty over the public finances rises in the run-up to a Budget.",
        "content": "John Healey, the Chancellor, faces a tax-raising Budget in October\nBritain’s soaring welfare bill forced John Healey to borrow more to balance the books last month.\nThe gap between tax receipts and spending unexpectedly resulted in a £1.8bn deficit in July, taking the national debt to within a whisker of £3tn.\nEconomists had predicted the Government would borrow nothing and blamed the deficit on t...",
