        "title": "Congo gambles on ‘old’ vaccine as new Ebola virus spirals",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/global-health/science-and-disease/congo-vaccine-old-ebola-virus-drc-bundibugyo/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473265+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/global-health/2026/08/21/TELEMMGLPICT000498928444_17873052658610_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqmKt-552QW7TVLc9f7CG2xMJxPIt4h66fwuAE1mh1wGA.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "  \n  \n\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\n  \n  \n    Nearly half of all those infected have gone on to die.\nDr Sania Nishtar, chief executive of the Gavi global vaccine partnership, earlier this week said to beat the outbreak “we must deploy every tool at our disposal”.\nShe went on: “To avoid this becoming the worst recorded Ebola outbreak in history, it is critical that all effective countermeasure options are brought to bear.\n“We have a tool that could help save lives while we wait for [Bundibugyo]-specific vaccines to become available.”\nPrevious clinical trials have shown that Ervebo is safe, but its effect on other strains is unknown.\nThe World Health Organisation said in a statement that while it was not known if Ervebo may protect against the Bundibugyo virus, “early laboratory and animal data suggest it may provide some protection”.\nMedics told the Telegraph of anecdotal claims that health workers who had been given Ervebo to protect them during an outbreak of the Zaire strain in 2018 to 2020 had not fallen ill during the current Bundibugyo outbreak.\nHealth workers are at extreme risk of catching the disease because they come into contact with highly-contagious blood, vomit and diarrhoea from dying patients. When they fall sick themselves, they frequently go on to infect their own families.\nGiving health workers vaccine jabs to protect them as they tend the sick is a cornerstone of Ebola responses.\nThe WHO said the vaccine allocation includes 20,000 doses for a Phase 3 clinical trial to understand the impact of the vaccine on the Bundibugyo virus. The remaining 50,000 doses will be used for front-line and health workers as recommended by WHO experts.\nDr Nishtar said: “Having already been licensed for the Zaire strain, we know that Ervebo is proven to be safe.\n“And while the signs are that it may not stop transmission – which is essential for ending the outbreak - it could well save precious lives and reduce suffering.”\nHealth officials warn that the Bundibugyo outbreak centred on DRC’s Ituri province is easily outpacing relief efforts.\nThe virus is thought to have been spreading for months before an outbreak was finally declared in mid-May and medics have been unable to make up the head start.\nAn analysis by the Africa Centres for Disease Control meanwhile warned the current outbreak dwarfed all others on record\nThe current outbreak had almost ten times more cases than any other previous outbreak at a similar stage.  \nProtect yourself and your family by learning more about Global Health Security\n  \n ",
