        "title": "Officials selling yellow fever certificates to unvaccinated travellers",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/global-health/science-and-disease/officials-selling-yellow-fever-certificates-to-unvaccinated/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473261+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/global-health/2026/08/21/TELEMMGLPICT000499018783_17873097330610_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqufPWexoDNO0wvV3FQMbBIEljCEHhppWeqFAzBrNtD18.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Standard",
        "full_content": "Officials in Malawi are selling yellow fever certificates to unvaccinated travellers for around $20 and sending them on their way, a Telegraph investigation has found.\nThe passport-sized, yellow document, officially called the International Certificate of Vaccination or Prophylaxis (ICVP), is supposed to provide proof of vaccination against yellow fever, a mosquito-borne virus that kills tens of thousands of people every year.\nThe jab provides lifelong protection, beginning 10 days after vaccination. Most countries in Africa and Latin America require them for entry, either for all travellers or for those arriving from risk areas.\nYet travellers leaving Malawi through two major airports can get hold of the certificates without ever being vaccinated, according to interviews with multiple travellers and an undercover investigation.\nSeveral passengers who travelled recently from Malawi to destinations including Uganda, Kenya, Ethiopia and China said they had obtained yellow fever vaccination certificates at airports or public hospitals without receiving the vaccine.\nOne traveller, who recently flew to Uganda for work from Kamuzu International Airport, which serves the capital, Lilongwe, said he was initially told it was too late to get vaccinated because his departure was less than 10 days away.\n“When I asked the health officials if I could get vaccinated at the airport, I was told it was too late because I needed to be vaccinated ten days before my trip,” he told The Telegraph.\n“But then they offered to arrange the certificate for me for roughly $21,” said the traveller, who asked not to be named. “I had to pay because I could not postpone the trip.”\nYellow fever kills tens of thousands of people a year and is spread by daytime biting mosquitoes\nCredit: Joao Paulo Burini/Moment RF\nThe traveller said officials took his personal details after he paid the money and, within minutes, produced a completed and signed vaccination certificate falsely indicating he had received the vaccine.\nThe document was accepted when he arrived at Entebbe International Airport in Uganda, he said.\nTwo other travellers said the practice was well known amongst frequent flyers.\n“You just have to choose whether you want to get it at the airport or at a public hospital, as long as you have the cash,” one traveller said. “There are people who will guide you on what to do.”\nA Telegraph journalist went undercover at Kamuzu International Airport, posing as a tourist travelling to Kenya.\nHe was stopped by health officials who enquired about our yellow fever vaccination status, asking to see an ICVP.\nAfter explaining that he did not have a certificate, he was offered the opportunity to buy one for approximately $21 or else be stopped from boarding the flight.\n“It won’t take long to process. As long as you pay the money, you will have no problems with officials at your destination airport,” one health official told the reporter.\nThe official then directed the reporter to hand the money to a colleague.\nThe Telegraph subsequently obtained a completed yellow fever vaccination certificate with an official Ministry of Health stamp and signature of the vaccinator – despite never receiving the vaccine.\nA yellow fever vaccination certificate that The Telegraph was able to obtain at Kamuzu Airport\nCredit: Henry Mhango\nA second undercover operation at Chileka International Airport in Blantyre also resulted in the acquisition of a yellow fever vaccination certificate without the undercover journalist receiving the vaccine.\nSeveral other travellers claimed to have obtained certificates without being vaccinated in public hospitals including Mzuzu referral hospital, Lilongwe District Hospital, and Queen Elizabeth Central hospital in Blantyre.\n“I got mine at Queen Elizabeth Central Hospital for my trip to China,” said one of the travellers, who asked to remain anonymous.\nAnother passenger said she bought her vaccination certificate at Lilongwe District Hospital for a trip to Kenya, while another reported that he obtained his copy of the certificate at Mzuzu central hospital for his trip to Addis Ababa.\nThe scale of the abuse of the certificates is unclear, but one health official, who asked not to be named, claimed that as many as 80 per cent of passengers travelling through the airport to risk countries obtain certificates at the airport or through public hospitals without being vaccinated.\nThe Telegraph was unable to independently verify that figure.\nThe allegations of widespread falsification of yellow fever certificates are particularly significant because the disease remains a serious public health threat, especially in Africa.\nYellow fever causes between 31,000 and 82,000 deaths each year globally, as well as between 67,000 to 173,000 severe infections, according to the World Health Organization (WHO) and public health models.\nUp to half of those who develop severe yellow fever symptoms, notably jaundice and internal bleeding, will die within 7 to 10 days.\nThe virus is spread by daytime biting mosquitoes and remains endemic in over 40 countries, but the vast majority of the burden of disease is borne by Africa.\nThe yellow fever jab uses a weakened live virus and gives lifetime protection.\nThe allegations raise serious questions about the integrity of an international health safeguard designed to prevent the spread of yellow fever across borders.\n“If unvaccinated travellers are able to obtain vaccination certificates fraudulently, this poses a serious public health risk,” said Dr Chisomo Kanthanga, a Malawian medical microbiologist specialising in infectious diseases.\n“Fraudulent vaccination certificates create a false sense of security within public health surveillance systems. They could allow infected travellers to cross borders undetected, delay the identification of imported cases and increase the risk of transmission in populations with low immunity.”\nDr Kanthanga added: “For Malawi, the alleged sale or improper issuance of vaccination certificates raises particular concerns because the country has mosquito species capable of transmitting yellow fever, even though it is not currently considered endemic for the disease.\n“If an infected traveller were to enter an area with competent mosquito vectors and low population immunity, local transmission could potentially occur.”\nHis concerns are echoed by the WHO, which has warned that fraudulent or improperly issued yellow fever vaccination can undermine international health measures intended to prevent the spread of the disease.\nPassengers queue for the check on their yellow fever vaccination status at Kamuzu Airport\nCredit: Henry Mhango\nCollins Boakye-Agyemang, a spokesman for the WHO Regional Office for Africa, told The Telegraph that fraudulent certificates represent a significant vulnerability.\n“WHO reports, independent studies and several newspaper reports consistently link fraudulent or improperly issued yellow fever vaccination certificates to increased vulnerability and transmission risk,” he told The Telegraph.\nMr Boakye-Agyemang also said that fraudulent certificates could undermine a key element of the International Health Regulations, which WHO member states sign up to, by allowing unvaccinated travellers to cross borders without appropriate protection.\n“They neutralise a cornerstone IHR intervention, enable silent transmission across borders and undermine trust and compliance in the international system,” he said.\nHe also warned that the practice could create epidemiological risks by allowing unvaccinated travellers to move freely between countries, potentially increasing the risk of yellow fever being introduced into populations with little or no immunity.\nThe WHO has called for stronger systems for verifying vaccination status and has highlighted the move towards digital and electronically verifiable vaccination certificates as one way to reduce fraud.\n“Some countries like Zimbabwe and Nigeria are transitioning from the high-fraud-risk paper-based ICVP systems towards digital, verifiable and interoperable vaccination certification systems,” Mr Boakye-Agyemang said.\n“Several countries are not yet fully digital, but are moving towards hybrid systems, with Kenya, Ghana and South Africa using online pre-registration and physical issuance of vaccination cards.”\nA spokesman for Kamuzu International Airport claimed the officers responsible for the checking of yellow fever vaccination status work under the ministry of health.\nA spokesman for the health ministry denied that its personnel were involved in the distribution of fraudulent yellow fever certificates.\nBenedicto Mbewe, the ministry’s spokesperson, told The Telegraph: “The ministry is not aware of such [activities].”",
        "content": "Officials in Malawi are selling yellow fever certificates to unvaccinated travellers for around $20 and sending them on their way, a Telegraph investigation has found.\nThe passport-sized, yellow document, officially called the International Certificate of Vaccination or Prophylaxis (ICVP), is supposed to provide proof of vaccination against yellow fever, a mosquito-borne virus that kills tens of t...",
