        "title": "Root delivers perfect riposte to captaincy doubters as England hammer Pakistan",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/cricket/2026/08/21/england-vs-pakistan-live-score-latest-update-first-test-day/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473258+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/cricket/2026/08/21/TELEMMGLPICT000499051504_17873237826970_trans_NvBQzQNjv4Bq1bMxp-5f6dqJCl5Loy_W3TPjczfazQI7RI4Vi4hrk7g.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Live",
        "full_content": "Joe Root celebrates a dominant victory on his return to the England captaincy\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nThere was no attempt to be a Ben Stokes tribute act by Joe Root, just a measured captaincy performance very much in his own undemonstrative style.\nRoot, ever the team man, pushed his bowlers forward to lead England off at the end, recognising the match-winners in a walkover against Pakistan, who were bowled out for 135 in under two sessions.\nVictory by an innings and 103 runs inside three days marked a perfect return to the job and put the smile back on England faces for the first time in a long while. The chants of “F--- the ECB” from Trent Bridge, after the New Zealand regime-toppling loss, were replaced by the Yorkshire faithful’s “Roooot”.\nThere was plenty of scepticism around rebooting the Root captaincy, but he was the only realistic choice. Harry Brook’s batting here was more sensible and at a tempo in the captain’s mould. “He is still learning the art of Test cricket,” Root said. Thrusting Brook into the job would have put more pressure on him, he may well have felt he needed to make risky statements with the bat. Now he can mature under Root and Stephen Fleming.\nTactically, Root was astute and showed enough to suggest he is going to be different this time. The old Root would not have whipped Jofra Archer off after two overs of the Test, but it was a prod that worked as he bowled quicker and more accurately to strike twice with the second-innings new ball, using his new-found skills with a scrambled seam to good effect.\nIt also showed his bowlers he wanted a fuller length and, from that first hour, they controlled the match and did not let their grip slip.\nRoot promised to keep the good aspects of Bazball and apply common sense. He continued Stokes’s leg-slip tactic and it paid off with the wicket of opener Azan Awais, and urged Ollie Robinson to bowl with the keeper standing up. England batted at 4.5 an over – a touch quicker than the Bazball years – but it was never reckless, just a calm tempo, with no ramps or scoops.\nWhereas Stokes could be showy in the field, there was no funkiness or snap declaration. To be fair, there was no need. Pakistan were so poor that England only had to apply the basics for long enough to win. It is hard to draw any significant conclusions when England take a wicket every four and a bit overs, as Pakistan proved Scyld Berry’s view that this team is their worst for 50 years.\nThere were positives everywhere. The bowlers combined as an attack, relentlessly probing with the wobble seam and there were no weak links. Jordan Cox made an encouraging start at No 3 as much for his mature presence as well as 73 runs, while Brook batted within himself for once to make 91. Emilio Gay will need a score to stay in the side, but Cox’s emergence, with Jacob Bethell to come back, hands England top-order options for once.\nHarry Brook was one of a number of England batsmen to make runs without scoring a hundred\nCredit: Lee Smith/Reuters\nEngland batted with more precision and care in this Test than against New Zealand and insiders say Fleming is already making his presence felt from across the other side of the world. He is in daily contact with Root, working on their vision for the team. Mike Hussey is expected to join as batting coach, adding heft to the backroom and knowledge of the Australian side for the Ashes. England already look a more serious outfit.\nRobinson was superb from his wicket-taking first ball of the Test, finishing with eight for 80 in the match and has 15 in two games since his return. He exposed the folly in not picking him at Trent Bridge. Archer was quick and hostile, and could be let off the leash with runs in the bank and bowlers at the other end keeping it tight. Gus Atkinson will bowl worse in the future and take more wickets than his one for 11 from eight overs, but he was accurate and deserved more. Josh Tongue’s potent wicket-taking ability returned, mopping up the tail in the first innings and making crucial breakthroughs in the second for a match haul of eight for 89. He had a quiet series against New Zealand, but winning the Hundred appears to have refreshed him.\nArcher and Tongue’s pace and the wiles and accuracy of Robinson and Atkinson is a potent combination. “They don’t know how good they are just yet,” Root said. “But the more they perform like that they will very quickly realise and prove to themselves what they can do at this level,” Root said. They are emerging from the shadows of Anderson, Broad, Woakes and Stokes.\nAfter a little fun from Archer with the bat in the first half hour, England were bowled out for 409, a 238-run lead and a three-day finish a real prospect.\nArcher’s pace was too hot for Imam-ul-Haq; a short ball aimed at the body was gloved to Jamie Smith for nought. Root stationed a leg slip for Azan Awais to encourage Archer to bowl straighter and five balls later he popped a catch straight down his throat.\nRobinson tested Abdullah Shafique with a full length outside off and he nicked to slip. Root had another slap on the back when he moved Dan Lawrence to an unorthodox deepish square leg because he could see Saud Shakeel was falling over to the off side and vulnerable to one on his legs. He clipped Tongue straight to Lawrence.\nWhen Shan Masood edged to first slip after lunch, day-four tickets were redundant. Tongue was on a hat-trick for the second time in the match but failed to convert. It did not matter. Three balls later he thumped one into Salman Ali Agha’s pads. It looked high, but he was stuck on the crease and ball-tracking returned three red lights.\nRobinson tucked into the tail with two in two overs and Atkinson had his deserved wicket when he bowled Mohammad Rizwan having a slog. Archer finished it off and Root double-fist pumped at the winning moment, a feeling he thought had gone for ever. This was his first Test win as captain since Headingley 2021, and only Robinson survives from that match. You would have got decent odds a few weeks ago on those two being captain and match-winner in a Headingley Test again.\nRobinson: Six weeks of hard work got me fit\nOllie Robinson credited six weeks of hard graft on his much-discussed fitness after claiming his second man-of-the-match award in two Tests this summer.\nRobinson became the first bowler in Test history to take a wicket with the first ball of a series in England, and finished with match figures of eight for 80 as England thumped Pakistan by an innings and 103 runs. This followed another man-of-the-match display at Lord’s in June, when he took seven for 77 in the win over New Zealand at Lord’s.\nBetween times, he missed the Test at the Oval with a minor knee injury, then was overlooked as England sought “greater air-speed” on a flat pitch in hot conditions at Trent Bridge. He was not playing in the Hundred, so turned out in four MetroBank One-Day Cup matches for Sussex, and became a father for the second time in the days before the first Test against Pakistan.\nRobinson’s fitness has been a theme of his five-year Test career, and he spent two years in exile after the latest of a string of mid-match injuries in Ranchi in 2024.\n“It’s really good,” he said of his performance at Headingley. “I had a hard six weeks before this, played a few one-day games, but I was working with the ECB medical team on getting as fit as I could. I felt like today, coming back again for another spell up the hill, I maintained my speeds, maintained my skill and that’s what I’m looking to do. There’s still a lot of work to do, I’m not there yet, but it’s a pleasing start.\n“I’m just trying to be as consistent as possible and tweak my skill. I’ve got guys at the other end bowling 90mph, which is always a nice place to be. I feel the batters are coming harder at me because of it and it brings me into the game more. We complement each other really well.”\nOllie Robinson celebrates one of his eight wickets in the opening Test at Headingley\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nCaptain Joe Root was full of praise for Robinson’s ability to maintain his performance levels as the – albeit short – match wore on. Robinson bowled 18 overs in the first innings and 11 in the second, more than any other bowler.\n“I thought his performance out there was brilliant, and the fact he was still as effective in the second innings having bowled two pretty long spells in the first innings,” Root said. “Understanding how is he going to operate and what his requirements are in different phases of the game is important. It will be great to see him pull up really well ahead of Lord’s, and back that up again.”\nAnd finally, Pakistan stand-in captain Salman Ali Agha speaks to Mark Butcher\nLink copied to clipboard\nEngland have outplayed us and that is fair to say. We have made many mistakes. Our batting is inexperienced in these conditions but they need to be consistent. There was moisture when we bowled but our bowlers did not make the most of it. [Is your attack too samey?] We do have bowlers who can click 140kph but these three fast bowlers had done well in West Indies and these conditions suit them. I still back them.\nWe need to eradicate mistakes and come back stronger in the next match. I am very happy for Ali Usman because of the way he bowled in these conditions. Also I’m very happy for how Abdullah Shafique batted coming back into the side. Hopefully Babar will come back in at Lord’s.\nA brilliant performance. You look at the prep we had coming in [the Hundred finishing only on Sunday], the fact that we are in a transition stage. The clarity we showed this week in how we approached this match was outstanding. To put in a performance like that is really enjoyable. The bowlers found the right areas and once we got ahead in the game, we showed maturity on how to manage the conditions.\nIt is very exciting. I think they don’t really know how good they are and what they’re capable of. That’s a great place to be when they out in performances like that. The variety that these bowlers bring and the fact they bowled very well in partnerships. Really good stuff in that department.\nWith the bat in unfriendly conditions we found a really good tempo with Jordan Cox coming in at three and playing with such control and calm nature. Dan [Lawrence] coming in at six and getting the score he did, Brooky showing why he’s the best player in the world. Great way to start the series.\n[What pleased me most was] the fact we played what was in front of us [rather than having a preplanned strategy of running towards danger]. The more we can roll that out… it will look different every game and the more we understand that [that they have to be flexible in their approach] and being skilful enough to deliver it [we will get to where we ant to be].\nLord’s will be a different challenge. [How will you balance the team at Lord’s and play a frontline spinner without Ben Stokes to balance the XI] Let’s see what [type of pitch] we get down there and the good thing is that we have time. Let’s enjoy a brilliant performance and ready ourselves for Lord’s when we get there.\nI love playing for England and batting for England, that’s never going to waver. Very disappointed with my dismissal. I was set and in and to put that on Dan and Brooky having already lost Jordan wasn’t great example setting by me but the way they responded was even more pleasing for us as a group.\nOllie Robinson speaks to Mark Butcher of Sky Sports\nLink copied to clipboard\nIt was a great way to start by taking wicket on the first ball. I was just trying to be consistent because I was not feeling rhythm. It was a good wicket to bowl on as it was nipping both ways. I had a hard six weeks before this and was working with the ECB medical team on my fitness. So that has helped me maintain my speeds throughout both innings. I am still not there yet, but this was a pleasing start. [Will you be OK for Lord’s?] Yes, I’ll go back to the newborn [a daughter born last Thursday] and try to get as much sleep as I can.\nEngland have won the first Test of Joe Root’s second stint as full-time Test captain convincingly, their seam attack to the fore with Tongue and Robinson taking eight apiece in the match, Archer three and Atkinson one. Pakistan did not look undercooked so much as having a bowling attack without variety or penetrative consistency and batsmen who were far too loose in their technique.\nAbbas c Lawrence b Archer 6 Abbas decides to have some fun and backs away to leg to have a swing, slashing the first ball over the slips for four, swishing and missing the next then slices the third high to point while aiming for cow corner and Lawrence waits for it with hungry hands. FOW 135 all out.\nRizwan b Atkinson 41 Rizwan decides to play some shots, tries to slog sweep an 85mph delivery and loses his off stump. Atkinson, who bowled well with no reward after his first spell on Wednesday, takes his first wicket of the match. FOW 131/9\nNow Rizwan after some sober marshalling of Abbas, takes a single off the first ball of Robinson’s over. Robinson beats the Abbas edge as he pushes forward to four balls in succession.\nAtkinson replaces Tongue and starts with one that’s too wide and full. Rizwan strides across and drills it down to the cover sweeper for two. After defending and then playing and missing, Rizwan takes a single off the fourth ball with a Harrow drive which squirts off the inside edge down to long leg. Abbas doesn’t have to play at his two.\nMohammad Abbas, a cheerful soul, blocks out a Robinson maiden and smiles approvingly when the bowler manipulates one back into him, as if on a string. Game knows game.\nAbbas drives the first ball down to long off and Rizwan reluctantly walks a single. He genuinely didn’t want to take strike, though and hung back until Abbas was three-quarters of the way down.\nThe keeper-batsman then finds the sense to drive for two through cover and then pulls hard and low, collaring the bouncer behind square for four. He’s so talented that it beggars belief that he isn’t farming the strike. He follows that with the most elegant of on-drives for four more. Tongue hits him on the fleshy part of the thigh (hello Sopranos fans) with the last ball that nips-back into his back leg which means Abbas will be on strike for Robinson.\nRobinson strikes on Nelson. To elaborate on the ‘dozy’ or ‘negligent’ conundrum. I think the board lurches from short-term fix to short-term fix so wildly and so frequently scapegoats players that it is understandable that they play for themselves first and the team second. It is the board’s policy and meddling that have led to such corrosion.\nAbbas plays tip and run to cover and then Rizwan takes two off the last ball to expose Abbas to Tongue. Talk about ignoring a lesson just learnt.\nThat rather sums it up for Pakistan. What on earth is Rizwan doing scurrying two off the last ball of the over, exposing Abbas to Tongue? Farm the strike!\nKhurram c Cox b Robinson 8 Rizwan must be a red-inker. Here he takes a single off the first ball with an eye on the not out and Khurram edges the very next ball to third slip. Pakistan’s cricket has been at best dozy, at worst positively negligent throughout the match. The set-up and the politicking that has ruined it needs root and branch reform. FOW 111/8\nAlthough Archer seemed to be stretching, Root is sticking with Tongue. Rizwan flicks a full, straight one off middle through long leg for four, works the next in front fo square for two and takes a single from the third to mid-on. Nasser Hussain thinks he’s gone too soon and should not be exposing Khurram to three balls from Tongue.\nAnd Tongue duly finds Khurram’s edge twice in three balls, nicking them in the same gap between Cox at third slip and Lawrence at gully for two fours.\nRobinson almost has another when he nips one back into Khurram who thinks it was going to straighten, misses it and the ball misses off stump by a gnat’s whisker. Wicket maiden.\nAli Usman c Cox b Robinson 7 Robinson strikes with the first ball of a spell again! He has the keeper up and he provides the assist. Usman pushes forward, nicks the ball which cannons into Smith’s thigh then loops to his right. Cox launches himself into a headlong dive at third slip and scoops his hands underneath it. FOW 95/7\nTongue tries to york Rizwan but serves up a half-volley by mistake and Rizwan flicks it off his pads for a single. England decide to take a punt on a review with three to use when Tongue pins Usman on the back thighpad with his nip-backer but Smith should have known it was outside the line. Over the past two years I am yet to be convinced that Smith is a good judge. If he is then he is not assertive enough.\nArcher bounces Usman who is so shocked by it that he neglects to play at it or take effective evasive action, wearing it painfully on the shoulder. As the second part of the old two-card trick, Archer follows it up with a tempter outside off, seeking the edge, but it’s too wide and Usman reaches over and steers it wide of gully for four. It’s only his third Test at the age of 32 but Ali Usman does have a first-class century and five fifties to his name.\nRizwan may have scored the last of his three Test centuries two years ago (to the day) but he can certainly still bat and times the pants off a straight drive that races past Tongue for four. He does miss out on a freebie off a short ball on leg stump, though, swishing at thin air.\nArcher beats Usman four times in succession with screeching deliveries that whistle past the right-hander’s outside edge as he plays at them as if expecting them to come back in on the angle. “Too good for thee” as Fred Trueman used to say.\nUsman gets off the mark and off a pair by working a straight one through square leg for a single. Rizwan plays tip and run towards cover as England attack with three slips a gully, a leg slip and short leg, leaving plenty of gaps. Usman also takes advantage of the spaces in front of square by running a single off a forward defensive.\nRizwan pushes hard outside off stump and edges Archer down through the slips for four. It’s the only injury in the over to Archer’s figures as the crowd prepares tp roar Tongue on from the other end. Not often Archer has to play a supporting role.\nTongue was thought to be world-class against right-handers and Ilford seconds-class against left-handers has picked up two in two balls, one at the end of his previous over. The ball that did for Masood did straighten a bit.\nRizwan flicks the hat-trick ball off middle through midwicket for three putting Salman on strike and Tongue sends him packing, too. He has three for seven off two overs.\nMasood c Duckett b Tongue 29 Angling in to the left-hander from round the wicket and the well-set Durham graduate pushes at it with hard hands and nicks off to first slip. FOW 73/5\nArcher loses his grip as he tries to scramble the seam and loops down a gentle full toss on Salman Agha’s legs. The captain flogs it through midwicket for four.\nTongue starts round the wicket to these two left-handers and bangs the first one in, surprising Saud who fends it off his ribs in a but of a flap but the ball lands safe. The leg slip who did for Awais is not there for Tongue. When Tongue goes fuller, Saud shows his class with another creamy on-drive for four. But he departs two balls later with a lazy flick, seemingly unaware that Lawrence had been posted halfway to the boundary behind square, more long leg than fine leg in terms of the angle of his position.\nSaud Shakeel c Lawrence b Tongue 14 Seemed to be a juicy half-volley on middle and Saud clipped it hard round the corner and chipped it straight to Dan Lawrence standing in an unusual position at backward square. Tongue has a happy knack of wicket-taking with ordinary deliveries. Beefy-esque. FOW 68/4\nGood afternoon. Jofra Archer is given the first over after lunch after his blistering spell this morning. Saud, who averages nearly 44 in 23 Tests, plays a handsome on-drive off the second ball, high elbow, the bat barely through the perpendicular. Archer is bowling fast and full so far and Saud is meeting the ball from over and round the wicket, as Archer switches approaches, with a straight bat, pushing a single to cover to take the strike.\nTongue, who took his fourth Test five-for in the first innings, hasn’t been used so far but gets the honours after lunch.\nEngland moved towards victory, three wickets with the new ball and a large first innings deficit still to knock off for Pakistan. Archer bowled quickly and struck twice in five overs. He was expensive but with so many runs to play with the crucial aspect was his wickets. Robinson on the money along with Atkinson which gives Archer the freedom to crank up the pace. Pakistan batsmen, unused to such bounce and pace, looked uncomfortable against Archer.\nShan Masood is the vital wicket, he could hang around and take the game into day four. Otherwise, England will be sniffing a three-day win.\nAtkinson, who has been very economical thus far, will send down the final over before the break. Masood is content to see out the over and he makes it to lunch unbeaten on 28.\nPakistan head into the interval at 59/3, still 179 runs behind.\nThat is it from me. I will hand you over to the very capable hands of Rob Bagchi, who will take you through the rest of the day.\nSpin is brought on as Dan Lawrence is into the attack. Is that a way of making sure England get two more overs before lunch? Will the Pakistan batsmen be willing to take a risk against Lawrence just before the interval? The answer seemingly is no as Shakeel is satisfied to mostly block out the over.\nThe TV cameras have just picked up Sir Gareth Southgate in attendance at Headingley today. Interestingly, we are still yet to see Josh Tongue bowl in this second innings.\nAtkinson sends down his second straight maiden.\nA former England manager watching on\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nSaud Shakeel is in at number five and is off the mark first ball with a single down to fine leg.\nThe thing about this Pakistan batting lineup is that these are not rookies. Of the top seven, only the rookie opener Azan is new to Test cricket. The rest are seasoned players. Two of them should be able to stick in and make a big partnership, and push this game into tomorrow. So far, it’s not happening, though.\nShafique c Brook b Robinson 15 Robinson took five wickets in the first and now has his first in the second. Robinson finds the outside edge of Shafique’s bat and Brook takes the catch at second slip. That pair was starting to build a partnership but that is now at an end. FOW 51/3\nArcher’s opening spell is indeed at an end as he is replaced by Robinson, who has now switched ends. Josh Tongue not yet brought into the attack. Robinson has summoned Smith up to the stumps. There is also a short mid-off in place.\nRobinson offers up too much width and Masood tucks into it by easily cutting away in front of square for four. You can repeat that off the final ball as it is the same as the first boundary.\nPakistan still trail by 192 runs.\nPakistan need Shan Masood to score a lot of runs to keep them alive in this Test\nCredit: PA/Mike Egerton\nAtkinson strikes Shafique on the pads but umpire Allahudien Paleker shakes his head. England never looked convinced of reviewing it and Root indeed does not send it upstairs. It was a good decision not to review as the ball was missing the stumps by quite some way.\nArcher is going to continue into a fifth over, despite being a tad expensive. His economy rate might be on the high side but he has taken both wickets and posed a real threat. The over costs just two runs. Will that be the end of Archer’s spell?\n...and Stokes is out, for 53. He was caught and bowled by the nibbly Anuj Dal of Derbyshire. Lots of low scores across the country, so Durham’s position of 182 for five is perhaps not too bad.\nThe first change of bowling sees Gus Atkinson replace Robinson and he immediately beats Masood’s outside edge. Atkinson begins with a tight, probing over that goes for just one run. No let-up for Pakistan.\nTime for drinks.\nI’ll admit to having half an eye on events at Derby, where Ben Stokes has a fifty in his first first-class innings since his Test retirement. I am fascinated by this final stage of Stokes’s career.\nThe runs are starting to flow for Pakistan. Archer bangs one in short outside off stump and Shafique upper cuts over the slip cordon for four. A bit of a gift really from Archer.\nArcher then over corrects and sends one onto the pads, which Shafique clips away for a couple.\nRobinson had kept is so tight and offered up no freebies up until this point. He strays onto the pads and Masood easily flicks away through square leg for four. He then gets another one on his pads that he clips away this time for a couple.\nArcher strays onto the pads and Masood flicks away through square leg for four. The next ball he runs well between the wickets by dropping into the offside and coming through for a quick single.\nShafique then gets off the mark with a boundary. His drive comes off the outside half of the bat but runs away through a vacant point region for four.\nAbdullah Shafique, who was by far Pakistan’s best batsman in the first innings, joins Masood in the middle with his side in real trouble. Archer bowls a beauty to him first up that just beats the outside edge.\nJofra Archer was taken off after two overs at the Football Stand End in the first innings. No danger of that this time. His last delivery was 91mph, but his line - far harder to leave than in the first innings - has been as important.\nAzan c Root b Archer 9 Azan has picked out the leg slip to absolute perfection. After his first boundary on English soil, Azan gets one on is pads and clips it straight to Root, who had positioned himself at slip. Great captaincy from Root, taking a leaf out of the Stokes captaincy book. FOW 13/2\nOff his ninth ball, Azan finally gets off the pair as he guides one behind point for three. Good fielding from Atkinson on the rope prevented the boundary. Azan will be a relieved man.\nThe slip cordon is packed with England well on top. This is tough for these Pakistan batsmen.\nShan Masood is the new man in and his experience playing in this country will be essential. He is off the mark first ball with a boundary. Archer bowls onto the pads and Masood flicks away fine for four.\nImam c Smith b Archer 0 Jofra Archer will open the bowling alongside Robinson and gets a wicket with his fifth ball. Imam tries to sway out of the way of a short delivery from Archer but does not drop his hands enough. It clips the glove and Smith takes an easy catch. Imam knows he has hit it and does not think about a review. The worst possible start for Pakistan. FOW 0/1\nJofra Archer wasn’t at his best in the first innings and seemed to struggle to readjust to the Test game after his Hundred exertions. But that wicket shows Archer at his most hostile best. Archer and Ollie Robinson have the makings of a terrific, complementary opening attack.\nAlready the breakthrough for England\nCredit: Mike Egerton/PA\nAs he did in the first innings, Ollie Robinson will start proceedings with the ball. Azan is not just on a pair but a king pair. He survives the first ball to get off a king pair but is still on a pair. He plays out a maiden from Robinson and is still not off the pair.\nDon’t we all want Pakistan to make a substantial second innings - to see what speed Ollie Robinson musters in his third and fourth spell?\nPoor Azan Awais. This is a painful pair to be sat on, with Ollie Robinson all over him like a cheap suit.\nAzan Awais and Imam-ul-Haq make their way out to the middle and the tourists have so much work ahead of them just to stay in this Test match. Azan will be hoping for a better start to this innings than the first having been dismissed off the first ball of the match. These are probably better batting conditions than they were two days ago when this pair came out to bat on day one.\nTongue b Khurram 9 Tongue drills Khurram down the ground for four but is then beaten by the Pakistan seamer and England’s innings is brought to a close. After the first innings, England lead Pakistan by 238 runs and the tourists will have to bat so much better than they did first time around. Ali Usman walks off with the ball in his hand after his five-for. FOW 409 all out\nWhat a lovely way to get off the mark! Tongue drills Abbas back over his head for four and that boundary brings up England’s 400. Abbas follows that up with a beauty which beats Tongue’s outside edge and the off stump.\nI reckon that Jofra Archer and Josh Tongue should ask the groundsman again, to cut up this second new ball, to commemorate their small but frolicsome tenth wicket stand - the finishing touches to England’s handsome first innings lead.\nJosh Tongue is the last man in and gets off strike courtesy of a leg bye. Considering the aggression he has already shown this morning, you would anticipate Archer will continue to be positive with Smith now out.\nThat is good running as Archer cuts away through point and quickly comes back for two. Archer ends the over with a fortuitous boundary. He tries to clip into the legside but a leading edge runs away quickly behind point for four.\nIt’s too easy to forget what a rounded cricketer Jofra Archer is. He was brought up in Barbados club cricket with all the high standards entailed. He always chases hard in the field, he knows how to wield a bat in orthodox fashion as well as a hitter, he runs hard between wickets (or he did just now), he is not just a tearaway quick who switches off when he is not bowling.\nSmith c Masood b Khurram 25 Pakistan have not started well particularly well this morning but they make the breakthrough. Despite the Pakistan seamers not looking a massive threat so far, Smith top edges an attempted pull shot and Masood pouches the catch at mid-on. FOW 392/9\nArcher perhaps got a little carried away with that six in the previous over and goes for an almighty hoick over the legside but misses by a mile.\nFrom the final ball of the over, Archer advances at Abbas and clubs him away through wide mid-on for four. Not the most elegant of shots and Ali perhaps should have stopped it but Archer gets four nonetheless.\nUsman bowled just the one over from the Kirkstall Lane End and has already been replaced by Khurram Shahzad. Archer has looked reasonably comfortable this morning; does that suggest this is a pretty good pitch to bat on or the lack of potency in the Pakistan attack? Maybe a bit of both.\nWhat a shot that is from Archer! On the swivel he pulls Khurram into the stands for the first six of the game. The connection on that shot was pretty pure.\nOne ball into Abbas’ second over of the day, Pakistan opt to take the new ball. A single off Smith’s bat out to deep backward point takes England’s lead up to the 200-run point.\nA couple of balls later, Archer clips one over mid-wicket’s head and the ball looked like it would run away for four but it holds up near the boundary rope and Archer comes through for three.\nEngland continue to build their lead\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nPerhaps this is the reason Pakistan have not taken the new ball as spinner Ali Usman, who was the first opposition spinner to take five wickets at Headingley since 1972, opens up alongside Abbas. Stuart Broad on the Sky commentary cannot understand why Pakistan have not taken the new ball.\nSmith clearly is trusting of Archer as he takes a single up to long off first ball. Three deliveries later, Archer gets off the mark with a single of his own up to long off.\nMuhammad Abbas will send down the first over of the day but Pakistan have decided not to take the new ball quite yet. The second ball of the day is a beauty that strikes Smith on the back pad but it would have been going over the stumps.\nSmith then gets the first run of the day via the inside edge out to deep square leg. Archer sees out the rest of the over so one run comes from the opening over of this third day.\nJamie Smith (16) and Jofra Archer (0) make their way out to the middle, aiming to extend England’s 195-run lead. How will Pakistan respond to two pretty tough days?\nOn Pakistan’s bowling attack, could Shaheen Shah Afridi be given an SOS to come for the second Test? He took 7/36 in a domestic 50-over match on Tuesday. Shaheen has lost pace in recent years, but could still surely enhance the Pakistan attack.\n“It was nice to spend time in the middle and get a decent score. I was a bit disappointed I did not get to three figures. I went to bed thinking about the nine runs I did not get and I was very disappointed with the manner I got out so close to a hundred.\n“I did not feel like I played any rash shots of sorts yesterday. I felt I did not need to do that on this surface. It was about batting time.\n“It was about reading the conditions. There was not any dangerous lateral movement like before so I could back my defence and pick up runs. On a different day, it if it is nipping around, my gameplan might change.\n“I try to stay away from chat about me, to be honest. I got into a bit of a rabbit hole reading all sorts. Everybody is entitled to their opinion but I try to trust the people I am working with closely.\n“I understand what people think when I get out to a rash shot or give it away but I just want to be a match-winner for England and put us into good positions.”\nThankfully, it is dry at Headingley so we will get under way on time this morning. The sun is out but it is fair to say the temperature is not particularly warm. There is a risk of a few showers at points during the day but we should at the very least get most of the day in.\nScyld Berry caused a stir in Pakistan with his piece yesterday, writing that this is their worst team to tour England in half a century. It is hard to argue against that point. Even if they beat England, that would say more about England than about Pakistan. Hopefully there will be some fight in the second innings. Babar Azam coming back at Lord’s will strengthen them but it is the lack of pace and mystery that is a worry.\nScyld Berry: This is Pakistan’s worst side in 50 years\nLink copied to clipboard\nPatriots may be quick to assume that England’s re-booted, or rather re-Rooted, Test team are back on course after the Brendon McCullum era, or error, with a new and ready-made number three in Jordan Cox and a bowling attack that will blow ageing Australians off the park next summer.\nIt is safer to say, however, that Pakistan’s Test side has never been poorer in the professional era of the last half-century – and that the gap between Pakistan and India in cricket is widening at the same rate as that between rich and poor everywhere.\nOften in the past Pakistan have not been a team, but they have been an eleven of brilliant individuals. Batsmen have had wrists greased with oil poured from a magician’s lamp, like Javed Miandad and Zaheer Abbas, who did not speak to each other but that did not stop them batting together. None has been more majestic than Majid Khan, none has immersed himself in a cocoon of concentration like Mohammad Yousuf, none has had the zeal to bat all day in Ramadan without touching a drop of water from dawn to dusk like Younis Khan.\nGood morning from Headingley. It’s a lovely day here, and the weather is set fair. I think, based on the below, we could be going until 7.30pm to get a result.\nAfter a second straight day of dominance, England head into day three of the first Test against Pakistan at Headingley in complete control of the match. England closed day two on 366/8, already 195 runs ahead of Pakistan, and will look to press home their advantage today.\nDespite rain delaying the start to day two, England took advantage of the 58 available overs yesterday. Harry Brook, who is back to number one in the Test batting rankings, led the way for the hosts with a blistering 91 from 93 balls, falling nine runs short of a Test hundred on his home ground. It is the second straight summer in which Brook has fallen agonisingly short of a century at his home ground having fallen for 99 against India a year ago. With Headingley not hosting another England Test match until 2029, Brook will have to wait at least three more years to get another chance of a Test century on this ground. Jordan Cox (73), Joe Root (66) and Dan Lawrence (51) also added fifties for England. With Jacob Bethell missing the series due to injury, the chance has been presented to Essex’s Cox and the 25-year-old registered his maiden Test fifty, although against this Pakistan attack he may be regretting not taking the opportunity to score his first Test hundred.\n“Once I got to fifty, I moved to sixty quickly, and thought ‘there is a chance here’,” Cox said after day two. “Just keep being disciplined, ball-by-ball. I had it at the back of my mind, thinking you have worked so hard, do not give this opportunity up. That is exactly what I do in county cricket. I get to fifty, see how many runs I need to score, and work down from there. Every team, every player wants to score Test hundreds. Four of us got fifties, so we were hoping one would kick on, and instead of making it just a hundred, make it 150 or 170. I am thrilled. I would have taken it at the start of the Test match. I let myself down a tiny bit, but at the end of the day, you take 70. I am not [happy] quite yet! Hopefully we can get another 50 then we will get to our work with the ball.”\nJordan Cox hit his maiden Test half century on day two but could he have got a ton?\nCredit: Stu Forster/Getty Images\nSpinner Ali Usman was the most potent of the Pakistan bowlers, with the 33-year-old claiming his maiden Test five-wicket haul. The tourists have a job on their hands to avoid a ninth defeat in their last 12 Tests. Jamie Smith resumes on 16 not out and is joined in the middle by Jofra Archer, who is yet to get off the mark. Play on day three from Headingley gets under way at 11am.",
        "content": "Joe Root celebrates a dominant victory on his return to the England captaincy\nCredit: Philip Brown/Getty Images\nThere was no attempt to be a Ben Stokes tribute act by Joe Root, just a measured captaincy performance very much in his own undemonstrative style.\nRoot, ever the team man, pushed his bowlers forward to lead England off at the end, recognising the match-winners in a walkover against Pakis...",
