    {
        "title": "Russell takes Dutch GP sprint pole as Antonelli struggles to fifth",
        "link": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/formula-1/2026/08/21/dutch-grand-prix-2026-f1-latest-updates-sprint-qualifying/",
        "source": "www.telegraph.co.uk for the latest news from the UK and around the world.",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.473254+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/content/dam/formula-1/2026/08/21/TELEMMGLPICT000499058468_17873333062100_trans_NvBQzQNjv4BqpVlberWd9EgFPZtcLiMQf0Rf_Wk3V23H2268P_XkPxc.jpeg?impolicy=OG-Live",
        "full_content": "George Russell has an opportunity to get his season back on track\nCredit: Getty Images/Bryn Lennon\nGeorge Russell took a much-needed sprint pole position at the Dutch Grand Prix as he tries to get his season back on track after a trying season so far. The Briton trails Kimi Antonelli, his Mercedes team-mate, by 59 points after 11 rounds, having won two grands prix to the Italian’s six.\nRussell went into this season as the strong favourite for the title, but has simply lacked the pace and consistency of his teenage team-mate. There has been some bad luck, too, but even Russell himself admitted that he needs to improve his performances if he wants to claim a first drivers’ championship.\nAt a sunny and breezy Zandvoort on Friday, though, he began the second part of the season exactly the way he needs to if he is to eat into the deficit. Antonelli could only manage fifth position, 0.227sec off Russell’s pole time after an uncharacteristically scruffy lap.\n“It’s great to be back here after the break. It was obviously a tough end to the first half of the year but really happy to come back. It’s been a while since I’ve done one of these [pole position] interviews,” Russell said after the session.\n“The lap felt really good to be honest, it all came together nicely. The McLarens look quick, Ferrari look quick, but really glad to pull it off when it mattered. It’s been great, it’s just the sprint qualifying and the big points are on Sunday – just trying to carry the momentum into tomorrow.”\nRussell taking sprint victory – and the eight points with it – was far from a formality. The top four drivers here were separated by less than a tenth of a second. Lando Norris, the defending champion who won the last race before the break in dominant style at the Hungaroring, was second, 0.041sec behind Russell. Charles Leclerc, the lead Ferrari, was third, just 0.055sec adrift. Oscar Piastri in the second McLaren was fourth, but was within a tenth of a second of the pole time.\nAs for the home hero? Max Verstappen has won this race three times before, and will want to put on a good show for the orange army before the race falls off the calendar after this year. By far the biggest news this weekend is his commitment to Red Bull until 2030, which will mean he will end up having spent a staggering 15 seasons at the team.\nAs has been the case for much of the year, at no point did the Red Bull look capable of threatening the front two rows, even in Verstappen’s capable hands. He will start the sprint in fifth, after finishing more than half a second down from Russell in another frustrating afternoon. Red Bull are, however, a team who seem to be capable of turning a poor Friday into a stronger weekend, but even having a sniff of the podium looks a big ask.\nLewis Hamilton, who is Antonelli’s closest championship challenger, albeit 50 points back, struggled in the final part of sprint qualifying, finishing behind even Verstappen and more than half a second adrift of his team-mate.\nAs for Antonelli? This is nothing to worry about. The points in the sprint race only ever make a small difference to the championship picture. Losing even four or five should not be a concern, especially with so many available on Sunday.\nOn this track last year Antonelli had a worryingly messy weekend in a debut season that was littered with them. He spun out of practice and then punted Leclerc out of the race, picking up a penalty for his error. Toto Wolff, the Mercedes team principal, said it was all part of the learning process. In the 12 months since, he has learnt enough to give himself a commanding lead in the world championship in only his second season in F1. The pressure is still on Russell to deliver.\nElsewhere, Liam Lawson, who has been promoted to Red Bull for this weekend from Racing Bulls because of a wrist injury to Isack Hadjar, was 11th. Lawson was dropped from Red Bull in 2025 after just two rounds, but he appears to be making a slightly better fist of it here, even if this is likely to be his only chance to impress for the senior team. Yuki Tsunoda, who is a substitute for Lawson for this weekend, was 12th in his first outing in the new-regulation cars.\nAston Martin, who brought their first power-unit upgrade of the season, having jumped forward after an impressive update to their car in Hungary, had a day that was in keeping with their woeful season. Lance Stroll was 18th whilst Fernando Alonso, who ran off track at a crucial time in SQ1, was 22nd and last, more than three seconds off the pace.\n“It’s great to be back here after the break. It was obviously a tough end to the first half to the year but really happy to come back. It’s been a while since I’ve done one of these interviews.”\n“The lap felt really good to be honest, it all came together nicely. The McLarens look quick, Ferrari look quick but really glad to pull it off when it mattered. It’s been great, it’s just the sprint qualifying and the big points are on Sunday, just trying to carry the momentum, into tomorrow.”\nHe is probably too far back to affect the title picture but a handful of wins would be no bad thing for him. He is doing a prep lap but a fairly fast one... relatively speaking.\nMcLaren looking strong again, Ferrari and Mercedes not too far off. Three different constructors within 0.2sec of one another at the top. Red Bull do not look at the races. Verstappen down in eighth there.\nNorris moves fastest with a 1:12.514 but Piastri is looking a little faster. It was not an especially tidy lap for Norris, so a fair chance Piastri can beat it. He does, by nearly half a second.\nLeclerc and Hamilton up next... Leclerc second, 0.164sec off Piastri and Hamilton third, 0.419sec off Piastri.\n10 minutes, 10 drivers through to SQ3 and six drivers will be eliminated. A good performance by Ocon to get ahead of Bearman, which he has not done too much this year.\nBut then Leclerc goes faster still to shift them all down. McLaren and Ferrari leading the way at the moment but Antonelli and Alonso are yet to set representative lap times.\nIn the Red Bull battle, Lawson is ahead of Verstappen, interestingly enough.\nAntonelli releases the pressure and moves into seventh. That should see him comfortably through.\nHe is just ahead of his Ferrari team-mate Charles Leclerc... but then Norris beats them both marginally. McLaren went well in Hungary and will hope that continues here. There is a decent chance of that, I think.\nAntonelli still in the pits and with no time on the board.\nHe gets a snap of oversteer entering the banked final corner and runs into the gravel, but keeps it out of the barrier... and comes into the pits.\nRussell completes his first lap with a 1:13.829, which is fastest of anybody so far, not that that means much given how few drivers have set a lap time.\nAugust 23: Dutch Grand Prix September 6: Italian Grand Prix September 13: Spanish Grand Prix September 26: Azerbaijan Grand Prix October 4: Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia October 11: Singapore Grand Prix October 25: United States Grand Prix November 1: Mexico City Grand Prix November 8: Brazilian Grand Prix November 21: Las Vegas Grand Prix November 29: Qatar Grand Prix December 6: Abu Dhabi Grand Prix\nAston Martin’s first big aerodynamic upgrade of the year helped it off the back of the grid in Hungary. This weekend they have their first power unit upgrade, courtesy of Honda. What are the chances that they can get a car (or both?) into SQ2 today? Even knocking on the door would be decent. That said, this is not the best test of an engine here. Fernando Alonso (below) was 12th in FP1.\nMercedes cruising towards their first title since 2021, so it seems. To be fair, 72 points is not that much at this stage. But it is hard to see Ferrari clawing that back in the second half of the season.\nIsack Hadjar will not race for Red Bull because of a wrist injury he picked up in the summer break. He is replaced by Liam Lawson, who steps up from Racing Bulls, whilst Yuki Tsunoda returns to F1 to replace the Kiwi.\nThis should have been the half-way point of the season, but they added another race in Malaysia. If they cancel any of the races in the Middle East towards the end of the season then it may well end up being the half-way point again. Or even later. Anyway, here is how it stands after 11 rounds in 2026.\nThe gap from Antonelli to Russell is significant but hardly insurmountable. Of course, Hamilton is still second after a disappointing weekend in Hungary, despite showing good pace. He cannot be counted out. For those two it is as much about making sure they do not lose any further ground in the next few races. With the way Antonelli has been operating in 2026, though, that might not be easy.\n1. ANT 1:12.949 2. NOR +0.121 3. RUS +0.125 4. HAM +0.190 5. LEC +0.289 6. PIA +0.659 7. HUL +0.835 8. GAS +0.962 9. BOR +1.043 10. LIN +1.336 11. VER +1.376 12. ALO +1.562 13. COL +1.877 14. LAW +1.912 15. BEA +1.925 16. OCO +1.948 17. TSU +2.161 18. STR +2.250 19. ALB +2.408 20. BOT +2.631 21. PER +2.751 22. SAI +3.396\nWelcome to our live coverage for sprint qualifying for the 2026 Dutch Grand Prix from Zandvoort. This is the last time the race will be on the F1 calendar for the foreseeable future, having been reintroduced in 2021 after a 35-year absence. It will be a shame to see it leave again, but never rule out it coming back, especially with Max Verstappen signing a lengthy contract extension with Red Bull.\nWhat of the competitive action this weekend? As it is a sprint weekend, there is not a lot of preparation time, with just one practice session before sprint qualifying. In that it was a fairly close fight at the top, and it was Kimi Antonelli, the championship leader, who was fastest of all. Behind him was the McLaren of Lando Norris who was 0.121sec adrift then George Russell, who will be looking to kickstart his title campaign here, a further 0.004sec behind him.\nThen came the two Ferraris of Lewis Hamilton (0.190sec) and Charles Leclerc (0.289sec) before a fairly significant gap of 0.659sec to Oscar Piastri in sixth. Verstappen was down in 11th, nearly 1.4sec off Antonelli’s ultimate pace but 0.5sec or so ahead of new (and old) team-mate for the weekend, Liam Lawson.\nIt was a tricky session for the drivers, though, as it began damp on a showery and blustery day by the Dutch seaside. Several drivers ran wide and off the track, though they all avoided anything more serious than gravel rash. The rest of the day looks to be drier but there are still a few showers around and the wind is all over the place, as it often is here.\nSprint qualifying starts at 3.30pm BST and we will be here for all of the updates for the nearly hour-long session.",
        "content": "George Russell has an opportunity to get his season back on track\nCredit: Getty Images/Bryn Lennon\nGeorge Russell took a much-needed sprint pole position at the Dutch Grand Prix as he tries to get his season back on track after a trying season so far. The Briton trails Kimi Antonelli, his Mercedes team-mate, by 59 points after 11 rounds, having won two grands prix to the Italian’s six.\nRussell wen...",
