        "image": "https://variety.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Sally-Field.jpg?w=1000&h=563&crop=1",
        "full_content": "Awards season loves a fresh face, but this one also loves a weathered one.\nWhen the Television Academy announced its nominations in July, the story wasn’t about the ingenues. It was about the lifers, the character actors, the movie stars and the beloved figures we lost along the way. Voters have handed us a September ceremony where nearly every envelope could contain a piece of Emmy history.\nHarrison Ford, 84 on Emmy night, is back for Apple TV’s “Shrinking” — still chasing the first major acting trophy of a 60-year screen career. The oldest winner in the category’s history is Henry Winkler, who was 72 when he won for the first season of “Barry” in 2018. Ford would shatter that record. He would also supplant Bob Newhart — 84 when he finally won for “The Big Bang Theory” in 2013 — as the oldest man ever to win a comedy acting Emmy, by a matter of weeks.\nAnd here’s the beautiful part: If Ford doesn’t do it, Stephen Root might. The 74-year-old, nominated for the freshman horror-comedy “Widow’s Bay” after some 250 credits and zero wins, would top Winkler’s mark. Two men older than the standing record, in the same category, in the same year, is pitch perfect.\nPopular on Variety\nThe lead comedy actor race carries the same energy. Martin Short, 76, lands his fifth consecutive nomination for Hulu’s mystery romp “Only Murders in the Building” — his eighth acting nod overall, but still without an acting win. A victory would make him the oldest lead comedy actor champion ever, sailing past Eugene Levy, who was 73 when “Schitt’s Creek” swept in 2020. His competition includes a fellow member of the 0-fer club: Steve Carell, 64, whose pivot to the dark comedy “Rooster” brings his career acting ledger to 0-for-7 — six straight losses for “The Office,” one for “The Morning Show.” Will he ever win?\nThe women’s races are just as historic. Sally Field, 79, returns to the limited series/TV movie field in the film adaptation of the acclaimed novel “Remarkably Bright Creatures,” finally getting the kind of showcase that may remind voters what she’s capable of. Her last Emmy came in 2007 for the ABC drama “Brothers and Sisters”; a win here would make her, by a matter of months, the oldest winner in the category’s history, something Kathy Bates missed last year with “Matlock.” Jessica Tandy was also 79 when she won for “Foxfire” in 1988.\nYoun Yuh-jung, 79, may have the best narrative in the entire field. Five years after becoming the first Korean performer to win an Oscar for “Minari” (2020), she earned her first Emmy nomination as the imperious Chairman Park in the second season of the anthology series “Beef.” A win would make her the first Korean actress to take a Primetime acting Emmy — Lee Jung-jae broke the barrier for the men with “Squid Game” in 2022 — and the first Korean performer to own both an Oscar and an Emmy for acting. She’d also join Eva Le Gallienne (1978) and Ellen Burstyn (2013) as only the third woman 79 or older to win the category.\nMichelle Pfeiffer, 68, finally gets the television vehicle worthy of her in Apple TV’s “Margo’s Got Money Troubles,” earning her second career nomination and positioning herself for a first Emmy that would double as a coronation for her small-screen era.\nAnd spare a cheer for Dale Dickey, 64, whose first nomination ever — supporting comedy actress for “Widow’s Bay” — arrives after four decades of being the best thing in everything from “Winter’s Bone” onward. That show, it should be said, is a veterans’ full-employment act all by itself.\nAnd then we have the lifers and character actors who are constantly referred to as “that guy” or “that gal.” Jeff Kober, 72, is having the kind of season character actors dream about: a guest drama nomination for his quietly devastating turn as the patient Duke on HBO Max’s medical drama “The Pitt,” paired with a Daytime Emmy nomination this year for revisiting “General Hospital” — the show that won him a Daytime trophy in 2022, for which he also self-submitted. Forty years after “China Beach” and “Sons of Anarchy” made him television’s most reliable menace, he’s on both Emmy ballots at once.\nRichard Jenkins, 79, nominated in supporting limited for “DTF St. Louis,” would become the second-oldest winner in his category’s history — behind only Paul Newman, who was 80 when he won for “Empire Falls” in 2005. There are worse men to follow.\nWhich brings us to the nomination that will have the tear ducts full in the room. Rob Reiner, who was taken from us alongside his wife, Michele, in December at age 78, received a posthumous nod as guest comedy actor for his warm, funny arc as Albert, the franchising mentor in the fourth season of “The Bear.”\nThe history here is almost unbearable. Reiner’s two Emmys came as Michael “Meathead” Stivic on the classic comedy “All in the Family,” the last of which was in 1978 — meaning a win would arrive 48 years later, the longest gap between acting victories in Emmy history. The man whose record he’d break would be his father. Carl Reiner famously waited 38 years between wins, from “Caesar’s Hour” in 1957 to “Mad About You” in 1995 — a victory that came in this very category.\nA posthumous win would also be the Emmys’ first for an on-camera performance since Raúl Julia in 1995 for “The Burning Season.” (Chadwick Boseman’s 2022 win was for voice work.)\nNone of these wins are guaranteed.\nEmmy voters are not a sentimental bunch, and the categories are stacked and fiercely competitive. But with a veteran class that includes Ford and Field, Short and Root, Youn and Jenkins, Kober and Dickey — and Reiner above them all — the industry that spent decades treating its veterans as scenery would love to hand them their due on the big stage.\nOn Sept. 14, somebody’s going to make history just by standing up.\nThis week’s updated Emmy winner predictions are below.\n\t\tEmmy Predictions Tracking\n(Aug. 21, 2026)\t\n\tDrama Series: “The Pitt” (HBO Max)\nComedy Series: “Widow’s Bay” (Apple TV)\nLimited or Anthology Series: “Beef” (Netflix)\nOutstanding Movie: “Remarkably Bright Creatures” (Netflix)\nLead Actor (Drama): Noah Wyle, “The Pitt” (HBO Max)\nLead Actor (Comedy): Matthew Rhys, “Widow’s Bay” (Apple TV)\nLead Actor (Limited/Movie): Oscar Isaac, “Beef” (Netflix)\nLead Actress (Drama): Rhea Seehorn, “Pluribus” (Apple TV)\nLead Actress (Comedy): Jean Smart, “Hacks” (HBO Max)\nLead Actress (Limited/Movie): Carey Mulligan, “Beef” (Netflix)\nSupporting Actor (Drama): Tom Pelphrey, “Task” (HBO Max)\nSupporting Actor (Comedy): Stephen Root, “Widow’s Bay” (Apple TV)\nSupporting Actor (Limited/Movie): David Harbour, “DTF St. Louis” (HBO Max)\nSupporting Actress (Drama): Sepideh Moafi, “The Pitt” (HBO Max)\nSupporting Actress (Comedy): Kate O’Flynn, “Widow’s Bay” (Apple TV)\nSupporting Actress (Limited/Movie): Linda Cardellini, “DTF. St. Louis” (HBO Max)\nGuest Actor (Drama): Colman Domingo, “Euphoria” (HBO Max)\nGuest Actress (Drama): Brittany Allen, “The Pitt” (HBO Max)\nGuest Actor (Comedy): Rob Reiner, “The Bear” (FX)\nGuest Actress (Comedy): Betty Gilpin, “Widow’s Bay” (Apple TV)\nVariety Series: “The Late Show With Stephen Colbert” (CBS)\nVariety Special (Live): “The Apple Music Super Bowl LX Halftime Show Starring Bad Bunny” (NBC)\nVariety Special (Pre-Recorded): “Taylor Swift | the Eras Tour | the Final Show” (Disney+)\nDirecting (Drama): Vince Gilligan, “Pluribus” (Apple TV) — “We Is Us”\nDirecting (Comedy): Hiro Murai, “Widow’s Bay” (Apple TV) — “Welcome to Widow’s Bay!”\nDirecting (Limited/Movie): Lee Sung Jin, “Beef” (Netflix) — “Oh, the Comfort, the Inexpressible Comfort”\nDirecting (Documentary/Nonfiction): Alexandria Stapleton, “Sean Combs: The Reckoning” (Netflix)\nDirecting (Variety Series): Yvonne De Mare, “The Late Show With Stephen Colbert” (CBS) — “The Late Show with Stephen Colbert Series Finale”\nDirecting (Variety Special): Hamish Hamilton, “The Apple Music Super Bowl LX Halftime Show Starring Bad Bunny” (NBC)\nDirecting (Reality Program): Ben Archard, “The Traitors” (Peacock)\nWriting (Drama): “The Pitt” (HBO Max) — “1:00 P.M.” by Kirsten Pierre-Geyfman and R. Scott Gemmill\nWriting (Comedy): “Hacks” (HBO Max) — “Hacks (Finale)” by Lucia Aniello, Paul W. Downs and Jen Statsky\nWriting (Limited/Movie): “DTF St. Louis” (HBO Max) by Steven Conrad\nWriting (Variety Series): “The Late Show With Stephen Colbert” (CBS)\nWriting (Variety Special): “Wanda Sykes: Legacy” (Netflix) by Wanda Sykes\nWriting (Nonfiction): “The Daily Show Presents: Jordan Klepper Fingers the Pulse: Give the Man a Prize” (Comedy Central)\nGame Show: “Jeopardy!” (ABC)\nHost (Reality or Reality Competition): Jeff Probst, “Survivor” (CBS)\nHost (Game Show): Jimmy Kimmel, “Who Wants to be a Millionaire” (ABC)\nCompetition Program: “Survivor” (CBS)\nStructured Reality Program: “Queer Eye” (Netflix)\nUnstructured Reality Program: “Love on the Spectrum” (Netflix)\nDocumentary or Nonfiction Special: “My Mom Jayne: A Film by Mariska Hargitay” (HBO Max)\nDocumentary or Nonfiction Series: “Sean Combs: The Reckoning” (Netflix)\nHosted Nonfiction Series or Special: “My Next Guest Needs No Introduction With David Letterman” (Netflix)\nExceptional Merit in Documentary Filmmaking: “The Librarians” (PBS)\nAnimated Program: “South Park” (Comedy Central) — “Sermon on the ‘Mount”",
