        "image": "https://images.ctfassets.net/lzny33ho1g45/1HPXzNfc1qv08sSKEdFaBR/158b3fdf427a17c544cb408d8db5f49f/boomi-alternatives-hero.jpg",
        "full_content": "Boomi runs the integration layer for thousands of enterprises, connecting ERPs and legacy systems and on-premises databases that most modern tools refuse to acknowledge exist. If you need SAP talking to Oracle while both of them shake hands with a healthcare system behind a firewall, Boomi says \"how high?\"\nBut for everyone else, whose stack is mostly cloud apps or who wants their entire team to be able to build without an IT backlog, Boomi isn't where you should go first.\nI work at Zapier, so you won't be surprised to learn I think it's one of the best Boomi alternatives for most organizations. But also because I work at Zapier, I've spent a lot of time researching and thinking about other automation tools. So based on all that experience, and lots of research about what Boomi does (and doesn't) do well, here are my picks for the best Boomi alternatives.\nThe best Boomi alternatives\n- Zapier for building safely with AI\n- Workato for recipe-style automation\n- MuleSoft for API management\n- Microsoft Power Automate for Microsoft-heavy teams\n- Tray for developer-led automation\n- UiPath for UI automation and legacy systems\nWhat is Boomi?\nBoomi is an enterprise iPaaS (integration platform as a service) that connects cloud applications, on-premises systems, and legacy infrastructure. Its platform spans integration, API management, data management, B2B/EDI, event streams, and AI agent tooling. It's built for IT teams managing complex hybrid environments—the kind where some of your data lives in Salesforce and some of it lives on a server in a room nobody has a key to anymore.\nBoomi's strength is its depth in on-prem and hybrid connectivity, its governance controls, and its compliance credentials (see: FedRAMP and HIPAA). Its weaknesses are the steep learning curve, the dated interface, and a pricing model that will make SMBs run the other way.\nWhat makes the best Boomi alternative?\nHow we evaluate and test apps\nOur best apps roundups are written by humans who've spent much of their careers using, testing, and writing about software. Unless explicitly stated, we spend dozens of hours researching and testing apps, using each app as it's intended to be used and evaluating it against the criteria we set for the category. We're never paid for placement in our articles from any app or for links to any site—we value the trust readers put in us to offer authentic evaluations of the categories and apps we review. For more details on our process, read the full rundown of how we select apps to feature on the Zapier blog.\nThe right Boomi alternative depends on why you're looking. But here are the things that matter most in this category:\n- Who can actually build. Boomi requires developers and integration architects. If you want business teams to own their automations without opening an IT ticket every time, you need a platform built for that.\n- Integration breadth. Boomi covers around 1,000 enterprise connectors and runs deep on legacy systems. Keep an eye on how many—and which types of—integrations these alternatives offer to be sure they match your tech stack.\n- Hybrid and on-prem support. All of the apps on this list at least offer workarounds for hybrid and on-prem support, but if that's core to your workflow, you'll want to pick a tool that handles it easily.\n- AI and agent capabilities. The best alternatives offer AI automation, letting AI reason, decide, and act within your workflows with appropriate governance.\nBoomi alternatives at a glance\n|  | Best for | Standout features | Pricing | \n|---|---|---|---|\n| Zapier | Building safely with AI | 9,000+ integrations, with secure access from wherever you work | Free plan available; paid plans from $19.99/month | \n| Workato | Recipe-style automation | Low-code builder | Custom pricing | \n| MuleSoft | API management | Full API lifecycle management; | Custom pricing | \n| Microsoft Power Automate | Microsoft-heavy teams | Deep Microsoft 365 integrations | From $15/user/month | \n| Tray | Developer-led automation | Tray Helix for governed AI-built app deployment | Custom pricing | \n| UiPath | UI automation and legacy systems | RPA bots for non-API systems | From $25/month | \nBest Boomi alternative for building safely with AI\nZapier\nZapier pros:\n- Governed AI agent access via Zapier MCP, SDK, and CLI\n- 9,000+ pre-built, maintained integrations (nearly 9x Boomi's connector library)\n- Anyone can build, but IT maintains oversight\nZapier cons:\n- On-premises only available via VPC peering\n- Not FedRAMP or HIPAA compliant\nBoomi is all about control: IT sets the rules, and everything flows through a centralized integration layer. You can guess why that's important when you're connecting healthcare systems or government infrastructure. But for most enterprises, that model creates a bottleneck: the 20% of your company with technical chops ends up building and maintaining automations for everyone else, and the backlog grows.\nZapier flips things around. With Zapier Copilot, anyone can describe a workflow in plain language and get a working automation, adding agentic AI wherever it's needed. Folks can also build from wherever they already work. Zapier MCP connects AI tools like Claude and ChatGPT directly to your app stack, with OAuth-managed credentials that never reach the model's context window. Agents can send emails, update CRM records, triage tickets, and route requests—across 9,000+ apps—but IT still sets the guardrails. They decide who can connect which apps and what approval flows are required, and they can always review the audit log.\nYou're on the Zapier blog, so I don't blame you for wanting a second opinion. To start, read about how enterprises like ClickUp and Palo Alto Networks are already using Zapier to orchestrate AI across their organizations.\nZapier pricing: Free plan available; paid plans from $19.99/month (billed annually); Enterprise pricing by quote.\nRead more: Zapier vs. Boomi\nBest Boomi alternative for recipe-style automation\nWorkato\nWorkato pros:\n- Deep SAP, Oracle, Salesforce, and Workday coverage\n- Low-code recipe builder is more approachable than Boomi's canvas\nWorkato cons:\n- Still designed primarily for IT and developer teams\n- Task-based pricing can spike unpredictably at high volume\n- Time-to-value measured in weeks or months, not days\nBoomi and Workato are both enterprise iPaaS platforms aimed at IT teams, they're both priced for massive organizations, and they're both built for scale. What separates them is mostly their philosophy.\nWorkato has been leaning hard into speed and AI execution. Its \"recipe\"-based building block is a low-code workflow that's more approachable than Boomi's AtomSphere canvas; you can get something running without Boomi's weeks-long implementation timeline, though you'll still want practiced hands for anything substantial. Workato also excels at agentic orchestration: its Genies reason through each step of a workflow, choosing different paths based on what's actually happening.\nWorkato's MCP exposes your existing recipe library as callable skills for external AI tools like Claude or Cursor—so if your organization has already built a deep library of approved automations, you can make all of that available to AI agents without rebuilding anything. Just keep in mind that if you're starting from scratch, you'll need to invest the time to build that recipe library first before the MCP value kicks in. Zapier MCP, by contrast, gives you immediate access to 9,000+ apps with no prior build required.\nWorkato pricing: Custom pricing\nRead more: Workato vs. Boomi and The best Workato alternatives\nBest Boomi alternative for API management and regulated data\nMuleSoft Anypoint Platform\nMuleSoft pros:\n- Full API lifecycle management: design, build, test, deploy, and govern in one platform\n- Tightly controlled access layer for AI agents operating on regulated data\n- Salesforce ecosystem alignment\nMuleSoft cons:\n- Not accessible to non-technical builders\n- Implementation timelines run longer than almost any competing tool\nMuleSoft and Boomi have more in common than most alternatives on this list. The most important overlap is that both are built for infrastructure-level work rather than app-to-app automation.\nWhere Boomi's strength is hybrid/on-prem connectivity, MuleSoft's strength is API architecture: building a reusable layer of governed APIs that every system in your enterprise—including AI agents—can call without touching the underlying data directly. You expose a tightly scoped API; the AI calls it, and your underlying database never has direct contact with the model.\nMuleSoft also offers Agent Fabric, a control plane for managing AI agents across the multi-vendor landscape. It's a signal of where MuleSoft is heading—less pure API management and more agentic infrastructure—but it's still early days.\nImplementation for MuleSoft requires developers who are comfortable with XML configuration and API architecture, but if you're coming from Boomi, it's possible you already have that on hand.\nMuleSoft pricing: Custom pricing\nRead more: MuleSoft vs. Zapier and The best MuleSoft alternatives\nBest Boomi alternative for Microsoft-heavy teams\nMicrosoft Power Automate\nMicrosoft Power Automate pros:\n- Native, seamless integration across the full Microsoft 365 ecosystem\n- Desktop flows (RPA) for automating legacy and UI-only systems\n- Process and task mining to surface automation opportunities\nMicrosoft Power Automate cons:\n- Value drops sharply outside the Microsoft ecosystem\n- Complex flows still require Power Platform specialists\nAn automation tool built around one software ecosystem sounds limiting—until that ecosystem is Microsoft's. If your enterprise runs on Outlook, Teams, SharePoint, OneDrive, and Excel, Power Automate is the easiest path to making all of those tools talk to each other. The flows are native, the licensing often lives inside what you're already paying for Microsoft 365, and you don't need a developer to get started.\nWhere Power Automate competes with Boomi specifically is RPA. Boomi is an API-first integration platform, so if your legacy system has no API, Boomi can't help you. Power Automate's desktop flows, on the other hand, are RPA bots that simulate human interaction (like clicking, typing, and navigating) on apps that were never designed for automation.\nMicrosoft being Microsoft, it's also been layering in AI. Copilot lets users describe automations in plain language, AI Builder handles document processing and data extraction, and the platform's process mining tools help identify where automation would actually have impact before you build anything.\nBut once your workflows start involving more than a handful of non-Microsoft tools, Power Automate starts to feel like what it is: a tool built to keep you inside the Microsoft Corporate Universe. If your stack is mixed—or if you want your whole company, not just your Microsoft power users, to build automations—go with another app on this list.\nMicrosoft Power Automate pricing: From $15/user/month for the Premium plan (billed annually); basic cloud flows included with some Microsoft 365 plans.\nRead more: Zapier vs. Power Automate and The best Power Automate alternatives\nBest Boomi alternative for developer-led automation\nTray\nTray pros:\n- Deep API-level control and custom connector development\n- Merlin Agent Builder for AI agents with guardrails, knowledge ingestion, and audit trails\n- Governed runtime for deploying AI-built apps to production\nTray cons:\n- Built for technical teams\n- Limited connector set\nTray is the alternative for teams that want Boomi's philosophy of developer control, centralized IT governance, and precise data mapping, but with a more modern builder and a stronger bet on AI agents.\nTray's core iPaaS platform is API-first and designed for technical users who want full control over integration logic. You can build complex workflows, handle advanced data mapping, and integrate directly with APIs when the pre-built connectors don't cover what you need. The governance features are also strong, with centralized IT ownership, standards enforcement, and predictable agent behavior across the organization. Add to that Tray's Merlin Agent Builder, which lets you create autonomous agents that work across your company's data—with guardrails, audit logs, and multi-channel deployment.\nThe newest thing in Tray's world is Tray Helix. It's a governed runtime specifically for AI-built apps—the stuff your finance analyst built in Claude Code over lunch that's been sitting on their laptop ever since. Helix takes those apps to production with one command, applies SSO and managed auth, and gives IT a named owner and audit trail for everything that ships.\nUnfortunately, Tray's 500ish connectors becomes a limitation if your goal is spreading automation across many teams using many different tools. But for developer teams building bespoke integrations against specific APIs, that might not matter as much.\nTray pricing: Custom pricing\nRead more: Tray vs. Zapier and The best Tray alternatives\nBest Boomi alternative for UI automation and legacy systems\nUiPath\nUiPath pros:\n- Industry-leading RPA for automating legacy, desktop, and UI-only systems\n- Combines RPA bots with AI agents and API-based workflows in the same platform\nUiPath cons:\n- Requires more technical expertise than most low-code platforms\n- Very limited connector set\nThink of the cases where Boomi gets called in for legacy infrastructure: the mainframes, the ERPs with no modern API, and the systems that were enterprise-critical before cloud existed. UiPath addresses that same problem from a completely different angle—not by building API bridges, but by deploying software bots that do what a human would do on-screen.\nIf a system has a UI but no API, UiPath can automate it. That's the use case it was built for, and it remains one of the strongest platforms in the RPA space for exactly that reason. For enterprises still running Citrix environments, desktop applications, or legacy software that predates the concept of an integration partner, UiPath fills a gap that most iPaaS tools can't.\nWhere UiPath has evolved is in how it blends RPA with modern automation. You're not limited to brittle, click-based bots anymore. UiPath now supports agentic automation: AI that can reason about what a bot should do, classify documents, extract data from unstructured inputs, and make decisions mid-workflow. You can mix RPA bots, AI agents, and API-based integrations in the same flow, which makes it more flexible than its RPA-first reputation suggests.\nEven with UiPath's visual builder, getting meaningful results—especially when combining RPA with AI and API logic—requires someone with development experience. And its pre-built connector library (200ish) is a far cry from what you'd get with a platform built specifically for broad SaaS integration.\nUiPath pricing: From $25/month for the Basic plan; Standard and Enterprise plans require contacting sales.\nRead more: Zapier vs. UiPath and The best UiPath alternatives\nWhich Boomi alternative should you choose?\nBoomi earns its place in stacks where the hard problem is legacy connectivity, on-premises data, or strict compliance requirements like FedRAMP or HIPAA. If your stack is mostly cloud, your teams are frustrated by IT bottlenecks, and you want AI that actually runs in production—you have other options.\nIf the goal is giving your whole organization the ability to build AI-powered automations safely and quickly, Zapier is the place to start. With secure access to 9,000+ apps from wherever you work, it's hard to beat.\nRelated reading:",
