        "full_content": "Why proprietary software isn’t a retrospective trend but a trap for failure\nThink proprietary software is more secure because its code is hidden? In the AI era, that assumption is dangerously outdated. AI tools can now expose vulnerabilities in closed systems without ever seeing the source code. Discover why true network resilience requires more than just secrecy. Learn more\nTechStrong.ai - Red Hat brings enterprise AI at scale into focus\nMoving past AI pilot phases is tough. Red Hat's Tushar Katarki discusses the shift toward open source models for better cost, data, and infrastructure control, and why proper AI governance is no longer optional. Learn more\nIT Ops Query - Lightwell reflects new agentic AI security urgency\nRed Hat and IBM are pushing for open, secure AI ecosystems, but systemic risks remain vast. Discover how Lightwell is addressing an urgent need to overhaul software supply chain defenses as autonomous agents take center stage. Learn more\nPolicy as code: What happens when you layer policy enforcement onto the automation you already have\nDiscover how layering policy as code onto your existing automation enhances governance, compliance, and security. Red Hat’s latest blog explains how Ansible Automation Platform provides real-time compliance reporting, automated remediation, and detailed audit trails—all without adding new tools. Learn more\nFrom experiment to production: A reliable architecture for version-controlled MLOps\nEver lost track of the exact dataset used to train your AI model? Red Hat’s new AI quickstart pairs Red Hat OpenShift AI with lakeFS to bring Git-style version control to MLOps. Explore how zero-copy branching and instant rollbacks can streamline data management and boost AI reproducibility. Learn more\nAbout the author\nMore like this\nHow a global financial messaging network secured millions of containers and defeated alert fatigue\nFrom fragmented to flawless: Unifying the AI development lifecycl