    {
        "title": "Air Coolers vs Air Conditioners: Which Cooling Solution Is Right for You?",
        "link": "https://paytm.com/blog/bill-payments/electricity-bills/what-is-the-difference-between-air-coolers-vs-air-conditioners/",
        "source": "A Comprehensive Guide to Money Transfer, Recharges, Bill Payments and Other Digital Payments | Paytm Blog",
<<<<<<< Updated upstream
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.246434+00:00",
=======
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.471561+00:00",
>>>>>>> Stashed changes
        "image": "https://wpblogassets.paytm.com/paytmblog/uploads/2021/11/13_electricity_A-complete-guide-to-the-difference-between-air-coolers-vs-air-conditioners.jpg",
        "full_content": "Air coolers and air conditioners cool a room in fundamentally different ways, and that difference drives everything else: price, electricity use, how comfortable a humid evening feels, and whether you can keep the door shut. The right choice depends on where you live, how large the space is, and how much you are willing to spend each month to stay cool.\nThis article covers how each system works, what they cost to buy and run, how much electricity each consumes, maintenance, environmental impact, and which one suits which situation.\nAir Coolers and Air Conditioners: A Quick Comparison\nPrices and running costs are indicative and vary by brand, capacity, city and local electricity tariff.\nHow Do Air Coolers Work?\nAir coolers work on evaporative cooling. A pump wets a set of honeycomb or wood-wool pads, a fan pulls warm air through those wet pads, and the water evaporating out of them absorbs heat from the air. The air that comes out the other side is cooler and more humid than the air that went in.\nBecause the cooling depends entirely on water being able to evaporate, the drier the incoming air, the more the temperature drops. In genuinely dry heat a good cooler can bring air down by 8–12°C. In humid air, where there is little room for more moisture, the same unit might manage 2–3°C and leave the room feeling clammy.\nVentilation is not optional. An air cooler needs a steady supply of fresh, dry air and a way for humid air to escape: an open window, a door ajar, or an exhaust fan. Run one in a sealed room and the air saturates within an hour, cooling stops, and the room ends up warm and muggy. Coolers don’t need ducting or an outdoor unit, but they are not “close the room and run it” appliances the way an AC is.\nTypes of air coolers\n- Personal coolers: small, 10–20 litre tanks, for one person or a small bedroom.\n- Tower coolers: slim, better airflow spread, suited to living rooms with good ventilation.\n- Desert coolers: large 50–100 litre tanks and powerful blowers, for big rooms, terraces and dry regions.\n- Window coolers: mounted in a window frame, drawing outside air directly; efficient but fixed in place.\nHow Do Air Conditioners Work?\nAn air conditioner moves heat rather than adding moisture. Warm room air passes over a cold evaporator coil carrying refrigerant. The refrigerant absorbs that heat and evaporates into a gas. A compressor then pressurises the gas, raising its temperature, and pushes it to the condenser coil in the outdoor unit, where the heat is released outside. The refrigerant condenses back to liquid and returns to start the cycle again.\nA useful side effect: because the evaporator coil is colder than the room’s dew point, moisture condenses on it and drains away. That is why an AC makes a room feel dry as well as cold, and why it works in Chennai in August while a cooler does not.\nTypes of air conditioners\n- Window AC: single unit, cheaper, easier to install, noisier indoors.\n- Split AC: separate indoor and outdoor units, quieter, better looking, higher install cost.\n- Portable AC: wheeled unit with an exhaust hose out a window; convenient but less efficient.\n- Inverter vs fixed-speed: an inverter compressor varies its speed to hold temperature instead of switching fully on and off, cutting electricity use by roughly 20–30% over a season. Fixed-speed units cost less upfront.\n- Hot-and-cold (heat pump) AC: reverses the cycle to heat in winter. Note that this is a separate feature from inverter technology; an inverter AC does not heat unless it is specifically sold as hot-and-cold.\nInitial Cost and Installation\nAir coolers start around ₹4,000 for a personal unit and run up to about ₹20,000 for a large desert cooler. There is nothing else to pay: you fill the tank, plug it in, and it works. No technician, no wall drilling, no gas charging.\nAir conditioners start around ₹28,000 for a 1.5-ton split and go past ₹60,000 for premium inverter models. On top of that, professional installation typically adds ₹1,500–₹3,500, more if you need extra copper piping, a stabiliser, or a stand for the outdoor unit. Landlord permission and a suitable outdoor wall are practical prerequisites that a cooler doesn’t demand.\nWhat size do you need?\nFor an AC, a rough rule for Indian homes is about 0.06 tons per square metre of floor area: roughly 1 ton for a small bedroom up to 11 sq m (120 sq ft), 1.5 tons for 11–18 sq m (120–200 sq ft), and 2 tons beyond that. Top-floor rooms, west-facing walls and large windows push you a size up.\nFor a cooler, match air throw (measured in cubic metres per hour) to room size. A personal cooler suits a single small room, while a hall needs a desert or tower unit.\nEnergy Consumption and Running Costs\nThis is where the two appliances separate most sharply.\nAn air cooler runs a fan and a small water pump, together typically 80–250 W. Over 8 hours that is roughly 0.7 to 2 units of electricity. At a tariff of ₹8 per unit, that works out to about ₹6–₹16 per day.\nA 1.5-ton 5-star inverter AC draws roughly 900–1,600 W depending on load, ambient temperature and thermostat setting. Over 8 hours, expect 6–10 units, or about ₹48–₹80 per day at the same tariff. Across a four-month summer that difference is easily ₹6,000–₹8,000.\nHow to cut the AC bill\n- Check the BEE star rating and ISEER value before buying. ISEER (Indian Seasonal Energy Efficiency Ratio) is the number that actually predicts your bill; higher is better. A 5-star inverter unit can use 25–30% less electricity than a 3-star fixed-speed unit of the same tonnage.\n- Set the thermostat to 24°C. Each degree lower raises consumption by roughly 6%. The Bureau of Energy Efficiency recommends 24°C as the default setting.\n- Service the unit before summer. Dirty filters and coils force the compressor to work harder.\n- Seal the room. An AC fighting an open door will run continuously.\nMaintenance Requirements\nCoolers need frequent, simple attention. The tank needs refilling daily or every couple of days during peak summer; a desert cooler can get through 10–30 litres a day. The tank and pads need cleaning every week or two, because standing water breeds mould, algae and mosquitoes. Cooling pads themselves usually need replacing every one to two seasons (₹300–₹1,500 depending on type). None of this needs a technician, but it does need you.\nAir conditioners need less day-to-day attention and more periodic expertise. Wash or vacuum the indoor filters every two to four weeks in season. Book a professional service once a year for coil cleaning, a drain check and a refrigerant pressure check, typically ₹500–₹800 per service. Bigger repairs (gas leak, compressor, PCB) are infrequent but expensive, which is why the AMC plans some brands offer are worth pricing.\nHealth and Air Quality Considerations\nCoolers raise indoor humidity, which helps if you find AC air uncomfortably dry, but a neglected tank can grow mould and bacteria that the fan then circulates. If anyone in the house has asthma or dust allergies, cleaning the tank and pads on schedule matters more than usual.\nAir conditioners dry the air, which some people find harsh on skin, eyes and throat over long hours. Their filters do trap dust, and models with additional filtration help in polluted cities, but only if the filters are actually cleaned. A closed, air-conditioned room also gets stale, so ventilate it periodically.\nEnvironmental Impact\nAir coolers have the lighter footprint on both counts that matter: they use no refrigerants, and they consume roughly a fifth of the electricity, which means a fifth of the associated power-generation emissions. Their trade-off is water, which is meaningful in water-stressed cities.\nAir conditioners rely on refrigerants that contribute to global warming if they leak or are disposed of carelessly. Newer refrigerants such as R-32 have a lower global warming potential than the older R-22 and R-410A, and India has been phasing out R-22. The bigger environmental cost, though, is simply the electricity: millions of ACs running simultaneously on hot evenings is a major driver of peak grid demand.\nEffectiveness in Different Climates\nClimate is the deciding factor for most buyers.\n- Dry heat: Delhi, Jaipur, Ahmedabad, Nagpur, Chandigarh in April–June, when humidity is low. A cooler performs excellently here and is genuinely comfortable, not just cheap.\n- Humid heat: Mumbai, Chennai, Kolkata, Kochi, and much of the country after the monsoon arrives. Coolers underperform badly, adding moisture to air that is already saturated. An AC is the practical choice.\n- Mixed climates: many north Indian cities are dry until the monsoon and humid after it. Some households run a cooler through May and June and an AC through the sticky weeks that follow.\nComfort Features and Flexibility\nAir conditioners give you control: a thermostat that holds a set temperature, sleep modes, timers, multiple fan speeds, remote and app operation on newer models, and dehumidification. They are quiet, particularly split units, and they work with the room sealed, which matters for noise, dust and mosquitoes.\nAir coolers offer fan speed, a swing function, and sometimes a timer. There is no thermostat, so output varies with the weather rather than with your preference. Their advantage is mobility: wheel one from bedroom to kitchen, or out to a balcony for an evening, which no split AC will do.\nLongevity and Seasonal Use\nA well-maintained air cooler lasts roughly 5–10 years; the pump and pads are the usual failure points and both are cheap to replace. Coolers are seasonal appliances, drained and stored once the monsoon arrives.\nAn air conditioner typically lasts 10–15 years. Repairs are less frequent but need a technician and cost more. A hot-and-cold model earns its keep year-round in cities with cold winters, which a cooler cannot do at all.\nWhich One Should You Buy?\nChoose an air cooler if: you live in a dry-heat region, your room has good cross-ventilation, your budget is under ₹20,000, you want to keep the electricity bill low, or you need something portable, including for a balcony, terrace or rented accommodation where installation isn’t possible.\nChoose an air conditioner if: you live in a humid city, you need reliable cooling regardless of weather, you want a room that stays at a set temperature, you sleep with doors and windows shut, or you want heating in winter as well. Budget for both the higher purchase price and roughly ₹1,500–₹2,500 extra per month on the summer electricity bill.\nConsider both if your city is dry in early summer and humid after the monsoon: running the cooler while it works keeps the AC’s meter turning less.",
        "content": "Air coolers and air conditioners cool a room in fundamentally different ways, and that difference drives everything else: price, electricity use, how comfortable a humid evening feels, and whether you can keep the door shut. The right choice depends on where you live, how large the space is, and how much you are willing to spend each month to stay cool.\nThis article covers how each system works, w...",
