        "image": "https://wpblogassets.paytm.com/paytmblog/uploads/2021/11/10_paytmbank_Paytm-Payments-Bank-The-Best-Bank-To-Open-a-Fixed-Deposit-Account.jpg",
        "full_content": "The best Fixed Deposit is rarely the one with the highest advertised rate. What you actually keep depends on how much of your deposit is insured, what breaking it early costs, and how much tax the interest attracts. This guide walks through the six things worth comparing before you commit money, the statutory limits that apply this year, and how to structure a larger sum so it stays both protected and accessible.\nEvery bank sells essentially the same product. You hand over a lump sum, it sits for a fixed period, and you get it back with interest at a rate agreed on day one. The rate is the part everyone compares, and it is genuinely important, but it is not the only thing separating a good deposit from an expensive mistake.\nThe terms around the rate matter just as much: how much of your money is insured if something goes wrong, what it costs if you need the funds early, and how the interest is taxed. This article covers how Fixed Deposits work, what to compare before opening one, and the numbers worth knowing in advance.\nWhat is a Fixed Deposit Account?\nA Fixed Deposit is offered by banks, small finance banks, post offices and NBFCs. You place a lump sum for a chosen tenure, anywhere from 7 days to 10 years, and earn a rate locked at the moment of booking. Market movements after that make no difference to what you receive at maturity.\nMost institutions accept a minimum of ₹1,000 to ₹5,000, though some tax-saver deposits start as low as ₹100. There is generally no upper limit, but retail rates apply only below ₹3 crore. Deposits at or above that are treated as bulk deposits and priced on a separate card.\nOne distinction shapes your return more than most people expect. Cumulative deposits reinvest the interest and compound it, usually quarterly. Non-cumulative deposits pay the interest out to you at intervals, with no compounding. Same rate, same tenure, noticeably different totals.\nSix Things to Compare Before You Open One\nRate, for the tenure you actually want\nAdvertised rates almost always belong to one specific tenure, often an unusual one such as 444 or 888 days. If your money is going in for two years, the headline number attached to a 444 day scheme tells you very little. Compare the rate for the period you genuinely intend to stay invested.\nAs a rough guide for general depositors:\nSenior citizens aged 60 and above usually earn 0.25% to 0.75% more, most commonly 0.50%, and some institutions offer a further premium from age 80. Rates track the RBI’s repo rate, so check the current card on the day you book rather than relying on a figure you saw last month.\nHow much of your deposit is actually insured\nThis is where the highest rate can quietly become the wrong choice. Bank deposits are covered by the Deposit Insurance and Credit Guarantee Corporation, which insures each depositor up to ₹5,00,000 per bank. That figure covers principal and interest together, and it applies across every account you hold at that institution in the same capacity, including your savings and current balances.\nAnything above ₹5 lakh at a single bank sits outside the cover. Company and NBFC deposits carry no DICGC protection at all, which is why they should be judged on credit rating rather than headline rate.\nPut concretely: placing ₹15 lakh with one small institution to capture an extra 1% leaves ₹10 lakh uninsured. Spreading the same money across three institutions keeps all of it covered and usually costs very little in yield.\nWhat it costs to get out early\nLife interrupts five year plans, so the exit terms deserve a proper look. Most institutions reduce your interest rate rather than charging a separate fee. A common structure is 0.50% on deposits up to ₹5 lakh and 1% on anything larger, though some levy up to 2%. Many pay no interest whatsoever if the deposit is closed within 7 days of booking.\nTwo variants remove the option entirely. Non-callable deposits do not permit premature withdrawal, and tax-saver deposits are locked for the full five years. Both often carry a slightly better rate, which is the trade you are making for giving up access.\nBefore booking, it is worth asking whether partial withdrawal is allowed, and whether you can borrow against the deposit instead. A loan or overdraft against an FD, typically up to 90% of its value, is frequently cheaper than breaking it.\nWhen the interest reaches you\nCumulative suits money you will not touch until maturity, since compounding does the work. Non-cumulative suits anyone living off the income, with monthly, quarterly, half yearly or annual payouts available. Monthly payouts usually come at a marginally lower rate, because the institution loses the benefit of holding the interest.\nHow easily you can manage it\nOver a five year term, being able to book, track, renew and close the deposit without a branch visit matters more than a marginal rate difference. Check whether the maturity instruction can be amended later and whether a nominee can be registered online, since both are easier to sort at the start than in a hurry years later.\nWhat happens at maturity\nDecide upfront whether the deposit renews automatically or credits back to your account. An auto-renewal nobody is watching can lock funds for another full term at whatever rate happens to prevail on that day, which is rarely the rate you would have chosen.\nWhat Your Money Actually Earns\nNumbers make the cumulative and non-cumulative choice concrete. Take ₹1,00,000 booked for five years at 7% per annum.\nThat gap of roughly ₹6,500 is what compounding adds over five years. Neither option is better in the abstract. If you need the quarterly income, taking it is not a loss, it is the point of the deposit.\nTypes of Fixed Deposits\nBeyond the cumulative and non-cumulative split, a few variants exist for specific situations.\nTax-saver deposits carry a mandatory five year lock-in, and the principal qualifies for a deduction of up to ₹1.5 lakh per financial year under Section 80C, now Section 123 of the Income-tax Act, 2025. No premature withdrawal is permitted and no loan can be taken against them.\nSenior citizen deposits are available to residents aged 60 and above at rates 0.25% to 0.75% above the general card.\nSpecial tenure schemes run for limited periods on odd tenures and often carry the highest rate an institution is offering at any given moment. They are worth checking, provided the tenure suits you.\nHow Fixed Deposits Are Taxed\nInterest from a Fixed Deposit is fully taxable at your slab rate as income from other sources, and it has to be declared every year as it accrues, not only in the year the deposit matures. This catches people out on long tenures.\nTax is deducted at source once interest from an institution crosses ₹50,000 in a financial year, or ₹1,00,000 for resident senior citizens. The rate is 10% where your PAN is on record and 20% where it is not, which makes registering your PAN one of the cheapest decisions available. For deposits with non-bank payers such as NBFCs, the threshold is lower at ₹10,000.\nIf your total income falls below the taxable limit, you can submit a self declaration so nothing is deducted. From 1 April 2026, Form 121 replaces the earlier Form 15G and Form 15H as a single unified form covering all ages.\nThe tax-saver benefit comes with an important condition. The ₹1.5 lakh Section 80C deduction is available only under the old tax regime. Under the new regime it does not apply, so a tax-saver deposit becomes an ordinary FD that happens to be locked for five years. Senior citizens can also claim up to ₹50,000 on deposit interest under Section 80TTB, now Section 153, again only under the old regime.\nRemember that TDS is not your final tax bill. If more was deducted than you owe, you claim it back when filing, and Form 26AS or the AIS will show what has been recorded against your PAN.\nStructuring a Larger Deposit\nOnce you are past ₹5 lakh, splitting the money across institutions solves several problems at once rather than being merely cautious.\nIt keeps everything insured, since the DICGC cover applies per bank and includes your savings and current balances at the same institution. It can also keep interest below the TDS trigger at each place, as the ₹50,000 and ₹1,00,000 thresholds apply per institution. That last point affects your cash flow rather than your final liability, since the interest stays fully taxable and must still be declared either way.\nThe other technique worth knowing is laddering. Instead of locking the entire sum to a single maturity date, you stagger the deposits so one matures every few months. It costs nothing, and it means an unexpected expense rarely forces you to break a deposit and surrender the interest.\nHow to Open a Fixed Deposit Account\nStart by comparing rates for your intended tenure, reading the premature withdrawal terms alongside the rate rather than after choosing. Then decide the amount, the tenure and whether you want the interest compounded or paid out.\nThe application itself needs KYC documents, typically PAN and Aadhaar plus address proof. Fund the deposit by transfer, net banking or cheque, and before you finish, register a nominee and set your maturity instruction.\nSave the FD receipt, which records the amount, rate, tenure and maturity date, and set a reminder about a week before maturity. That way the renewal decision stays yours rather than defaulting to whatever the auto-renewal produces.\nManaging Your Finances with Paytm\nFixed Deposits are opened with banks, but the everyday money management around them is where Paytm helps. Paying utility bills, recharging your mobile and handling regular payments in one place keeps your outflows visible, and every transaction sits under “Balance & History” for review.\nThat visibility is more useful than it sounds when you are deciding on a deposit. Knowing what a typical month actually costs you is how you work out how much can be locked away safely, rather than committing too much and having to break the deposit six months later.",
