        "image": "https://www.livemint.com/lm-img/img/2026/08/21/1600x900/logo/Delhi-Rain--27_1787314360113_1787314379455_639d4231-77fe-445e-994f-0e71aa3d43c4.jpg",
        "full_content": "A low-pressure area over northeast Madhya Pradesh and adjoining areas is likely to trigger isolated heavy to very heavy rainfall in east Madhya Pradesh on 22 August, while monsoon activity is expected to remain subdued over peninsular India during the coming week, the India Meteorological Department (IMD) said.\nCentral India is likely to receive fairly widespread to widespread rainfall through the week, according to the latest forecast. West Madhya Pradesh is expected to see such activity during 22-25 August, while east Madhya Pradesh and Chhattisgarh are likely to receive widespread rainfall during 22-27 August. Vidarbha is forecast to receive widespread rainfall during 23-25 August.\nIn northwest India, widespread rainfall is forecast over Uttarakhand through 27 August, while Jammu and Kashmir, Ladakh and adjoining areas are likely to receive widespread rain during 22-23 August. Haryana, Chandigarh and Delhi may see widespread rainfall on 22 August, while east Uttar Pradesh is likely to receive rain during 22-24 August.\nIn east India, widespread rainfall is expected over Gangetic West Bengal and Sub-Himalayan West Bengal and Sikkim during 22-27 August. Jharkhand and Odisha are also likely to receive rain during the period.\nFairly widespread to widespread rainfall is also likely across several parts of northeast India over the coming week, with intensity varying across the region, the IMD said.\nArunachal Pradesh is expected to receive widespread rainfall during 26-27 August. Assam and Meghalaya, along with Nagaland, Manipur, Mizoram and Tripura, are likely to experience widespread rainfall during 22-23 August and again from 25-27 August.\nAccording to the IMD, rainfall across the country in July was 1% above normal, compared with a 35.4% deficit in June. Cumulative rainfall during 1 June-18 August was 12.6% below normal.\nAmid the wet conditions, the IMD has forecast a low to moderate flash-flood risk over a few watersheds and adjoining areas during the next 24 hours. The risk covers districts in Assam, Meghalaya, Manipur, Mizoram, Tripura and Nagaland.\nIn Assam, the districts at risk include Darrang, Hailakandi, Karbi Anglong, Dima Hasao, Nagaon and Karimganj. Meghalaya’s East Khasi Hills, West Khasi Hills and Jaintia Hills are also at risk.\nIn the Nagaland-Mizoram-Manipur-Tripura region, the alert covers Chandel, Churachandpur, Senapati, Tamenglong and Ukhrul in Manipur; Aizawl in Mizoram; and several districts in Nagaland. Expected rainfall over the next 24 hours could lead to surface runoff and localised inundation in fully saturated soils and low-lying areas within the areas of concern.\nIn south peninsular India, rainfall is expected to remain mostly isolated to scattered. However, Coastal Karnataka, Kerala and Mahe, and Lakshadweep are likely to receive fairly widespread to widespread rainfall through 27 August.\nIn the past 24 hours, very heavy rainfall of 12-20 cm was recorded over west Uttar Pradesh, east Madhya Pradesh, Mizoram and Sub-Himalayan West Bengal, according to the IMD.\nHeavy rainfall of 7-11 cm was reported from east Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, Uttarakhand, west Madhya Pradesh, the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, Assam and Tamil Nadu.\nVijay C. Roy is a journalist with over 21 years of experience covering various news beats across different organisations such as Business Standard and The Tribune. In the past, he has covered beats such as finance, auto, MSME, commodities, FMCG, pharmaceutical, agriculture, IT/ITES, infrastructure and start-ups. He joined Mint in February 2025, and covers agriculture, food processing, fertilizers, environment and climate change, bringing over two decades of experience reporting on farm policy, food inflation, crop trade, and rural livelihoods.<br><br>Vijay’s areas of reporting include food security and climate change policies, focusing on their impact on different stakeholders and their implications. His expertise lies in simplifying complex agri-economic issues such as edible oil import dependence, cotton and wheat trends, fertiliser subsidies, and climate-related risks. He has covered key developments including global supply disruptions and evolving trade policies, offering both macroeconomic perspective and field-level context. Known for his credible and balanced reporting, he follows a rigorous, fact-based approach that prioritises accuracy and context. He is driven by a commitment to public interest, aiming to make critical agricultural and economic issues accessible while contributing to informed policy and industry discussions.\nOops! Looks like you have exceeded the limit to bookmark the image. Remove some to bookmark this image.",
