        "image": "https://www.livemint.com/lm-img/img/2026/08/21/1600x900/logo/pexels-photo-7649100_1787324319518_1787324331598_a3545c4a-2b61-4b3f-bd87-411aa052a325.jpeg",
        "full_content": "The United States Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS) has announced that a revised green card application form will be introduced next month, with applications submitted using the previous version being rejected from September 18, according to PTI.\nForm I-485 is used by eligible individuals already in the US to apply for lawful permanent residency, commonly referred to as a Green Card.\nThe updated Form I-485, used to apply for permanent residence or adjust immigration status, will replace the edition dated January 20, 2025. According to the USCIS, the revised form has been updated to reflect new public charge regulations that will take effect on September 18.\nApplications using the January 20, 2025 version will not be accepted if they are postmarked or filed electronically on or after September 18, the agency said.\nUSCIS has released a preview of the updated Form I-485 and accompanying instructions to help applicants prepare, particularly because the agency will not provide any grace period for the transition to the new edition.\nThe USCIS has advised applicants to not submit the updated version of Form I-485 before September 18, when the revised form officially comes into effect.\nThe existing version will continue to be accepted only if applications are postmarked or filed electronically before September 18.\nThe change follows the Trump administration’s expansion of the definition of a “public charge”, under which immigration applications can be denied if applicants are deemed likely to depend on certain government benefits, including Medicaid, food assistance or other forms of public support.\nThe revised “public charge” rules are set to take effect on September 18.\nThe revised Form I-485 has been introduced to align the adjustment-of-status process with the recently issued final rule governing inadmissibility on public-charge grounds, according to USCIS.\nThe public-charge assessment is used to determine whether an applicant is likely to depend primarily on government assistance for their livelihood. The criteria have changed under different US administrations and continue to be a contentious issue in US immigration policy.\nUSCIS did not announce any changes to filing fees or application deadlines as part of the update. The agency has advised applicants to visit the Form I-485 webpage for a preview of the new form and detailed filing instructions.\nApplicants who file Form I-485 can also apply for work and travel permits while their adjustment-of-status cases are being processed.\nMeanwhile, the change is likely to be closely watched by Indian nationals pursuing employment- or family-based permanent residency, especially those who have faced prolonged waits for an immigrant visa number to become available, as per ANI.\nIndian professionals account for a significant portion of the employment-based immigration pool in the US, particularly in fields such as technology, healthcare, engineering and other specialised industries. Many have been waiting years for permanent residency due to annual country-specific limits on immigrant visas.\nGarvit Bhirani is a journalist based in Gurugram. He is a Deputy Chief Content Producer at LiveMint, where he covers national and international news stories, focusing on accuracy and compelling storytelling for readers. <br><br> With a total of six years of experience in journalism, he has previously worked with Vaco Binary Semantics for Google, taking on the role of news curation lead, and reported from the field on health, education, and agriculture stories for 101reporters and News9. He has also served as a content editor for entertainment and news media organisations. <br><br> Garvit holds bachelor’s and master’s degrees in journalism and mass communication from Guru Gobind Singh Indraprastha University and Gurugram University, respectively. During college days, he joined India’s only non-profit student journalism network, where he anchored daily news updates and produced his own weekly show called ‘Data Fix’. <br><br> He was selected for the YES Foundation Media for Social Change Fellowship in Delhi, the Talking Data to the Fourth Pillar residential workshop, and the VOICE Fellowship in Pune. <br><br> He holds certificates in COVID-19-verification reporting, data journalism, food & agriculture, tech policy, media literacy and countering misinformation, and tackling election disinformation courses from Thomson Foundation, IndiaSpend, The Dialogue, US Mission in India, and AFP. <br><br> He can be reached on <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/garvit-bhirani\">LinkedIn</a> or on <a href=\"https://x.com/GarvitBhirani\">@garvitbhirani</a> on X\nOops! Looks like you have exceeded the limit to bookmark the image. Remove some to bookmark this image.",
