    {
        "title": "Only 223 urban local bodies have ratings, limiting access to municipal credit",
        "link": "https://www.livemint.com/news/india/only-223-urban-local-bodies-have-ratings-limiting-access-to-municipal-credit-11787312631468.html",
        "source": "mint - news",
<<<<<<< Updated upstream
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:40:18.244046+00:00",
=======
        "timestamp": "2026-08-21T20:51:27.469140+00:00",
>>>>>>> Stashed changes
        "image": "https://www.livemint.com/lm-img/img/2026/08/21/1600x900/logo/Heatwave_1783069395349_1783069395566_a590917e-e8a8-4cec-8417-932e9d8a5e7a_1787324832267_517a295f-1a11-453e-acb2-34adcfeda244.jpg",
        "full_content": "New Delih: Cities are staring at an ₹11.5 trillion capital expenditure requirement between FY27 and FY31, but most of them are in no shape to borrow for it. Only 223 of the country's 4,332 urban local bodies (ULBs) currently have credit-rating reports, a gap that keeps the vast majority of municipalities locked out of market-based financing, even as an indicative ₹5.8 trillion credit opportunity exists over the same period.\nThe mismatch was among the central issues discussed at the two-day Public Sector Banks (PSB) Conclave 2026, held earlier this week in the national capital in the presence of Union finance minister Nirmala Sitharaman, according to people aware of the discussions and a document reviewed by Mint.\nThe stakes are high. India's urban population is expected to cross 53% of the total by FY2047, and cities are projected to generate nearly 70% of GDP by then. Around 70% of the urban infrastructure needed to support that growth, spanning housing, metro rail, roads, water supply, waste management and logistics, is yet to be built.\nTo close the gap, the framework under discussion envisages a bigger role for public financial institutions (PFIs) in helping municipalities become more creditworthy. The idea is to link credit access to improvements in how municipalities collect property tax and user charges, and to how they modernise revenue systems more broadly, one person aware of the discussions said. The plan also includes standardising financial reporting across municipalities and building common frameworks for municipal credit assessment, so that rating exercises can happen more regularly.\nThat's a big ask: roughly half of ULB revenue currently comes from taxes and fees, with the rest made up of grants and investment income, leaving many municipalities financially dependent on higher levels of government.\nSeparately, the National Bank for Financing Infrastructure and Development (NaBFID) said it could step in with partial credit enhancement (PCE), essentially, a guarantee that improves a project's credit profile, for eligible municipal projects.\n“PCE is closely linked to credit ratings and is equally applicable to municipal projects. From NaBFID's perspective, PCE may be extended to eligible municipal projects to enhance their credit rating profile, thereby improving bankability and facilitating access to institutional and market-based financing,” a NaBFID spokesperson said in an emailed response. The spokesperson added that one of the biggest hurdles municipalities face is the absence of solid financial management, bookkeeping and accounting practices.\nGovernment schemes such as the Urban Challenge Fund (UCF) are meant to push municipal corporations toward better financial discipline and stronger cash-flow management, particularly for projects run as public-private partnerships, the spokesperson said.\nEmailed queries to the finance and housing ministries, and to India Infrastructure Finance Co. Ltd, went unanswered as of press time.\nThe municipal bond market itself remains tiny, despite a revival in recent years. As of 31 July 2026, ULBs had raised a total of ₹4,540.34 crore through 31 municipal bond issuances by around 22 ULBs, according to SEBI data.\nThe current phase of the market picked up after 2017, helped by incentives under the Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT and AMRUT 2.0), along with better credit ratings and regulatory support. Most recent issuances have carried investment-grade ratings, typically AA or AA+, with maturities of five to 10 years and coupons broadly between 7.6% and 8.5%. The money raised has largely gone into water supply, sewerage, roads, and green infrastructure projects.\nBefore 2017, issuances were limited and dated back to the late 1990s. Including those older bonds and special cases like the ₹2,000 crore Amaravati Bonds issued by the Andhra Pradesh Capital Region Development Authority, the cumulative market size is estimated at roughly ₹6,000-6,100 crore. The Amaravati bond is sometimes included in the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs' broader tallies, but it doesn't figure in the Securities and Exchange Board of India's dedicated ULB bond statistics.\nEven after the post-2017 revival, the market remains shallow by global standards: India's municipal bond market is equivalent to just 0.06% of its corporate bond market, compared with 7% in the US.\nOfficials pointed to two structural problems. First, project cash flows aren't adequately ring-fenced, meaning revenue from a specific project isn't clearly separated to guarantee repayment. Project-finance structures and escrow mechanisms for project revenues could offer investors more certainty on debt servicing, one of the people said.\nSecond, weak disclosure and small, fragmented project pipelines make it hard to attract long-term institutional money. Pooling smaller projects into larger financing vehicles — potentially through state-level intermediaries or dedicated municipal infrastructure funds — could help create bigger, more investable portfolios.\n“The experience of Surat shows how greater participation of urban local bodies in the municipal bond market can directly translate into better urban infrastructure and public amenities,” Hemali Boghawala, former mayor of Surat Municipal Corporation. Surat's green bond proceeds are funding water treatment, renewable energy, waste processing and electric buses, showing how market financing can reduce cities' dependence on government grants, she added.\nThe broader framework aims to attract a broader base of institutional investors to municipal bonds through mechanisms such as expanded credit enhancement, underwriting of anchor tranches, and partial guarantees.\n“For India's urban infrastructure financing needs, the longer-term opportunity will therefore depend not only on the quantum of capital available but also on whether municipalities can strengthen their own-source revenues, improve financial disclosures, ring-fence project cash flows and build sufficient creditworthiness to access debt markets,” said a senior government official, requesting anonymity.\nHarsh Kumar is a policy reporter at Mint (HT Media Group), where he covers the Ministry of Commerce and Industry along with key departments of the Ministry of Finance, including the Department of Economic Affairs (DEA) and the Department of Financial Services (DFS). With over five years of experience in business and economic journalism, he has developed strong expertise in tracking policy developments and their wider economic impact.<br><br>He has previously worked with Business Standard, Moneycontrol, and Outlook Money, where he reported extensively on banking, financial services, and the broader economy. Over the years, he has built a reputation for delivering accurate, insightful, and impactful stories, supported by a keen eye for detail and a consistent track record of breaking exclusive news.<br><br>An alumnus of Jamia Millia Islamia, Harsh closely follows regulatory changes and key economic trends shaping India’s financial and industrial landscape. His reporting aims to simplify complex policy issues for a wider audience while maintaining depth and credibility.<br><br>Outside of work, he enjoys tracking policy developments, finding scoops, and travelling, reflecting his curiosity about how economic decisions shape everyday life.\nOops! Looks like you have exceeded the limit to bookmark the image. Remove some to bookmark this image.",
        "content": "New Delih: Cities are staring at an ₹11.5 trillion capital expenditure requirement between FY27 and FY31, but most of them are in no shape to borrow for it. Only 223 of the country's 4,332 urban local bodies (ULBs) currently have credit-rating reports, a gap that keeps the vast majority of municipalities locked out of market-based financing, even as an indicative ₹5.8 trillion credit opportunity e...",
