        "image": "https://www.livemint.com/lm-img/img/2026/08/21/1600x900/logo/pexels-photo-4031634_1787323953354_1787323967766_594e3024-be63-4faa-922c-47dbd6ea793c.jpeg",
        "full_content": "New Delhi: Union health minister JP Nadda on Friday reviewed the country’s preparedness for H1N1 influenza, with a particular focus on Delhi, where cases have risen sharply this year.\nAccording to surveillance data from the National Centre for Disease Control (NCDC), Delhi has recorded 1,449 H1N1 infections by mid-August, compared with 229 during the corresponding period last year.\nData from the NCDC and the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP) also show significant seasonal influenza activity in Karnataka, Kerala, Maharashtra and Gujarat.\nDuring the meeting, Nadda assessed the national epidemiological situation, laboratory surveillance networks, and tracking trends for influenza-like illness (ILI) and severe acute respiratory infection (SARI), according to an official statement. He emphasised the need for heightened vigilance, robust surveillance and preparedness across state health systems to identify and manage clusters or unusual surges in respiratory illness.\nH1N1 influenza, commonly known as swine flu, is a contagious respiratory infection that spreads when an infected person coughs, sneezes or talks. It can cause serious complications, including pneumonia and respiratory distress, particularly in children, elderly people, pregnant women and those with chronic conditions or weakened immunity.\n\"Although influenza infection can recover itself through proper rest and support care, H1N1 virus may cause serious complications like pneumonia and respiratory distress in susceptible people like children, elderly patients, pregnant women and people suffering from chronic diseases and weak immunity,” said Dr. Niveditha Alok Swamy, consultant -Internal Medicine, SPARSH Hospital, Bangalore.\nEffective surveillance, prompt testing of cases suspected of having a severe infection, and hospital preparedness are vital to detect an upsurge in the disease at the earliest and to avoid unnecessary complications, Swamy said. \"High fever, difficulty in breathing, worsening of cough or unusual tiredness are symptoms that should not be ignored and one should report to doctors immediately without resorting to self-medication. Apart from H1N1 preparedness, prevention of dengue and malaria is equally important now.”\nNadda also called for integrated disease management, urging states to coordinate preventive measures for overlapping seasonal diseases such as dengue and malaria. Highlighting Delhi's recent gains in tuberculosis detection through community engagement, he urged public representatives and municipal bodies to spearhead public awareness.\nThe high-level review was attended by Delhi health minister Dr. Pankaj Kumar Singh, Union health secretary Punya Salila Srivastava, and other senior officials.\nThe review follows an earlier directive from the Union health secretary on 10 July asking states and Union territories to strengthen preparedness for acute respiratory illnesses.\nPriyanka Sharma is a journalist at Mint, where she covers the Union Ministry of Health and the pharmaceutical industry. Her work focuses on explaining government policies and how they impact healthcare and the medicine market in India. With 12 years of experience in journalism, she has built a reputation for providing clear and honest news on important health topics that affect the entire country.<br><br>Her educational background includes a journalism degree from the prestigious Indian Institute of Mass Communication (IIMC) and specialized training in public health from the Public Health Foundation of India. Before her current role at Mint, Priyanka worked with India Today, The Pioneer, and ANI. She also served as a lead consultant for the National Health Authority, which gave her firsthand knowledge of how the government manages large-scale health programmes.<br><br>Priyanka is based in New Delhi and is an avid traveller who loves visiting the mountains. She has a great interest in regional flavours, particularly South Indian food.\nOops! Looks like you have exceeded the limit to bookmark the image. Remove some to bookmark this image.",
